      "Two Colorado rules work together here, and between them they decide whether you ever see a dollar of attorney fees. First, a clause that only lets the landlord recover attorney fees and court costs - but not the tenant, if the tenant wins - is void under C.R.S. 38-12-801(3)(a)(II), and any fee-shifting clause must award fees to the prevailing party once the court finds the party prevailed and the fee is reasonable. Second, and less well known: under 13-40-123 a prevailing residential landlord or tenant is not entitled to recover attorney fees at all \"unless the residential rental agreement between the parties contains a provision for either party to obtain attorney fees\". So silence costs you the fees, and a landlord-only clause costs you the fees too, because it is void. The only version that works is a mutual prevailing-party clause - which is why the Default by Tenant clause has one, and why it is worth checking any clause you write yourself for the same thing.",
