      "Wyoming recognises three ways to serve the abandoned-property notice, and any of them starts the seven-day clock: certified mail to an address the renter gave you in writing specifically for that purpose, personal service under Rule 4 of the Wyoming Rules of Civil Procedure with a copy delivered to the renter personally, or publication in a newspaper published in, or widely circulated in, the county where the unit is (W.S. 1-21-1210(a)(i)(A)-(C)). Publication is the fallback when a renter vanishes without leaving an address. One trap in the same section is worth knowing before you dispose of anything. If the renter answers in writing within the first seven days saying he intends to collect the property, W.S. 1-21-1210(a)(iii) tells you in one sentence to hold it 'for an additional period of seven (7) days', and in the next sentence refers to 'the additional fifteen (15) day period'. The statute gives two different numbers for the same hold, and no Wyoming court has sorted it out. Hold for the longer period: the extra storage is chargeable to the renter under W.S. 1-21-1210(b), while disposing too early is conversion of someone else's property, and nothing caps what that costs you.",
