    topicKey: "tenant-death",
    bodyText:
      "Unlike Colorado's 'Letty's Act,' Wyoming has no dedicated statute addressing what happens to a lease when a tenant dies during the term. Article 12 (all 11 sections) never addresses tenant death. In practice, if a tenant dies mid-lease, the estate generally remains liable for the remaining lease term under ordinary contract principles, subject to your general duty to make reasonable efforts to re-rent the unit (a broad contract-law mitigation-of-damages principle, not a landlord-tenant-specific statutory duty). There's no Wyoming equivalent to Colorado's specific protections against liquidated damages, accelerated rent, or clawed-back move-in concessions when a lease ends this way.",
    notes: "WY added 2026-08-21, backlog item #9 from session 5's full-library audit (§11). Confirmed via full-text read of all 11 Article 12 sections (session 1) plus a targeted search finding no WY-specific tenant-death statute anywhere — unlike Minnesota's 504B.265, which does exist and was checked as a reference point for what such a statute looks like when a state has one. | WY: RETRO 2026-10-04 (SOP 1.56, rule 79). BASIS: section-open, official Wyoming Legislature NXT gateway, 2026 Wyoming Statutes, read 2026-10-04 (Art. 12 at /1/53/65, Art. 13 at /1/53/66). Both articles' live text is character-identical to the 2026-09-30 read, so no amendment intervened and no history-line discrepancy arises. VERDICT: absence confirmed on a whole-article read of all eleven Art. 12 sections. BASIS: absence rests on Title 1 ch. 21 articles 10, 12 and 13 read WHOLE section-open (2026-09-30, re-pulled and confirmed identical 2026-10-04) plus the secondary agreement already recorded above. NOT a statute-wide search with terms and hit counts: no loadable Wyoming corpus exists for this session (org egress policy returns 403 for every statute host; the official site publishes no bulk text and no documented query URL), so the boundary is three articles, not the code.",
