    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "holdover",
    bodyText:
      "Nebraska allows a landlord to recover up to three months' periodic rent or threefold the actual damages, whichever is greater, plus reasonable attorney's fees, from a tenant whose holdover is WILLFUL AND NOT IN GOOD FAITH (§76-1437(3)) - a higher ceiling than Kansas's 1.5x cap, and the fee award is a genuine statutory remedy tied to this specific claim rather than a lease promise, so it does not run into the attorney-fee-clause ban. Two things follow that are easy to miss. FIRST, the willfulness condition is real: for an ordinary holdover that is not willful and not in bad faith, Nebraska sets NO damages measure at all, and your remedy is the action for possession plus whatever actual damages you can prove. SECOND, the statutory figure is a LUMP measure, not a daily rate, so it does not translate into a per-day charge. The standard holdover clause asks for holdover damages in the maximum amount permitted by applicable law and otherwise defers to law, which keeps it lawful here but means it recovers nothing specific on a non-willful holdover. If you want a stated daily figure for that case, it has to be a lease term, and it has to be a reasonable estimate of your actual loss rather than a penalty.",
    notes: "Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1437(3). Per Taylor's call (session 1, Flag 6): the generic holdover clause's fixed 'double rent' figure remains valid in NE since it requests less than NE's 3x ceiling — no supersedes needed, states field extended directly. Revisit the double-rent baseline once more states are surveyed and recalibrate to whatever figure is most common across all 50. | CORRECTED (gap-discovery source #2, real lease-product comparison): the attorney's-fees component of §76-1437(3) was missed in the original session-1 statute read — caught when a real Nebraska commercial lease product (ILRG/PublicLegal, attorney-reviewed) included it and the citation was re-verified directly against primary source, confirming the commercial product was right and this clause's original session-1 version was incomplete. | *** NUMERIC ERROR CORRECTED 2026-08-31 (NE re-audit), full URLTA read verbatim from nebraskalegislature.gov. *** Statute: 'an amount not more than THREE MONTHS' PERIODIC RENT or threefold the actual damages sustained by him, whichever is greater, and reasonable attorney's fees.' The row said '3 times periodic rent.' NOT THE SAME. 'Three months' periodic rent' is TIME-based; '3x periodic rent' is a MULTIPLIER. They coincide only for month-to-month. For a week-to-week roomer under §76-1414(4) — a tenancy Nebraska expressly contemplates — periodic rent is WEEKLY, so three months' periodic rent is ~13 weeks' rent, not 3 weeks'. The prior wording understated landlord recovery ~4x in the tenancy where it matters most. The multiplier form ('threefold') applies ONLY to the actual-damages branch, which is why the two branches read differently and why collapsing both into '3x' loses the distinction. Cite: §76-1437(3). | CORRECTED 2026-09-03: this row described double monthly rent as 'Colorado's double-rent figure.' That attribution is WRONG. Colorado has NO holdover multiplier — C.R.S. §13-40-123 gives the prevailing party damages, costs, and (residential only if the lease so provides) attorney fees, with damages measured as reasonable rental value per Behr v. Burge, 940 P.2d 1084 (Colo. App. 1996). See edu-holdover-co. The comparison drawn in this row's body text should be read as against the old generic clause's figure, not against Colorado law. | NE: Read section-open 2026-09-30 from nebraskalegislature.gov in the built-in browser. The whole URLTA (§§76-1401 to 76-1449, 54 sections, 78,605 chars, SHA-256 73533d60a357f1a7f89f4dd8da75b8f2) was loaded via display_html.php and pattern-screened in page; named sections were then read individually. Retro checks, SOP 1.10. RULE 54 (SOP 1.10, optional clauses) — CORRECTED AND EXTENDED. §76-1437 re-read section-open. (1) STALE DESCRIPTION REMOVED: the row said 'The standard holdover clause requests double the Monthly Rent with no fee-shifting language'. The shared `holdover` clause no longer says that — the 2026-09-03 shared-clause pass removed the 'double' figure (see this log's HOLDOVER FIGURE — PROVENANCE FINDING section), and the row was never updated, so an NE row was describing text that had not existed for four weeks. (2) THE WILLFULNESS CONDITION IS NOW STATED: §76-1437(3)'s measure applies only 'if the tenant's holdover is willful and not in good faith', which leaves Nebraska with NO statutory damages measure for a non-willful holdover. (3) THE MEASURE IS A LUMP, NOT A DAILY RATE, while the shared clause's wording is 'for each day Tenant remains in possession'. That is lawful (it defers to applicable law and so recovers the statutory maximum or nothing) but it is a mismatch of shape, and it is why the optional stipulated holdover rate below is worth considering. RULE 54 VERDICT: a lease-set holdover rate for the non-willful case is an OPTIONAL CLAUSE Nebraska law appears to allow, as a liquidated-damages term that is a reasonable estimate of loss rather than a penalty. NOT DRAFTED: offering it means removing NE from the shared `holdover` row and writing an override, which is a product change beyond a scalpel retro, so it is recorded here with its citation and verdict for Taylor's call (SOP rule 54's 'list every one found' requirement) rather than built. The liquidated-damages-not-a-penalty standard is Nebraska contract doctrine I have NOT read this session — flagged as unread per rule 76.",
