      "Until January 1, 2031, a guest staying in a hotel, motel or compliant short-term lodging because a declared disaster substantially damaged, destroyed or made uninhabitable their home is not treated as a tenant, and their stay does not become a tenancy for eviction purposes, until they have stayed 270 days. Before or at check-in for a stay exceeding 30 consecutive days, the operator must give the statutory notice in at least 12-point type and a confirmation form on which the guest indicates whether they are disaster-displaced. Once such a guest has stayed more than 30 days, the operator must give 72 hours' written notice before requiring them to leave, unless the guest has failed to pay, interfered with other guests, damaged or threatened to damage property, or poses a risk of harm.",
    notes: "CA: Civ. Code s1954.071, added by AB 299 (Stats. 2025 ch. 531), EFFECTIVE 2025-10-10 as an urgency measure, SUNSETS 2031-01-01. An EXPRESS EXCEPTION to the s1940(b)/R&T s7280 30-day line recorded at edu-tenancy-scope-ca: for disaster-displaced guests the line moves from 30 days to 270. Cross-noted there. Relevant to short-stay operators rather than landlords, but a property-management product serving both should know it. Prescribed notice and a prescribed two-option confirmation form, both verbatim at s1954.071(c)-(d). A SEVENTH TYPE-SIZE FLOOR in CA (12-point, s1954.071(c)). If the guest refuses to select either statement the operator may rely on other reasonable information, or limit or refuse the stay.",
