    notes: "CA: CONFIRMED ABSENT. Civ. Code s1950.5 was read section-open IN FULL and contains no interest requirement anywhere - not in (b) permitted purposes, (c) the cap, (e) deductions, or (h) return mechanics. Proof-of-absence from the governing statute read end to end, not an inference from silence. Municipal layer flagged and NOT resolved - several CA cities impose deposit interest by ordinance; see edu-municipal-layer-ca. Row added 2026-09-19 for consistency with the confirmed-absence rows other states ship (edu-no-deposit-installments-ks etc.); the finding itself dates from the s1950.5 read earlier in the pass. GROUP NORMALISED 2026-09-19: 'Security Deposits' -> 'Security Deposit' (session drift; 12 canonical groups are used by all nine states). || UPGRADED TO CODE-WIDE 2026-09-28, gap-discovery source 4. The absence previously rested on reading Civ. Code s1950.5 in full, which is a section-level proof, not a statute-wide one. Official leginfo full-text search across all codes, run 2026-09-28: 'interest on security deposit' returns 0 sections; 'interest on the security deposit' returns 3, and every one is outside ordinary residential tenancy - Ins. Code s10458, Civ. Code s800.49 (FLOATING HOME MARINAS) and Civ. Code s798.39 (MOBILEHOME PARKS). So California does require deposit interest, but only in the mobilehome-park and floating-home-marina regimes, both of which are outside this product's scope (see the checklist's deprioritised-layers table). That is worth saying to a landlord, because it is the likely source of a belief that California requires interest. Local ordinances remain the live exception and are unchanged: several California cities do require interest, and the municipal layer is out of scope (edu-municipal-layer-ca). SCOPE: statutes only; regulations, local ordinances and case law not searched.",
