    topicKey: "radon-disclosure",
    bodyText:
      "California has no statute requiring a residential landlord to test for radon or to disclose radon to tenants. The state's radon provision addresses construction standards for new buildings rather than landlord duties, and California's Proposition 65 warning requirement does not reach radon, because radon is not on the state's list of chemicals known to cause cancer or reproductive toxicity. A known radon problem can still matter: conditions severe enough to affect habitability run through the ordinary habitability rules, and a landlord who conceals a known hazard can face liability on other grounds.",
    notes: "CA: CONFIRMED ABSENT FROM THE GOVERNING TITLE, with the scope stated. Civ. Code Title 5 Chapters 1-2 (ss1925-1954.071) were swept end to end 2026-09-19 and contain no radon provision. This is NOT a whole-code proof: a radon duty outside Title 5 - in the Health and Safety Code, say - was not searched for. Recorded at that strength deliberately, since CA's disclosure duties are scattered across at least four codes. | CA 2026-09-24 follow-up: NOT CLOSED - set to NEEDS_REVIEW. Searched: CDPH Radon Program 'Radon and Real Estate' page (points to natural-hazard disclosure sections and the Residential Environmental Hazards booklet; no rental disclosure duty), targeted searches for a CA radon landlord disclosure/testing statute (secondary sources only, all saying none), practitioner lists of CA required disclosures (radon absent from all). Consistent but not primary. NEW LEAD: Proposition 65 (H&S s25249.6) requires a business to warn before knowingly exposing anyone to a listed chemical, and OEHHA has residential-rental safe-harbor warnings (27 CCR ss25607.34-25607.35). Whether radon is on the Prop 65 list is UNVERIFIED; if it is, a landlord above the employee threshold who knows of a radon exposure may owe a warning. bodyText narrowed to the interim text pending that check. To close, needed (ranges): the H&S chapter TOC for CDPH's indoor-radon provisions (Div. 103/104); the current OEHHA Prop 65 list entry for radon (list dated 2023-12-29 or later); 27 CCR Art. 6, ss25600-25607.35; H&S ss25249.6 and 25249.11. | CA 2026-09-24 second follow-up: CLOSED, back to VERIFIED. Three independent routes, all negative: (1) Civ. Code Title 5 Chs. 1-2 read end to end - no radon provision; (2) H&S s105430 (Div. 103, Pt. 5, Ch. 7, Indoor Environmental Quality) is the radon provision and governs construction standards for NEW BUILDINGS and a radon assessment/mitigation plan conditioning building permits - no landlord, tenancy, disclosure or testing duty; s105430(e) makes (a)-(d) operative only if federal funds are available (same contingent-commencement shape as H&S s26147 for mold); (3) Prop 65 closed at the list - s25249.6 reaches only chemicals on the s25249.8 list, and the OEHHA list (dated 2023-12-29) has no radon entry; s25249.11(b) would exclude landlords under 10 employees regardless. SCOPE: confirmed absent from those three routes; Div. 103 and 104 were selected by part/article title, not read in full - not a whole-code proof. || CONFIRMED STATUTE-WIDE 2026-09-28, gap-discovery source 4, and TWO SECTION FAMILIES REVIEWED THAT THE EARLIER CLOSE-OUT HAD NOT SEEN. Official leginfo full-text search, all codes: 'radon' returns 12 sections. Page one lists H&S ss106750, 106775, 106780, 106785, 106790 and 106795, H&S s105430, H&S s25417.1, Wat. Code s79545 and B&P s10084.1. The six 106xxx sections are ARTICLE 2, RADON CERTIFICATION, in H&S Division 104 Part 1 Chapter 4 (Professional Certification): s106750 read section-open reads in full 'This article establishes requirements for radon certification.' It certifies radon professionals and imposes no duty on a landlord; none of the six mentions a lessor, landlord, tenant, rental or lease. B&P s10084.1 read section-open: it directs the Department of Real Estate to DEVELOP a consumer booklet on common environmental hazards including asbestos, radon gas, lead-based paint, formaldehyde, storage tanks and water and soil contamination, and does nothing else - no delivery duty and no landlord duty. 'environmental hazards booklet' returns 0 sections, so no statute requires or safe-harbours delivering that booklet to a tenant either, which also disposes of the C.A.R. RLMM 12/24 paragraph 38D Residential Environmental Hazards Booklet item as a matter of contract rather than law. The absence recorded in this row therefore stands, now on a statute-wide basis rather than a Title 5 one. The two sections not shown on page one of the results were not reviewed; every landlord-plausible hit was. SCOPE: statutes only; regulations, local ordinances and case law not searched.",
