      "Texas law voids city and county ordinances that regulate conduct in a field occupied by the Property Code unless another statute expressly authorizes them, and the occupied field expressly includes local rules that regulate evictions or restrict or delay notices to vacate or eviction filings. Anyone injured, or threatened with injury, by a preempted ordinance, or a trade association representing them, may sue the city or county for a court order and attorney's fees after giving it at least three months' notice of the claim. A 2023 challenge by several cities was dismissed in 2025 for lack of standing, and the law is in force. Do not assume a local tenant-protection ordinance in your area is enforceable, and do not ignore one either: whether a particular ordinance survives is a local, case-by-case question.",
    notes: "TX: Research pass 2026-09-25 (advanced research; sources as noted). Tex. Prop. Code §1.004 (Added by Acts 2023, 88th Leg., R.S., Ch. 899 (H.B. 2127), Sec. 15, eff. Sept. 1, 2023); §1.004(b) eviction-field language - its enacting history is UNCONFIRMED (Justia 2025 host + official-site snippet only). Tex. Civ. Prac. & Rem. Code ch. 102A (standing, remedies). Litigation: State v. City of Houston, No. 03-23-00531-CV (Tex. App.-Austin July 18, 2025) (reversed and rendered dismissal; cities lacked standing), en banc reconsideration denied May 14, 2026 (court opinion via CourtListener; TML report). No Supreme Court petition found (unconfirmed). A private enforcement suit against Dallas ordinances is reported pending in the Fifteenth Court of Appeals (secondary). NEEDS_REVIEW: paste the official PR.1 page (history of §1.004(b)). Pairs with §24.0043 (only the Legislature may modify eviction procedure) and Loc. Gov't Code §§214.902, 250.007. | TX 2026-09-25 (later same day): NEEDS_REVIEW CLEARED. Official Tex. Prop. Code ch. 1 (statutes.capitol.texas.gov) supplied by Taylor: §1.004(a) AND (b) carry a single history line - Added by Acts 2023, 88th Leg., R.S., Ch. 899 (H.B. 2127), Sec. 15, eff. Sept. 1, 2023 - so the eviction/notice-to-vacate field language was enacted in H.B. 2127 itself, not added later. Litigation status remains as sourced above (court opinion via CourtListener; no Supreme Court petition found - unconfirmed). | TX retro 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.29, rule 79): the statutes behind this research-pass row RE-READ section-open in the official codes. Tex. Prop. Code §1.004(a)-(b) (single history line, H.B. 2127, eff. Sept. 1, 2023) - matches the body. Tex. Civ. Prac. & Rem. Code ch. 102A read in full: §102A.002 standing for any person with an injury in fact, actual or threatened, or a trade association representing the person; §102A.003 declaratory and injunctive relief plus costs and fees (fees to the city or county only if the suit is frivolous); §102A.004 immunity waived; §102A.005 the city or county must get notice of the claim at least three months before suit; §102A.0015 (S.B. 1008, eff. Sept. 1, 2025) adds public health districts. Body sentence on suing now states the notice and trade-association points. §24.0043 (only the Legislature may modify or suspend ch. 24 procedure; H.B. 16, 2nd C.S. amendment) read. The litigation sentences rest on court opinions and reports, not statute, and are unchanged. Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the official Texas statutes (chapter HTML served by tcss.legis.texas.gov behind statutes.capitol.texas.gov; site current through the 89th Legislature, 2nd Called Session, 2025); the whole Property, Business & Commerce, Civil Practice & Remedies and Finance Codes and the Constitution were loaded, proved complete against the site's chapter lists, saved and hash-matched (TX log, 'Retro checks (SOP 1.29)').",
