    topicKey: "alarm-duties",
    bodyText:
      "New Jersey requires smoke alarms in every rental. In a building with three or more units, the owner must install and maintain smoke detectors or alarms as the Department of Community Affairs requires by rule. A home for no more than two households must have a smoke alarm on each level and outside each separate sleeping area near the bedrooms, and, if it has a secondary power source, a warning label within 18 inches of the main electrical panel and meter. Before leasing it or letting a new tenant move in, the owner must get a certificate showing it complies: from the municipal official who issues the certificate of occupancy or inspection where a local ordinance requires one, and otherwise from the local fire safety enforcing agency. The state fire code rules also require working carbon monoxide alarms before the certificate issues. Leasing without compliance can bring a fine of up to $500.",
    notes: "NJ: Official New Jersey Legislature statutes site full-text search (compilation 'updated through P.L.2026, c.30'), run by Taylor 2026-09-26 with alternate-word-form stemming ON and narrowing terms; every hit pasted and read. Controlling text: N.J.S.A. 55:13A-7.1 (L.1979, c.419): every hotel and multiple dwelling 'shall be equipped with smoke detectors or smoke alarms or both' per DCA rules on number, location, specifications, maintenance and testing. Other hits: 55:13A-13.1 (retirement communities exempt from 'multiple dwelling' if fire-safety self-inspection filed; common-area detectors, unit owners jointly responsible for batteries); 45:5A-2 (alarm-business licensing). NOT READ: the DCA rules themselves - N.J.A.C. 5:10 has no smoke-detector subchapter; the Uniform Fire Code N.J.A.C. 5:70 (which, by recall only, includes a certificate of smoke and CO alarm compliance on change of occupancy of 1-2 family dwellings) was not searched or read. That certificate is therefore NOT asserted. CONFIRMED ABSENT at statute level (L.7, bounded): a statutory smoke-alarm duty specific to 1-2 family rentals. | NJ: CORRECTED 2026-09-27 (gap-discovery backfill, source 2 lead from NJ Realtors Form 125-10/2022 §41). The 2026-09-26 row said statutes set no smoke-alarm duty specific to 1-2 family rentals and recorded that as a CONFIRMED ABSENCE. THAT WAS WRONG (L.13 shape: the official full-text search for 'smoke detector' + tenant/lease/occupancy did not surface the sections, which use 'smoke-sensitive alarm device' and 'alarm device'). Controlling text, read on the FindLaw host copy (current as of 2024-01-01): N.J.S.A. 52:27D-198.1 (P.L.1991, c.92): a structure used for residential purposes by not more than two households must have a smoke-sensitive alarm device on each level and outside each separate sleeping area (battery-operated acceptable), and, except seasonal rentals, at least one ABC-rated portable fire extinguisher of no more than 10 pounds within 10 feet of the kitchen; enforced only through 198.2 and 198.3. N.J.S.A. 52:27D-198.2: where a municipal ordinance requires a certificate of occupancy on change of occupancy, no certificate issues until the building is found equipped; otherwise 'no owner shall sell, lease or otherwise permit occupancy for residential purposes' without first obtaining a compliance certificate from the enforcing agency. Carbon monoxide: required in the certificate by N.J.A.C. 5:70-2.3 (Cornell LII copy; amended 56 N.J.R. 553(b), eff. 2024-04-15) - 'before any one- and two-family or attached single family structure is sold, leased, or otherwise made subject to a change of occupancy for residential purposes'; the CO statute itself not read. Multiple dwellings: N.J.S.A. 55:13A-7.1 unchanged. Checklist row 85 and the §14 proof-of-absence entry corrected. | NJ: Retro check 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.46), rule 79. Basis for 52:27D-198.1 and 198.2 was the FindLaw host copy, not the official site. Re-read officially: 52:27D-198.1 and 198.2 were AMENDED by P.L.2025, c.19 (approved 2025-02-03, effective immediately; chapter law read on pub.njleg.gov). The current 198.1 no longer requires a portable fire extinguisher (only the section heading still mentions one) and adds a secondary-power-source warning label. The host copy predated the amendment, so the body's extinguisher sentence was WRONG and is removed. 198.2(a)/(b): the certificate comes from the municipal certificate-of-occupancy official where an ordinance requires one, otherwise from the Uniform Fire Safety Act enforcing agency; the old \"local fire official\" wording merged the two paths. 198.3 (fine up to $500, amended by the same act). 55:13A-7.1 unchanged. Carbon monoxide: N.J.A.C. 5:70-2.3, Cornell LII copy only (regulation; the official N.J.A.C. was not reachable this session), not re-verified; whether the fire code still lists an extinguisher after P.L.2025, c.19 was not checked, so the body asserts none. Read section-open 2026-10-03 in the built-in browser on the official NJ Legislature statutes (lis.njleg.state.nj.us, compilation through P.L.2026, c.30); each section fetched twice, heading checked, SHA-256 saved (NJ retro sources, src-1003).",
