    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "veterans-incentive",
    bodyText:
      "In Broward, Escambia, Hillsborough and Santa Rosa Counties, a landlord who rents to a veteran in the HUD-Veterans Affairs Supportive Housing (HUD-VASH) program may apply for two kinds of funding, while money is appropriated. First, vacancy relief: proportional rent for holding the unit for up to 45 days after it becomes available, or until the veteran moves in if sooner, applied for within 60 days after move-in. Second, if the veteran moves out during the first 12 months of a year-to-year lease or after any lease expires, up to $2,000 for property loss caused by the veteran beyond the deposit, applied for within 60 days after move-out. The second is available only to a landlord who applied for vacancy relief. After move-out the landlord must give the program administrator the move-out checklist with current photos and a copy of the landlord's written deposit-claim notice to the veteran.",
    notes: "FL: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 (Fla. Stat. §83.684(3)-(5), leg.state.fl.us, history s. 1, ch. 2026-125); subsections (1)-(2), (6)-(7) seen only in summary (short title, definitions, first-come funding and suspension, rulemaking). Effective 2026-07-01 per the enrolled CS/CS/SB 1602 (research pass; approved 2026-06-11). Administered by the Florida Housing Finance Corporation through contracted program administrators; subject to annual appropriation (§83.684(3)). The deposit-claim notice referenced is the Fla. Stat. §83.49(3)(a) notice (security-deposit-return-fl). Not lease content; CONDITIONAL on county and HUD-VASH tenant. | FL: PARTIAL CLOSED 2026-09-26 (Claude Code, at the FL sync). Fla. Stat. §83.684(1)-(7) read in full from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov; history s. 1, ch. 2026-125); (1)-(2) short title and definitions, (6) first-come funding and suspension, (7) rulemaking, all as summarized. Effective date read from the session law itself (laws.flrules.org, Laws of Fla. ch. 2026-125): 'This act shall take effect July 1, 2026.' Approved by the Governor and filed June 11, 2026. Body unchanged. | FL retro 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.30) - rule 79: §83.684(1)-(2) and (6)-(7), previously 'as summarized', read verbatim on the official ch. 83 page; consistent. Read section-open 2026-10-03 on the official Online Sunshine text (leg.state.fl.us, 2026 Florida Statutes) in the desktop browser pane; excerpts and page hashes in the FL retro sources (lease-clause-decision-log-FL.md, Retro checks (SOP 1.30)).",
