    bodyText:
      "Florida condominium and homeowners' associations can affect a rental in three ways. First, approval: if the association's documents require it to approve leases, a condominium association may charge an approval fee only if its documents provide for one, capped at $150 per applicant (adjusted for inflation every 5 years) and not charged for a renewal with the same tenant; it may also require the tenant to put up a deposit of up to one month's rent to protect the common areas, handled like a landlord's deposit. Second, rental restrictions: a homeowners' association rule adopted after July 1, 2021 that prohibits or regulates rentals binds only owners who buy after it is adopted or who consent to it, except that any association may regulate leases shorter than 6 months and limit rentals to three times a year. Third, rent demands: if the owner of a condominium unit or of a home in a homeowners' association falls behind on association charges, the association can demand in writing, using a notice form set by statute, that the tenant pay the rent to the association until the owner's debt is paid. The tenant must then pay the association and is immune from the landlord's claim for the rent paid to it. The tenant also gets credit against the rent owed to the landlord, and the association can serve Chapter 83 notices and evict the tenant for not paying it. A landlord may not retaliate against a tenant for paying rent to an association on its demand.",
    notes: "FL: Gap found by the landlord-scenario screen (gap-discovery source 3, FL log, gap-discovery backfill). Primary text read section-open 2026-09-27 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov), with history line. Controlling text: Fla. Stat. §718.112(2)(k) (approval fee, lessee deposit); §720.306(1)(h)1-5 (HOA rental restrictions after 2021-07-01; s. 22, ch. 2021-99); §718.116(11)(a)-(f) (condominium rent demand: prescribed notice to the tenant, notice to the owner, receipts, tenant immunity, liability capped at rent owed, landlord credit, association may use §83.56 notices and §§83.59-83.625 eviction but has no §83.51 duties, receiver may supersede; s. 19, ch. 2023-203 latest); §83.64(1)(e) (retaliation). HOA rent demand: Fla. Stat. §720.3085(8)(a) and its prescribed tenant notice read verbatim on flsenate.gov 2026-09-27 (demand; pay until the debt is paid and the association releases the tenant or the tenancy ends; 'complete immunity from any claim for the rent by your landlord'); (8)(b)-(f) read in summary only, so the row states the credit and eviction powers for condominiums only (s. 10, ch. 2024-221 latest). Cooperatives (§719.108) show the same mechanism in Taylor's official search snippets but were NOT read section-open; the row does not mention cooperatives. Condominium rental-amendment rule (§718.110) not read. Lease clauses: association-approval-fl, hoa-compliance (tagged FL). | FL retro 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.30) - rule 79: §720.3085(8)(b)-(f), previously read only in a fetch-tool summary, read whole: (c) 'The tenant shall be given a credit against rents due to the landlord' and (d) the association 'may issue notice under s. 83.56 and sue for eviction under ss. 83.59-83.625', as for condominiums. The body's condominium-only limit on credit and eviction is removed. §718.116(11), §718.112(2)(k) and §720.306(1)(h) re-read whole; consistent. Cooperatives: §719.106(1)(i) read (approval fee capped at $100 per applicant; lessee deposit up to 1 month's rent; refund or claim within 15 days); the body still names condominiums and HOAs only. Read section-open 2026-10-03 on the official Online Sunshine text (leg.state.fl.us, 2026 Florida Statutes) in the desktop browser pane; excerpts and page hashes in the FL retro sources (lease-clause-decision-log-FL.md, Retro checks (SOP 1.30)).",
