    topicKey: "rent-tax",
    bodyText:
      "Florida charges a 6 percent state tax on rent for living quarters in a hotel, apartment house, rooming house, tourist or trailer camp, mobile home park, recreational vehicle park, condominium or timeshare resort. The tax does not apply to a tenant with a bona fide written lease longer than 6 months for continuous residence, to a tenant who has lived continuously in the same place for more than 6 months and has already paid the tax for the first 6 months, or to rentals in buildings intended primarily as people's permanent residence. Full-time postsecondary students and active-duty military are also exempt. A lease of 6 months or less, and a month-to-month tenancy during its first 6 months, can therefore carry sales tax, and county tourist taxes may apply as well. Whether the tax reaches a single-family house is not settled by this statute's list of facility types.",
    notes: "FL: Gap found by the landlord-scenario screen (gap-discovery source 3, FL log, gap-discovery backfill). Primary text read section-open 2026-09-27 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov), with history line. Controlling text: Fla. Stat. §212.03(1)(a) (taxable privilege; 6 percent; quoted in part), §212.03(4) (bona fide written lease longer than 6 months; continuous residence after 6 months' tax; read verbatim; history ends s. 2, ch. 2014-40), §212.03(7)(a) (students, active-duty military, buildings intended primarily for permanent residence; read in part). Lead: the Supreme Court-approved lease's §4 'taxes' line. Not read: county tourist development taxes (§125.0104), Department of Revenue rules, ch. 509 transient-rental licensing. Transient occupancy is outside Part II (§83.42(3)). | FL retro 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.30) - rule 37: §212.03(4) exempts a 'bona fide written lease for longer than 6 months' or a person who has resided continuously longer than 6 months and paid 6 months' tax; a month-to-month tenant has no such lease, so the body now names the first 6 months of a periodic tenancy. Rule 79: §212.03(1)(a), (2), (4) and (7)(a)-(c) re-read whole (previously 'quoted in part'/'read in part'); body otherwise holds. Read section-open 2026-10-03 on the official Online Sunshine text (leg.state.fl.us, 2026 Florida Statutes) in the desktop browser pane; excerpts and page hashes in the FL retro sources (lease-clause-decision-log-FL.md, Retro checks (SOP 1.30)).",
