    bodyText:
      "If a tenant never cashes a Florida landlord's deposit refund, the money does not become the landlord's. Under Florida's unclaimed property law, intangible property a business holds or owes is presumed abandoned after a dormancy period, generally 5 years from when it became payable unless another period is specified by law, and must then be reported and delivered to the state. A landlord should keep records of the refund and the tenant's last known address.",
    notes: "FL: Gap found by the landlord-scenario screen (gap-discovery source 3, FL log, gap-discovery backfill). Primary text read section-open 2026-09-27 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov), with history line. Controlling text: Fla. Stat. §717.102(1) (general 5-year dormancy; 'Unless otherwise specified by law'; s. 36, ch. 2026-174 latest). Not read: the rest of ch. 717, including any shorter period for a specific property type, holder reporting (§717.117) and due-diligence notice rules. The row therefore states the general rule and flags that a specific period may apply. Scenario: deposit refund never cashed. | FL retro 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.30) - rule 79: §717.102(1) re-read; consistent. Read section-open 2026-10-03 on the official Online Sunshine text (leg.state.fl.us, 2026 Florida Statutes) in the desktop browser pane; excerpts and page hashes in the FL retro sources (lease-clause-decision-log-FL.md, Retro checks (SOP 1.30)).",
