    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "towing",
    bodyText:
      "A Florida property owner, a tenant of the property, or someone either authorizes (including a condominium association's representative) may have a vehicle parked without permission towed by a regular towing business, without paying for the tow. Except for property that is part of a single-family residence, or when the vehicle's owner is told in person that the space is reserved, the owner must first post permanent signs at each driveway entrance: within 10 feet of the road, with light-reflective letters at least 2 inches high stating that unauthorized vehicles will be towed at the owner's expense, the words \"tow-away zone\" in letters at least 4 inches high, the tower's name and phone number, mounted 3 to 6 feet above the ground, and in place at least 24 hours before any tow. The tower must store the vehicle nearby, notify the police within 30 minutes, release it within 1 hour of request, and stop and release it for half the posted rate if the owner arrives during the tow. A person who improperly causes a tow is liable for the removal and storage costs, damages, attorney's fees and court costs. Counties and cities may add rules and set maximum towing rates.",
