    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "foreign-ownership",
    bodyText:
      "Kansas has no statute restricting the ownership of real property by foreign adversaries or by entities tied to designated countries. The legislature passed one in 2024 — House Substitute for Senate Bill 172 — and the Governor vetoed it on May 10, 2024. The veto was not overridden: the bill appears in the 2024 session laws only as a governor's veto message, with no session-law chapter number, and no Kansas enactment on the subject followed in 2025 or 2026. So a Kansas landlord selling to, buying from, or leasing to an entity with foreign ownership faces no state-law ownership restriction or registration duty of this kind. Federal rules still apply on their own terms, including CFIUS review of certain real estate transactions near sensitive sites and the federal agricultural foreign-investment reporting requirement, neither of which this Kansas absence affects.",
    notes: "KS: NEW ROW 2026-09-27, landlord-scenario screen (gap-discovery source 3), scenario 'buyer is a foreign-adversary entity'. Arizona carries edu-foreign-adversary-land-ban-az; the Kansas answer is the opposite and is worth a row for exactly that reason. | THIS IS PROOF BY LEGISLATIVE REFUSAL, the strongest form of proof-of-absence this project recognises: not 'no law found' but a bill passed and vetoed. Veto message read on the Kansas Secretary of State's 2024 session-laws site, quoting 'Therefore, under Article 2, Section 14(a) of the Constitution, I hereby veto House Substitute for Senate Bill 172', dated May 10, 2024. | NOTE THE INSTRUCTION-17 TRAP THIS PASS WALKED INTO AND OUT OF: search results led with headlines reading 'Legislature adopts ban on foreign adversary property ownership' (May 2, 2024), which would have supported a row asserting Kansas HAS such a law. The veto was nine days later. A pass that stopped at the first confident headline would have shipped the opposite finding. | THE VETO-SUSTAINED CONCLUSION IS INFERRED, not read: it rests on the absence of a 2024 session-law chapter for the bill plus contemporaneous reporting, not on a journal entry recording a failed override vote. A 2025-2026 search located no Kansas enactment; a national legislative tracker listing 2025 foreign-ownership enactments does not include Kansas. TO CLOSE COMPLETELY: confirm from the House or Senate journal that no override vote succeeded. | THE FEDERAL SENTENCE (CFIUS, agricultural foreign-investment reporting) IS RECALLED, NOT READ THIS PASS. Flagged under instruction 16 as non-state-code citations needing separate monitoring.",
