    topicKey: "pool-safety",
    bodyText:
      "Kansas has no state statute imposing a pool fence, barrier, gate or safety-notice requirement on a residential landlord. The Kansas pool rules that do exist sit in the Department of Health and Environment's child care regulations — K.A.R. 28-4-129 for child care centers and K.A.R. 28-4-594 for family child care homes — and they apply because of the licensed child care activity, not because the property is rented. Pool barrier requirements in Kansas therefore come from whichever building or pool code the city or county has adopted, which varies from place to place. Two things follow for a landlord. Check the local adopted code for each property with a pool rather than assuming a statewide rule exists, since the absence of a state statute is not the absence of a requirement. And note the overlap with the residential-use clause: if a tenant runs a licensed child care operation out of the home, the K.A.R. 28-4 pool and fencing rules start applying to your property through the tenant's license, which is one more reason the residential-use-only restriction is worth enforcing.",
    notes: "KS: NEW ROW 2026-09-27, landlord-scenario screen (gap-discovery source 3), scenario 'pool at the property'. Arizona carries pool-safety-notice-az as a REQUIRED disclosure (A.R.S. pool notice duty); Kansas has no analog, so the Arizona clause must not be imported (instruction 18). | EVIDENTIARY BASIS (instruction 15): SECONDARY-SOURCE AGREEMENT plus identification of the two Kansas regulations that DO exist. A targeted search returned only K.A.R. 28-4-129 and 28-4-594 (KDHE child care) and a KDHE policy document on fence-and-gate exceptions for pools at child care facilities. NEITHER REGULATION WAS READ SECTION-OPEN this pass; both are cited on title and evident subject matter. Downstream this should be CONFIRMED_ABSENT, not CITED. | The child-care-licence overlap with residential-use-only is a reasoned inference from what the two regulations cover, not a statement either regulation makes about landlords. Worth verifying if it becomes customer-facing. | K.A.R. citations are administrative-code, not state-code, and need agency-rulemaking monitoring rather than bill tracking (instruction 16) — the same treatment already flagged for the K.A.R. 4-27 bed bug citations in edu-no-bed-bug-disclosure-ks. | Local adopted pool and building codes are flagged, not resolved (instruction 20).",
