    topicKey: "criminal-activity",
    bodyText:
      "Minn. Stat. Sec. 504B.171 writes a covenant into every Minnesota residential lease, oral or written, so you do not need a crime-free clause to have one. Both you AND the tenant covenant not to allow controlled substances in violation of the criminal drug chapter, prostitution or prostitution-related activity, unlawful use or possession of a firearm, or stolen or robbery-obtained property -- and the covenant reaches the common area and curtilage, not just the unit. The covenant is not broken by a third party's conduct unless you or the tenant knew or had reason to know of it, so what matters in practice is what you did once you found out. The tenant separately covenants not to commit domestic-abuse-type acts against another tenant or authorized occupant. A breach voids the tenant's right to possession. Two limits worth knowing: you cannot prohibit a tenant from legally possessing cannabis products, lower-potency hemp edibles or hemp-derived consumer products, and the tenant cannot waive that right -- but smoking or vaping them is outside the protection, so a smoke-free policy is still enforceable. And because the covenant binds you too, allowing the conduct is your own breach, not merely a ground against the tenant.",
    notes: "MN added (gap-discovery backfill 2026-09-27, checklist instruction 36 -- sources 2, 3 and 4 only; not a re-audit). Official Minnesota Revisor full-text search of the 2025 Minnesota Statutes (api.revisor.mn.gov search service, exact-phrase with word variants), every landlord-relevant hit read section-open on revisor.mn.gov. Minn. Stat. Sec. 504B.171, read section-open. Subd. 1(a)(1)(i)-(iv) the four prohibited categories, each extending to 'the premises or in the common area and curtilage of the premises'; subd. 1(a)(2) the common-area controlled-substance clause with its express knowledge qualifier ('unless the landlord or licensor or the tenant or licensee knew or had reason to know of that activity'); subd. 1(b) the tenant's covenant not to commit an act enumerated in Sec. 504B.206 subd. 1(a); subd. 1(c) the cannabis/hemp possession protection with its combustion-and-vaporization carve-out; subd. 2 breach voids the right to possession. Currency: Recent History shows subd. 1 amended by Laws 2023 c 63 art 6 s 55 and subd. 2a added by Laws 2023 c 52 art 19 s 99. INSTRUCTION 19 CHECK THAT PROMPTED THIS ROW: Sec. 504B.171 was cited six times across MN rows but only ever in the NOTES of shared rows (tenant-maintenance, default-by-tenant) or inside rows about something else (edu-cannabis-possession-mn covers subd. 1(c) only; edu-eviction-expungement-mn and edu-lease-copy-defense-mn cite it in passing). No row stated the covenant itself, its common-area-and-curtilage reach, the knowledge qualifier, the subd. 1(b) cross-reference, or the fact that it binds the LANDLORD. The cannabis row is not a substitute: it covers one subdivision of a five-part covenant. Deliberately NOT drafted as a lease clause -- the covenant is imposed by operation of law on every lease including oral ones, so a clause restating it adds no protection and risks drifting from the statute; Arizona by contrast needed crime-free-addendum-az because A.R.S. Sec. 33-1368 gives no automatic covenant. Ties to edu-nuisance-abatement-exposure-mn (Ch. 617) and edu-expedited-eviction-mn (Sec. 504B.321 subd. 2, which names Sec. 504B.171 as an expedited ground).",
