      "Ohio gives a tenant no statutory, non-waivable right to summon police or emergency services. R.C. 5321.02's retaliation protection is close but narrower, and its trigger list is closed: complaining to a governmental agency about a code violation materially affecting health and safety, complaining to the landlord about a R.C. 5321.04 violation, or joining with other tenants to bargain collectively. Calling 911 is on none of those. So a landlord who penalises a tenant for repeated emergency calls faces no specific statutory bar in Ohio - and because Ohio also has no domestic violence tenancy protection, a survivor making repeated calls has no state-law shield either.",
    notes: "OH: CONFIRMED ABSENT, STATUTES ONLY, by official Ohio Revised Code search 2026-09-27 (source 4, outside-title). Instrument note: the codes.ohio.gov results page is JavaScript-rendered and could not be driven by fetch, so searches were run as site-restricted full-text queries against codes.ohio.gov and every landlord-relevant hit was opened. That is a weaker instrument than Arizona's browser-run official search - recorded honestly per instruction 15. Terms run: emergency assistance; law enforcement; police; 911; retaliation. No dedicated provision exists; 5321.02 was re-read section-open 2026-09-27 to confirm the trigger list is closed. Five states carry an emergency-assistance row (MN, CA, NV, SD, TX); Ohio had none. CROSS-REFERENCE: municipal criminal-activity nuisance ordinances can push the other way by penalising a landlord for police calls to the property - not searched, flagged, out of scope by the 2026-09-18 scope decision. Pairs with edu-retaliation-oh and edu-no-dv-lease-termination-oh in this delta.",
