      "Ohio's fair housing statute, R.C. 4112.02(H), does not list source of income or receipt of housing assistance as a protected class, and Ohio has no statewide voucher-acceptance mandate. There is one exception worth knowing, and it is easy to trip over. OAC 4112-5-07(A)(1)(c) makes it unlawful disability discrimination to refuse to consider the sources of a DISABLED applicant's income, or to subject that applicant's source of income or ability to generate income to automatic discounting in whole or in part, because of the disability. So a blanket 'no vouchers' or 'wages only' screening rule applied to an applicant whose income is disability-related benefits can become a disability claim even though Ohio has no general source-of-income protection. Several Ohio cities do protect source of income by ordinance.",
    notes: "OH: CONFIRMED ABSENT, STATUTES ONLY, by official Ohio Revised Code search 2026-09-27 (source 4, outside-title). Instrument note: the codes.ohio.gov results page is JavaScript-rendered and could not be driven by fetch, so searches were run as site-restricted full-text queries against codes.ohio.gov and every landlord-relevant hit was opened. That is a weaker instrument than Arizona's browser-run official search - recorded honestly per instruction 15. Terms run: source of income; lawful source of income; housing voucher; housing choice voucher; section 8. The ONLY hit was OAC 4112-5-07, read section-open 2026-09-18 and relied on here. INSTRUCTION 16 FLAG: the exception is ADMINISTRATIVE CODE, changed by Ohio Civil Rights Commission rulemaking with no bill and no legislative-tracking signal - the same monitoring problem as Ohio's assistance-animal right in the same rule. Ten states carry a source-of-income or deposit-cap style row; Ohio's absence had no CSV row. NOT DETERMINED: whether OCRC or any Ohio court has applied (A)(1)(c) to a voucher-screening rule - no case law searched, flagged. Municipal SOI ordinances flagged, not resolved.",
