      "Ohio gives a servicemember no state-law right to terminate a residential lease on military orders. Two Ohio provisions come close and neither does it. R.C. 1349.02 lets a deployed servicemember or spouse terminate a lease, but it is confined to MOTOR VEHICLE leases - read the definition before relying on it. R.C. 1923.062 gives a deployed tenant a stay or adjustment of an eviction action, not a termination, it runs 90 days unless justice requires otherwise, it reaches only rental agreements entered on or after May 18 2005, and it does not apply to a landlord operating fewer than four residential premises. A servicemember's actual termination right in Ohio is the federal Servicemembers Civil Relief Act, which R.C. 5321.04(A)(10) separately requires you to comply with.",
    notes: "OH: CONFIRMED ABSENT, STATUTES ONLY, by official Ohio Revised Code search 2026-09-27 (source 4, outside-title). Instrument note: the codes.ohio.gov results page is JavaScript-rendered and could not be driven by fetch, so searches were run as site-restricted full-text queries against codes.ohio.gov and every landlord-relevant hit was opened. That is a weaker instrument than Arizona's browser-run official search - recorded honestly per instruction 15. Terms run: servicemember; military; active duty; orders; deployment. R.C. 1349.02 READ SECTION-OPEN 2026-09-27: effective 2005-05-18, H.B. 426 (125th G.A.), and its definition confines it to motor vehicle leases - a NEAR-MISS a future pass would otherwise cite, since it sits in a consumer-protection chapter and reads on its face like a lease-termination statute. This is the inverse of the Arizona 26-168(D) lesson (a section whose heading named another subject carried a general rule); here a section that looks general is confined. R.C. 1923.062 was read in the 2026-09-18 chapter-1923 full read. 5321.04(A)(10) cites the SCRA as '117 Stat. 2835, 50 U.S.C. App. 501', a FROZEN-DATE federal citation since editorially reclassified - instruction 16 flag already recorded for Ohio.",
