    bodyText:
      "North Carolina lets a person who accepts a check 'in payment for goods or services' charge a processing fee of up to $35 if the bank refuses it for insufficient funds or because the account does not exist; whether rent counts is not settled by the statute's words, so treat $35 as the most a landlord may charge for a returned check. If the tenant knowingly wrote the bad check and does not pay the check amount, any bank service charges and the processing fee in cash within 30 days after a written demand sent by certified mail in the statutory form, the landlord may sue for those amounts plus damages of three times the check amount (at least $100, at most $500), after a second statutory-form demand if the landlord chooses to send one; a court may reduce the extra damages for economic hardship. No statute sets a fee for a failed electronic payment.",
