    bodyText:
      "Pennsylvania's eviction procedure is the only lawful way to remove a tenant: only a constable or sheriff executing an order for possession may enter by force and put a tenant out. The 1951 Act describes itself as a complete and exclusive system. No Pennsylvania statute sets a specific money penalty for a lockout, but courts treat self-help as unlawful, and a landlord who disposes of or takes control of property in an occupied unit without the tenant's express permission owes treble damages, attorney fees and costs. A landlord who holds the utility account may not have service shut off at its request unless the tenants agree, and may not retaliate against tenants who pay the utility to keep service on.",
    notes: "CONFIRMED ABSENT (statutes), searched two ways on 2026-09-29 (PA log §17): (1) regular-expression search over the full text of the Pennsylvania Constitution and all 74 titles of the Pennsylvania Consolidated Statutes (75 files) loaded in the built-in browser from the official title files (29.28 million characters; control term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 hits, 'security deposit' 8 hits in 6 sections, 'carbon monoxide' 1 hit, so the engine reports true empties); (2) the General Assembly's unconsolidated-statute keyword search (quoted phrases, AND/OR; control term 0 results; '\"security deposit\" AND residential' 15). The Landlord and Tenant Act of 1951 was read whole. Administrative rules (Pa. Code), court rules and local ordinances were not searched unless stated; case law not read (instruction 16). Searched 'self-help|lock ?out|change the locks' near tenant terms (Pa.C.S.: 0 landlord-tenant hits; unconsolidated: \"self-help\" AND tenant 5 results, all capital-budget items; lockout AND tenant 3, unemployment compensation). No express lockout statute. Controlling text read: 68 P.S. § 250.602 (Act § 602), 68 P.S. § 250.503 (Act § 503), Pa.R.C.P.M.D.J. 519 (court rule: the executing officer uses 'such force as may be necessary'); 68 P.S. § 250.505a(f), (i) (Act § 505.1); 66 Pa.C.S. § 1521 ('termination' includes cessation at the landlord ratepayer's request when a tenant does not agree), 66 Pa.C.S. § 1523(b), 66 Pa.C.S. § 1531. Case law on self-help and wrongful eviction not read (instruction 16). Evidentiary basis: full-corpus search plus full read of the 1951 Act (instruction 15).",
