    topicKey: "retaliation",
    bodyText:
      "Pennsylvania has no general anti-retaliation statute for residential tenants, but several statutes protect specific acts. A landlord may not end or refuse to renew a lease because a tenant or family member takes part in a tenants' organization. A landlord who threatens or takes reprisals against a tenant for paying a landlord's unpaid utility bill to keep service on, or for deducting that payment from rent, owes the greater of two months' rent or actual damages, plus costs and attorney fees, and a notice to end the tenancy, a rent increase or a substantial change in terms within six months is presumed retaliatory. In cities covered by the Rent Withholding Act, a tenant may not be evicted for any reason while rent is paid into escrow for a unit certified unfit. A municipality may not penalize a tenant or landlord for calls for police or emergency help by or for a victim of abuse or crime.",
    notes: "PA: Read section-open 2026-09-29 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official site (palegis.us) and, for court rules and regulations, the official Pennsylvania Code site (pacodeandbulletin.gov): The Landlord and Tenant Act of 1951 (1951 P.L. 69, No. 20) read whole from the official unconsolidated-statute text with every amendment note (newest: Act 88 of 2024, 2024 P.L. 944); the Pennsylvania Constitution and all 74 titles of the Pennsylvania Consolidated Statutes (75 files) loaded from the official title files (29.28 million characters; revision stamps 2022-03-07 to 2026-09-16) and searched whole; all 262 acts of 2024, 2025 and 2026 screened in full text for landlord-tenant terms; no special-session act in 2024-2026 (PA log §1). P.S. numbers are Purdon's, which the official site does not print; each 1951-Act cite gives the Act section in parentheses (kickoff rule). Controlling text: 68 P.S. § 250.205 (Act § 205, added 1984 P.L. 1003, No. 203: no lease 'terminated or nonrenewed on the basis of the participation of any tenant or member of the tenant's family in a tenants' organization or association'; 'tenants' organization or association' defined in 68 P.S. § 250.102 (Act § 102)); 66 Pa.C.S. § 1531 (utility reprisals: two months' rent or actual damages, costs and fees; six-month presumption, not for nonpayment of rent not withheld under 66 Pa.C.S. § 1529); 35 P.S. § 1700-1 (City Rent Withholding Act § 1); 53 Pa.C.S. § 304 (`edu-police-emergency-calls-pa`); PHRA retaliation provisions (43 P.S. § 955(d)) not read section-open beyond the housing clause. Kickoff lead 4 said 68 P.S. § 250.205 is a utility-complaint retaliation rule: it is the tenants'-association rule; the utility rule is 66 Pa.C.S. § 1531. A common-law or UTPCPL retaliation claim is case law, not read (instruction 16). 'retaliat' across the Pa.C.S. corpus: 18 Pa.C.S. witness/official retaliation and 66 Pa.C.S. § 1531 only (PA log §17). The Pennsylvania REALTORS® Form RL (rev. 9/17, rel. 1/18) ¶29(A) promises by contract no rent increase, service decrease or eviction threat for a code complaint, tenants'-organization activity or use of legal rights (PA log §15); no statute requires it.",
