    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "plain-language",
    bodyText:
      "Pennsylvania's Plain Language Consumer Contract Act applies to residential leases (not commercial leases or contracts over $50,000). A lease must be written, organized and designed to be easy to read. Courts consider whether it uses short words, sentences and paragraphs; active verbs; few legal terms; no Latin; personal pronouns or the parties' names; one condition per sentence; clear cross references; no double negatives; readable type size, line length, margins and spacing; boldface section captions; and sharply contrasting ink. It must also carry a statement describing what the tenant could lose on default and listing the tenant's waivers of rights. A landlord that fails the test owes the tenant actual loss, $100, costs and attorney fees, unless it made a good-faith and reasonable effort to comply; the lease stays valid and class actions are barred. The Attorney General will preapprove a lease on request; its guideline is an average of 25 words or fewer per sentence and 60 per paragraph. The Act excludes consumer contracts involving more than $50,000, and the text does not say how that amount is measured for a lease (for example, total rent over the term), so a high-rent or long lease may fall outside it.",
    notes: "PA: Read section-open 2026-09-29 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official site (palegis.us) and, for court rules and regulations, the official Pennsylvania Code site (pacodeandbulletin.gov): The Landlord and Tenant Act of 1951 (1951 P.L. 69, No. 20) read whole from the official unconsolidated-statute text with every amendment note (newest: Act 88 of 2024, 2024 P.L. 944); the Pennsylvania Constitution and all 74 titles of the Pennsylvania Consolidated Statutes (75 files) loaded from the official title files (29.28 million characters; revision stamps 2022-03-07 to 2026-09-16) and searched whole; all 262 acts of 2024, 2025 and 2026 screened in full text for landlord-tenant terms; no special-session act in 2024-2026 (PA log §1). P.S. numbers are Purdon's, which the official site does not print; each 1951-Act cite gives the Act section in parentheses (kickoff rule). Controlling text: 73 P.S. §§ 2201-2212 (Plain Language Consumer Contract Act, 1993 P.L. 128, No. 29, read whole): 73 P.S. § 2203 ('consumer contract' includes a written agreement to lease or rent real property for personal, family or household purposes), 73 P.S. § 2204 (exclusions incl. commercial leases and contracts over $50,000), 73 P.S. § 2205 (test of readability: (b) language guidelines, (c) visual guidelines incl. 'caption sections in boldface type', (d) consumer restrictions statement), 73 P.S. § 2206 (language required by other law does not violate), 73 P.S. § 2207 (remedies; UTPCPL violation), 73 P.S. § 2208 (limits: good faith, four years, no voiding, no class actions), 73 P.S. § 2209 (Attorney General preapproval), 73 P.S. § 2210 (no waiver). P.S. numbers confirmed by 37 Pa. Code § 307.4 (Attorney General statement of policy, read on pacodeandbulletin.gov; administrative, instruction 16), whose Appendix B guides are 'Sentence average 25 words or less' and 'Paragraph average 60 words'. LAYOUT (instruction 28): boldface captions and the consumer-restrictions statement (`consumer-restrictions-statement-pa`) are recorded in the PA log §4 as Addendum M.12 builder items; several shared clauses exceed the 60-word paragraph guide (PA log §4; Open for Taylor item 3). The Pennsylvania REALTORS® Form RL (rev. 9/17, rel. 1/18) meets 73 P.S. § 2205(d)(1) by describing what a landlord may take after a judgment (¶24(A)2) and flagging each waiver in capitals where it appears (¶24(B), ¶29(B)), and its ¶31 notes that the Attorney General has not preapproved added terms (PA log §15). The $50,000 exclusion (73 P.S. § 2204(b)(2)) has no stated measure for a lease. Case law applying the Act to leases not read (instruction 16).",
