    bodyText:
      "Many older Pennsylvania leases let the landlord 'confess judgment' against the tenant without a hearing. A confessed judgment for possession is abolished for a residential lease signed by an individual, so a landlord must use the ordinary eviction process. Confession of judgment for money is abolished for consumer credit transactions; whether that reaches a residential lease is not settled by the rule's text, and courts view these clauses with suspicion. The library includes no confession-of-judgment clause for Pennsylvania.",
    notes: "PA: Read section-open 2026-09-29 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official site (palegis.us) and, for court rules and regulations, the official Pennsylvania Code site (pacodeandbulletin.gov): The Landlord and Tenant Act of 1951 (1951 P.L. 69, No. 20) read whole from the official unconsolidated-statute text with every amendment note (newest: Act 88 of 2024, 2024 P.L. 944); the Pennsylvania Constitution and all 74 titles of the Pennsylvania Consolidated Statutes (75 files) loaded from the official title files (29.28 million characters; revision stamps 2022-03-07 to 2026-09-16) and searched whole; all 262 acts of 2024, 2025 and 2026 screened in full text for landlord-tenant terms; no special-session act in 2024-2026 (PA log §1). P.S. numbers are Purdon's, which the official site does not print; each 1951-Act cite gives the Act section in parentheses (kickoff rule). Controlling text: Pa.R.C.P. 2970 ('Confession of judgment in ejectment is abolished with respect to a residential lease executed by a natural person'), Pa.R.C.P. 2950 ('action' excludes an instrument executed by a natural person in connection with a consumer credit transaction; 'consumer credit transaction' = credit offered or extended to a natural person for personal, family or household purposes) - COURT RULES read on pacodeandbulletin.gov (231 Pa. Code; instruction 16). 68 P.S. § 250.511 (Act § 511) still preserves an 'amicable action of ejectment ... by confessing judgment in accordance with the terms of any written contract' - overtaken for residential leases by Rule 2970. Whether a residential lease is a 'consumer credit transaction' under Rule 2950, and the due-process case law on confessed judgments, are not read (instruction 16). The Pennsylvania REALTORS® Form RL (rev. 9/17, rel. 1/18) contains no confession clause (PA log §15). A confession clause would also be a waiver the consumer-restrictions statement must list (73 P.S. § 2205(d)(1)(ii)). No confession clause is offered (Open for Taylor item 4, recommendation: none).",
