    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "rent-control",
    bodyText:
      "Where Civ. Code 1947.12 applies, you may not raise rent over any 12-month period by more than 5% plus the change in the cost of living, or 10%, whichever is lower, measured against the lowest rent charged for the unit in the 12 months before the increase, and you may not impose more than two increases in any 12 months. Discounts, incentives, concessions and credits are left out of the calculation and must be listed separately in the lease (the lease's Rent Discounts and Concessions clause does this). Give notice of any increase as Civ. Code 827 requires (see the rent-increase notice note). The cost-of-living figure is regional and changes each year, and several exemptions apply (see the rent-increase cap row's notes and the TPA notices). Two exemptions change under you rather than staying put. A building is exempt while its certificate of occupancy is less than 15 years old, which means a building that is exempt when you sign becomes covered on its fifteenth anniversary, part way through a tenancy. And the exemption for a separately saleable home depends on who owns it: if the property passes to a corporation, a real estate investment trust or an LLC with a corporate member, the exemption is lost and the exemption statement printed in the lease is no longer true. Re-check both whenever a lease is renewed or the property changes hands.",
    notes: "CA: Created by the three-bucket scrub (2026-09-29) from rent-increase-cap-ca; content unchanged: Civ. Code § 1947.12. | CA: ADDED 2026-10-03, SOP 1.31 retro check 28 (rule 32). The rolling 15-year certificate-of-occupancy exemption (Civ. Code ss1947.12(d)(4), 1946.2(e)(7)) and the ownership-dependent separately-alienable exemption (ss1947.12(d)(5), 1946.2(e)(8), read section-open 2026-10-03) both expire or fail DURING a tenancy, and neither was stated anywhere a landlord would see it. Neither has a notice duty attached, so there is no clause to self-limit - which is why this belongs in education. See tpa-exemption-notice-ca for why that clause's verbatim statement was not wrapped in a self-limiting sentence.",
