    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "fee-transparency",
    bodyText:
      "For residential leases entered into after the law's effective date (official records give January 1, 2027; the Act's own text says July 1, 2026), every non-optional fee, one-time or recurring, must appear explicitly on the first page of the lease, or the tenant is not liable for it. A listing must disclose non-optional fees clearly and conspicuously (or through an accompanying web link), and the lease or listing must say whether utilities are included in rent. No landlord or lease may require: an application fee, including background checks, over $50 (a landlord may charge the actual cost of a third-party background check above $50 only if it paid the cost up front and bills the applicant with receipts within 14 days, or the fee is waived, and the fee can never be a basis for eviction in the first lease year); a fee at application that duplicates screening costs or covers costs unrelated to screening; a fee for modifying or renewing a lease; a fee for an eviction notice or for filing an eviction before the court grants an eviction order (court costs and filing fees remain recoverable); a fee for after-hours maintenance requests; a fee for contacting the owner or manager about maintenance, the lease or the tenancy; a maintenance travel fee; a maintenance-hotline fee; a fee for routine maintenance and upkeep; a pest-abatement fee where the tenant in no way contributed to the infestation; or a fee for a move-in or move-out walk-through. Renaming a fee does not avoid the law. Leases for units in owner-occupied premises of 6 units or fewer are exempt. Local governments may regulate fees, but only in a way at least as restrictive. A tenant may sue for injunctive and monetary relief, attorney's fees and costs.",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 705/35(a)-(f) (added by P.A. 104-479). Applies to 'all lease agreements for residential rental property entered into after the effective date' (765 ILCS 705/35(e)). Effective date: P.A. 104-479 Section 99 reads 'This Act takes effect July 1, 2026', while the Public Act page, bill status (HB 3564, approved 2026-06-26) and the ILCS source note give 2027-01-01; the library treats the law as applying now (IL log §1.2, §6.1). The first-page clause is fee-disclosure-first-page-il. Answers the collection-fee, notice-service-fee, fee-unprovided-service and required-fees topics for IL (IL log §18). Chicago and Cook County fee ordinances not read (flag). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
