    bodyText:
      "Under the rental-fee law (for leases entered into after its effective date, January 1, 2027 in official records; the Act's own text says July 1, 2026), an application fee, including background checks, may not exceed $50, subject to the third-party background check exception described in the rental-fee law, and no additional fee at application may duplicate screening costs. If a prospective tenant offers a reusable tenant screening report that a consumer reporting agency prepared at the tenant's request and expense within the previous 30 days, that is available to the landlord at no cost, and that includes all the criteria the landlord consistently uses in screening (at least the applicant's name, contact information, verification of source of income, last known address and eviction history), the landlord may not charge a fee to access the report or an application screening fee. The landlord may require the applicant to state that there has been no material change in the report's information, and may still collect and process an application of its own as long as it charges no screening fee for it. A local rule that protects prospective tenants more controls.",
