    bodyText:
      "A landlord who keeps any part of a residential security deposit for damage must, within 30 days after the tenant vacates or the tenant's right of possession ends, whichever is later, give the tenant an itemized statement of the damage and the estimated or actual cost of each repair or replacement, with paid receipts (or copies), by personal delivery, postmarked mail to the last known address, or email to a verified email address the tenant provided. If costs are estimated, the paid receipts follow within 30 days after the statement. The landlord may charge the reasonable cost of its own labor. Costs the written lease specifies for cleaning, repair or replacement may be withheld if they are for damage beyond normal wear and tear and reasonable; the statement must reference the amount and include a copy of that part of the lease. If receipts cannot be produced through no fault of the landlord, a verified statement with other evidence of cost may be used. Without a proper statement and receipts, the whole deposit must be returned within 45 days of the tenant vacating. A landlord is not liable where the tenant gave no mailing or email address. A court that finds the landlord refused to give the statement, or gave it in bad faith, and failed to return the deposit on time, awards twice the deposit due plus court costs and reasonable attorney's fees. A tenant does not forfeit a deposit solely because of an eviction for criminal activity or drug use, although the deposit may pay the sheriff's eviction fees in a criminal-activity eviction.",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 710/1(a)-(c) (P.A. 103-224, eff. 1-1-24; no unit-count threshold); 735 ILCS 5/9-120(c); 765 ILCS 705/5(b); 740 ILCS 40/11(b); radon: 420 ILCS 46/30(c) (edu-radon-tenant-rights-il). Clauses: security-deposit-return-il, deposit-cost-schedule-il, security-deposit-use (tagged IL). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
