    bodyText:
      "A landlord of residential property with 25 or more units, in one building or a complex on contiguous parcels, must pay interest on any security deposit held more than 6 months. The rate is the rate the largest commercial bank (by total assets) with its main banking premises in Illinois paid on minimum-deposit passbook savings accounts as of December 31 of the year before the rental agreement began, computed from the date of the deposit. Within 30 days after the end of each 12-month rental period the landlord must pay accumulated interest of $5 or more, in cash or as a credit against rent, unless the tenant is in default; all unpaid interest is due when the tenancy ends. A landlord who willfully fails or refuses to pay is liable for an amount equal to the deposit, plus court costs and attorney's fees. The Act does not apply to public housing. Some local ordinances, including Chicago's, have their own interest rules (not covered).",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 715/1 (P.A. 87-386; 88-449), 765 ILCS 715/2 (P.A. 99-253), 765 ILCS 715/3. The transferee of the property is liable for deposits 'including statutory interest' (765 ILCS 710/1.1). Payment method is the landlord's statutory choice; no lease clause needed (restating a landlord duty is education, rule 55). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
