    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "security-devices",
    bodyText:
      "In counties of more than 3,000,000 people (Cook County), after a unit with a written lease is vacated and on or before the day a new tenant takes possession, the landlord must change or rekey the lock (replace the lock or cylinder, change a combination or electronic code, or otherwise change the means of access). A landlord who fails to do so is liable for damages from a theft attributable to the failure. The duty does not apply if the written lease gives the tenant the right to change or rekey the lock, to an owner-occupied building of 4 or fewer units, or to a room in an owner-occupied home. Statewide, the owner of a rental building of 5 or more units whose construction began after the peephole statute took effect (enacted in 1967) must provide and maintain a peephole in each unit's entrance door; a tenant may sue to compel it, and each violation is a petty offense ($25 to $100 per unit, each day a separate offense). Hotels, motels, dormitories and similar buildings are excluded.",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 705/15 (P.A. 97-470). Cook County as the only county over 3,000,000 comes from secondary knowledge; the census was not read (IL log §7). 430 ILCS 160/1, 430 ILCS 160/2, 430 ILCS 160/3 (Peephole Installation Act; Laws 1967, p. 1803; P.A. 79-1362), found by battery 121 (deadbolt, peephole, door viewer, window latch, security device near landlord terms: 1 hit); battery 39 unreliable (ungrouped alternation). No deadbolt or window-latch statute located. Clauses: keys-il, tenant-rekey-right-il. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
