    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "utility-landlord-account",
    bodyText:
      "Where an agreement requires the landlord to pay for water, gas or electric service, the landlord must pay on time so service continues through the lease. If the landlord does not pay, the tenant (or all tenants served by a common meter) may end the lease, or pay the utility if nonpayment jeopardizes service and deduct the payment from rent; the landlord may not raise rent to recover lawful deductions. A utility must give tenants of buildings with 3 or more apartments notice before shutting off service for the landlord's nonpayment, and must restore service to a tenant who pays the past-due amount, or who puts the account in the tenant's own name, meets the utility's credit or deposit rules and agrees to pay future bills. Tenants who receive a utility's termination notice, or the utility, may also ask a court to appoint a receiver to collect rents and pay current utility bills. A tenant billed for service outside the unit because of the landlord's violation may recover it (trebled if knowing or intentional, plus attorney's fees if the utility award exceeds $3,000).",
