    topicKey: "disability-accommodation",
    bodyText:
      "A landlord must permit, at the expense of a person with a disability, reasonable modifications of the premises needed for full enjoyment of them, and must make reasonable accommodations in rules, policies, practices and services. Where reasonable, the landlord may condition a modification on the tenant agreeing to restore the interior at the end of the tenancy (reasonable wear and tear excepted), on a reasonable description of the work, and on assurances that it will be done in a workmanlike manner with required permits. The landlord may not increase the customary security deposit for a person with a disability, but where necessary to ensure restoration funds it may negotiate payments into an interest-bearing escrow account, over a reasonable period, of a reasonable amount not exceeding the restoration cost, with the interest going to the tenant. Refusing to rent to, or discriminating against, a blind, hearing-impaired or physically disabled person because of a guide, hearing or support dog is also a civil rights violation.",
    notes: "IL: 775 ILCS 5/3-102.1(A)-(C) (Illinois Human Rights Act); 775 ILCS 5/3-104.1. Assistance animals: edu-assistance-animals-il. The shared no-alterations clause (tagged IL) preserves 'reasonable modification that applicable law entitles Tenant to perform'. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
