    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "termination-notice",
    bodyText:
      "Illinois notice rules depend on the kind of tenancy. Fixed term: no notice is needed; the tenant must leave when the term ends. Year to year: 60 days' written notice ending at the end of the year, which may be given at any time in the 4 months before the last 60 days. Week to week, where the tenant holds over without special agreement: 7 days' written notice. Any other term of less than a year, including month to month: 30 days' written notice. Unpaid rent: a written demand giving at least 5 days to pay. Breach of any other lease term: a 10-day written notice to quit, which the statute does not require to offer a chance to cure and gives a suggested form for. Use of the premises for a felony or Class A misdemeanor, or drug activity: the landlord may void the lease and give 5 days' written notice to vacate. After a foreclosure, a new owner may end a bona fide lease only at the end of its term, and a month-to-month or week-to-week lease, with at least 90 days' written notice, except on the ordinary grounds such as nonpayment, breach or criminal activity. Demands and notices are served by delivering a copy to the tenant, leaving it with a person 13 or older residing on or in possession of the premises, certified or registered mail with a returned receipt, or, if no one is in actual possession, posting it on the premises. Once a tenancy is ended by one of these notices, no further demand is needed before filing.",
    notes: "IL: 735 ILCS 5/9-205, 735 ILCS 5/9-207, 735 ILCS 5/9-207.5, 735 ILCS 5/9-208, 735 ILCS 5/9-209, 735 ILCS 5/9-210, 735 ILCS 5/9-211, 735 ILCS 5/9-213; 735 ILCS 5/9-120(d); 765 ILCS 705/5(a); 740 ILCS 40/11(a). Farm tenancies (735 ILCS 5/9-206) out of scope. Clause: periodic-tenancy-notice-il. Answers cure-and-eviction-grounds and tenancy-at-will for IL (no separate at-will notice statute located). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
