    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "An Illinois eviction is a court action under Article IX of the Code of Civil Procedure; the complaint states that the landlord is entitled to possession and the defendant unlawfully withholds it. Under Illinois Supreme Court Rule 139, the landlord must attach to the complaint the notice or demand the case rests on, with proof of service, and, for a case based on breach of a written lease under the 10-day notice statute, a copy of the lease or its relevant portions (or, if unavailable, an affidavit on the Supreme Court's standardized form). Under Rule 99.2 (effective March 1, 2026), circuits may run residential eviction mediation or diversion programs, which must give litigants reasonable time to reach counsel and mediation before a default or eviction judgment. A claim for rent may be joined; unrelated claims may not. Either party may demand a jury despite any lease waiver. An eviction order may not be enforced more than 120 days after it is entered unless the court extends it on the landlord's motion, which must include a statutory notice to the tenant. Tenants may raise defenses including domestic or sexual violence, immigration status, retaliation and servicemember protections, described in their own rows.",
    notes: "IL: 735 ILCS 5/9-106(a), (f) (P.A. 104-317, eff. 1-1-26); 735 ILCS 5/9-108; 735 ILCS 5/9-117 (P.A. 99-753; 100-173); Ill. S. Ct. R. 139 (adopted and eff. July 17, 2020) and Ill. S. Ct. R. 99.2 (adopted Dec. 2, 2025, eff. Mar. 1, 2026), read from the Supreme Court's official rule PDFs (court rules, rule 21 flag; other Supreme Court rules and the standardized forms not read). Local circuit rules and mediation programs not read. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
