    topicKey: "expedited-criminal-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "If a tenant or occupant uses or permits use of the premises for a felony or Class A misdemeanor, the lease becomes void at the landlord's option; if the tenant does not leave within 5 days after written notice to vacate, the landlord may file an eviction (or, with consent, assign the right to file to the State's Attorney or corporation counsel), and a resulting eviction order may be stayed no more than 7 days unless all parties agree. A separate emergency procedure lets a landlord of privately owned or housing-authority property seek possession on a verified complaint alleging direct evidence of drug activity, an unlawful firearm, or listed violent or sex crimes on the premises with the knowledge or consent of those named: no predicate notice is required, the hearing is held after 14 days' notice, and continuances and stays are limited to 7 days. Where the landlord and tenant signed HUD's drug-free housing addendum or a similar document, a Class X felony charge on the premises followed by a probable-cause finding or indictment lets the landlord void the lease on 5 days' posted notice. A tenant does not forfeit the security deposit solely because of these evictions (in a criminal-activity eviction it may pay the sheriff's fees).",
