    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "self-help-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "Illinois law prohibits entering land or buildings except where the law allows entry, and then only peaceably, not by force. A landlord recovers possession through the court eviction process. A landlord may not cause or request utility service to tenants in an occupied building to be interrupted, discontinued or terminated, whether by not paying utility bills it is responsible for or by tampering with equipment or lines, except temporary shutoffs for emergencies such as gas leaks or fire, or, on 7 days' written notice to each affected tenant, for building repairs or rehabilitation. A tenant whose service is cut off in violation recovers a full rent abatement for the period plus consequential damages, and where the landlord acted deliberately, recklessly or in bad faith, statutory damages up to $300 per tenant (or $5,000 divided among affected tenants, if less).",
    notes: "IL: 735 ILCS 5/9-101 (P.A. 82-280); 765 ILCS 735/1.4 (P.A. 87-177; 87-895); 765 ILCS 735/2.1. No statute expressly addresses lockouts of residential tenants; case law on forcible entry and lockouts not read. Chicago's ordinance has lockout rules (flag, not read). Answers utility-shutoff-statute and lockout-for-rent-delinquency for IL (no opt-in lockout clause offered). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
