      "Illinois still allows distress for rent: a landlord may seize a tenant's personal property found in the county where the tenant lives for rent due, but only the tenant's own property, and must immediately file the distress warrant and an inventory with the circuit clerk, after which a court case follows. The right continues for 6 months after the term ends. Property exempt from judgment enforcement is exempt from distress (except crops on the leased premises). Landlords also have a statutory lien on crops grown on leased land. The procedure is technical and court-supervised; a lease clause is not needed to use it.",
    notes: "IL: 735 ILCS 5/9-301, 735 ILCS 5/9-302, 735 ILCS 5/9-303, 735 ILCS 5/9-313, 735 ILCS 5/9-315, 735 ILCS 5/9-316 (Part 3 of Article IX read whole). Personal-property exemptions (735 ILCS 5/12-1001) not read. No clause offered (IL log §6). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
