    topicKey: "foreclosure",
    bodyText:
      "After a foreclosure, the mortgagee, receiver, certificate or deed holder, or foreclosure-sale purchaser who takes control may end a bona fide lease only at the end of its term, and a month-to-month or week-to-week lease, with at least 90 days' written notice; an individual who buys at the judicial sale and will live in the unit as a primary residence may end the lease on at least 90 days' written notice. The successor keeps the ordinary grounds to end a tenancy, including nonpayment, breach of the lease, holding over and criminal activity. Eviction files against tenants under this rule are sealed. A holder or purchaser who receives tenants' security deposits is liable for them and must post a notice on each unit within 21 days. A court-appointed receiver may not reject an unexpired lease where the tenant occupies the premises as the tenant's primary residence.",
    notes: "IL: 735 ILCS 5/9-207.5 (P.A. 98-514; (c) preserves termination under 735 ILCS 5/9-118, 735 ILCS 5/9-119, 735 ILCS 5/9-120, 735 ILCS 5/9-201, 735 ILCS 5/9-202, 735 ILCS 5/9-203, 735 ILCS 5/9-204, 735 ILCS 5/9-209 and 735 ILCS 5/9-210; 'bona fide lease' defined in 735 ILCS 5/15-1224, not read); 735 ILCS 5/9-121(c); 765 ILCS 710/1.2; 765 ILCS 1090/17(h) (Illinois Receivership Act, read in part). Answers foreclosure-disclosure for IL: no statute requiring landlords to disclose a pending foreclosure to tenants was located (not separately searched; boundary in IL log §18). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
