    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "retaliation",
    bodyText:
      "A landlord may not knowingly end a tenancy, raise rent, decrease services, sue or threaten to sue for possession, or refuse to renew because the tenant in good faith: complained of code violations to a government agency, elected representative or official; complained of code violations or illegal landlord practices to a community organization, or sought its help; asked the landlord for repairs required by a code, ordinance, regulation or the lease; organized or joined a tenants' union or similar group; testified about the premises' condition; or exercised any right or remedy provided by law. A retaliating landlord faces the tenant's defense to its action and a civil action in which the tenant may end the lease and recover the deposit, interest and prepaid rent, recover possession, and recover the greater of 2 months' rent or twice the damages, plus attorney's fees. Evidence of protected activity within one year before the landlord's action creates a rebuttable presumption of retaliation, unless the activity began after the landlord's action. The landlord's action is not retaliatory if it had a legitimate non-retaliatory basis or began before the protected activity.",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 721/5, 765 ILCS 721/10, 765 ILCS 721/15, 765 ILCS 721/20 (P.A. 103-831, eff. 1-1-25; 765 ILCS 721/20 amended by P.A. 104-417, eff. 8-15-25). The Retaliatory Eviction Act (765 ILCS 720) is repealed (765 ILCS 721/95). Answers tenant-right-to-organize for IL. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
