    topicKey: "emergency-assistance-right",
    bodyText:
      "No Illinois municipality or county may enact or enforce an ordinance or regulation that penalizes tenants or landlords (through fines, license action, subsidy termination, or termination or nonrenewal of a lease) because of a contact to police or emergency services made to prevent or respond to domestic or sexual violence, or made by or about a person with a disability for a disability-related purpose, or because of domestic or sexual violence, or related criminal activity, against a tenant, household member or guest. In an eviction, it is a defense that the demand for possession is based solely on a tenant's or household member's status as a victim of domestic violence, sexual violence, dating violence or stalking, or on an incident of such violence. A landlord may bar a person who is not a tenant or household member by a written notice to the tenant that says the person is no longer allowed on the premises and that inviting the person onto the premises may be treated as a breach of the lease, whether or not the lease says so; it is a defense to an eviction on that ground if the tenant or household member who was a victim of domestic violence, sexual violence, stalking or dating violence did not knowingly consent to the entry, or a valid court order permitted it.",
    notes: "IL: 65 ILCS 5/1-2-1.5 (read in part); 55 ILCS 5/5-1005.10 (P.A. 99-441); 735 ILCS 5/9-106.2(a), (f). Neither statute gives the tenant a direct right against a private landlord beyond the eviction defense. No lease clause needed for the barring power ('whether or not this provision is contained in the lease', 735 ILCS 5/9-106.2(f)). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
