    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "immigration-status",
    bodyText:
      "Unless required by law or court order, a landlord may not threaten to disclose, or disclose, information about a tenant's immigration or citizenship status to anyone, including immigration or law enforcement agencies, to harass or intimidate the tenant, retaliate for the exercise of rights, or influence the tenant to leave, and may not bring an action for possession based solely or in part on the tenant's immigration or citizenship status. A tenant may recover actual damages, a civil penalty of up to $2,000 for each violation, attorney's fees and costs. A waiver of these rights is void. It is also a defense to an eviction that the demand for possession is based solely or in part on the tenant's immigration status, or, after the lease has begun and the tenant has taken possession, on the tenant's failure to provide a Social Security number, credit-report information or a form of identification the landlord accepts. Landlords may still comply with legal obligations (including rental-assistance programs and court orders), verify a prospective tenant's financial qualifications, give notices about lease violations, and collect rent. Immigration status is also a protected class under the Human Rights Act; inquiry or use is allowed only in compliance with State or federal law.",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 755/10, 765 ILCS 755/15 (P.A. 101-439); 735 ILCS 5/9-106.3; 775 ILCS 5/3-102; 775 ILCS 5/3-106(M). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
