    bodyText:
      "No Illinois statute requires a residential landlord to disclose bed bugs or sets treatment duties. For leases it covers, the rental-fee law bars charging a tenant a pest-abatement fee where the tenant in no way contributed to the infestation. Chicago has its own bed bug ordinance (not covered).",
    notes: "IL: Confirmed absent: whole-code battery 12 (bed bug) hit only 610 ILCS 85 (Railroad Sanitation Act) and 815 ILCS 309 (Bedbug Inspection Act, which covers merchants renting furniture or electronics, 815 ILCS 309/5). 765 ILCS 705/35(c)(10). Answers bed-bug-cooperation for IL. Chicago ordinance flagged, not read. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
