    group: "Default & Termination",
    states: ["IL"],
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "conversion-notice",
    bodyText:
      "Before a building is submitted to the Condominium Property Act as a conversion condominium, every tenant must receive a notice of intent at least 30 days and not more than a year before the declaration is recorded, together with a schedule of selling prices and an offer to sell the tenant's unit, open at least 30 days (except units to be vacated for rehabilitation). Tenants whose leases would expire within 120 days after the notice may extend on the same terms until the 120 days end by written notice within 30 days. The developer must tell each tenant whether the tenancy will be renewed and on what terms. For 120 days the tenant has a right of first refusal to buy the unit on substantially the same terms as a signed contract, exercisable within 30 days after notice of the contract. Occupied units may be shown only a reasonable number of times, at appropriate hours, and only in the last 90 days of an expiring tenancy. An owner who converts without the notice is liable to a tenant who moves out because of non-renewal for moving expenses up to $1,500, 3 months' rent and attorney's fees. Lease terms contrary to or waiving these rights are void.",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 605/30 (P.A. 101-81). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
