    title: "Tenant Right to Install EV Charging",
    group: "Parking & Storage",
    states: ["IL"],
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "ev-charging",
    bodyText:
      "In a single-family home or multifamily building that has parking spaces, and notwithstanding the lease, an Illinois tenant may install, at the tenant's expense and for the tenant's own use, a level 1 or level 2 receptacle or outlet or a level 2 electric vehicle charging system, subject to the landlord's bona fide safety requirements, registration within 30 days, reasonable aesthetic rules, and the tenant's written agreement to the landlord's design specifications, a licensed electrical contractor and insurance. The landlord may charge only for electricity used (including network fees), a reasonable access fee, a restoration security deposit, installation costs where the landlord installs at the tenant's request, and a reasonable fee to reserve a shared-area space. A tenant-installed system remains the tenant's property. A landlord who willfully violates the statute owes actual damages and a civil penalty up to $1,000, and a prevailing tenant plaintiff recovers attorney's fees.",
    notes: "IL: 765 ILCS 1085/35 (P.A. 103-53; 103-605). Clauses: ev-charging-requirements-il; ev-charging-shared-area-co and ev-charging-end-of-tenancy-co tagged IL. Applicability (765 ILCS 1085/10(b): existing and new single-family homes and multifamily residential buildings that have parking spaces) read; association provisions not read. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official ILCS pages (ilga.gov, which print each section's history line and flag text from Public Acts with delayed effective dates): 765 ILCS 705, 710, 715, 721, 730, 735, 740, 742, 750, 752 and 755 and Article IX Parts 2 and 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure (735 ILCS 5/9-201 to 5/9-321) read whole; Article IX Part 1 and outside-title sections read in the sections cited; all 3,486 ILCS acts (73,378 section versions) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits; the 854 Public Acts of the 104th General Assembly screened in full text for uncompiled changes (IL log §1).",
