    bodyText:
      "Pennsylvania's landlord-tenant statute supplies one form: the notice to a tenant about property left behind, which must be sent by first class mail to the rental address and to any forwarding or emergency address the tenant gave, in substantially this form: \"Personal property remaining at (address) is now considered to have been abandoned. Within ten days of the postmark date of this notice, you must retrieve any items you wish to keep or contact your landlord at (telephone number and address) to request that the property be retained or stored. If requested, storage will be provided for up to thirty days from the postmark date of this notice at a place of your landlord's choosing, and you will be responsible for costs of storage.\" Note that the form counts storage from the postmark date, while the statute's own rule counts the 30 days from the date of the notice; dating and mailing the notice the same day avoids the gap. Eviction filings before a magisterial district judge use court forms prescribed by the courts. Pennsylvania does not supply a statutory lease form or notice-to-quit form.",
    notes: "PA: PRESENT, SOP 1.2 rule 27 retro 2026-09-30. Pennsylvania Consolidated Statutes (all 74 titles and the Constitution, 75 official title files reloaded 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from palegis.us, 29,516,464 characters) searched by regular expression, control term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 hits; the General Assembly's unconsolidated-statute keyword search, control term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 results; The Landlord and Tenant Act of 1951 read whole (PA log §1). Boundary: administrative rules (Pa. Code), local ordinances and case law not searched unless stated (instruction 16). Battery saved as battery_rule27_2026-09-30.json. Read section-open: 68 P.S. § 250.505a(d)-(e) (Act § 505.1(d)-(e), as amended 2014 P.L. 2620, No. 167); form quoted verbatim from (e). Rule 77 cross-reference: the form says 'thirty days from the postmark date of this notice'; subsection (d) says 'thirty days from the date of the notice' (the ten days run from the postmark in both). `abandoned-property-pa` follows (d) and refers to 'the form Pennsylvania law sets'. Court rule: Pa.R.C.P.M.D.J. 503A (complaint 'on a form prescribed by the State Court Administrator'; court rule, read whole in PA log §3.3). Pa.C.S.: '(tenant|lessee)[^.]{0,300}substantially the following form|substantially the following form[^.]{0,300}(tenant|lessee|landlord)' 0; 'substantially the following form' in 16 sections, none landlord-tenant (42 Pa.C.S. § 6903 is rental-purchase of goods; 42 Pa.C.S. § 6206 unsworn declarations). Unconsolidated: '\"substantially the following form\" AND tenant' 9 results (1951 Act abandoned-property amendment and 1951 Act; the rest unrelated); '\"substantially the following form\" AND lease' 59 results, the only landlord-tenant one the same 1951 Act section.",
