    topicKey: "holdover",
    bodyText:
      "A tenant who stays after the lease ends without the landlord's permission is in unlawful detainer. In the eviction case the court assesses the landlord's damages and may enter judgment for three times the actual damages. Idaho has no statute fixing holdover rent or a double-rent penalty. No Idaho statute bars a lease from setting a daily holdover rate either, but the library does not offer one: the statutory measure already reaches every holdover the landlord sues over, as the actual damages (normally the rental value for the time the tenant stays) that the court may treble, and a contract rate would sit inside that trebled figure. A landlord who wants a set holdover rate can add their own clause and should take legal advice first. For agricultural land, a tenant who holds over for more than 60 days without a demand for possession is treated as renewing for another year.",
    notes: "ID: Idaho Code § 6-303(1)-(2); Idaho Code § 6-316(1); Idaho Code § 6-317 ('judgment may be entered for three (3) times the amount at which the actual damages are assessed'). Whole-code search 'double rent|holding over': 6 sections, none a residential holdover measure (Idaho Code § 6-303 is the agricultural rule). Rule 54 decision (ID log s6): no holdover-rate clause, because a statutory damages measure exists and a contract rate would sit inside the trebled damages. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from legislature.idaho.gov section pages, which print each section's history line and are current through the 2026 session (the site's currency page; its chapter PDFs lag and omit 2026 changes, so every section the 2026 session touched was reloaded from its current page): Idaho Code Title 6, Chapter 3 (all 28 sections in the official chapter index, Idaho Code §§ 6-301 to 6-324; no 6-306, 6-307 or 6-311B is listed) and Title 55, Chapters 2 and 3 read whole; the whole Idaho Code (1,470 chapter PDFs plus 1,166 current 2026-affected sections, 22,935 sections) loaded for full-text search, control term 0 hits (ID log s1, s17). | ID retro 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.38): Targeted fix 3 (rule 54 as amended at the NE sync). Idaho Code § 6-317 ('judgment may be entered for three (3) times the amount at which the actual damages are assessed') applies whenever a landlord 'recovers damages for a ... detention', with no willful or bad-faith condition, and Idaho Code § 6-316(1) has the court assess 'the damages occasioned to the plaintiff by any ... unlawful detainer' for the whole holdover; the measure is the actual damages, not a lump sum, and trebling is discretionary. So the statute leaves no case without a measure: the ordinary good-faith holdover gets actual damages (holdover-ca claims them). Verdict unchanged: no holdover-rate clause. Body now says the option is lawful as far as statute goes, why it is not offered and that a landlord may add their own clause with advice (rule 54: a declined lawful option gets an education row). Whether a court would enforce a premium rate as liquidated damages is case law, not read. Read section-open 2026-10-03: the saved section files from the 2026-09-30 pass, compared with the live legislature.idaho.gov section pages before reuse (Idaho Code §§ 6-201, 6-301 to 6-304, 6-316, 6-317, 6-320, 6-324, 12-120, 26-2229A, 55-305: content identical; ID retro log).",
