    bodyText:
      "Virginia has no statute that writes a covenant of quiet enjoyment into every residential lease. If a lease uses the short phrase that the landlord covenants 'for the lessee's quiet enjoyment of his term', Virginia law gives it a set meaning: a tenant who pays the rent and keeps the lease may peaceably possess the home for the term without interruption or disturbance from anyone. The Residential Landlord and Tenant Act protects the same interest in other ways: it limits the landlord's entry, bars lockouts and cutting off essential services, and makes each tenant responsible for keeping guests from disturbing the neighbors' peaceful enjoyment.",
    notes: "VA: VA-SCOPE: STATEWIDE. Retro check 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.6), rule 27. PRESENT (short-form covenant only): Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1604 (Code 1919, § 5181; read whole). Search: quiet pattern 6 hits (§§ 8.9A-610, 10.1-1436, 38.2-4905, 38.2-4923 unrelated; § 55.1-360 deed covenant; § 55.1-1604) (VA retro battery; control 0; the VA outside-title search had the same result, VA log §17). Related protections: Va. Code Ann. §§ 55.1-1229 (access; edu-entry-and-access-va), 55.1-1243.1 and 55.1-1252 (edu-self-help-eviction-va), 55.1-1227(A)(12). Case law on an implied covenant not read (rule 76 label: unread). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser on the official Code of Virginia (law.lis.virginia.gov/vacode/<section>/ pages; Chapter 12 re-verified the same day against the live vacodefull chapter page), each saved file matched to the browser's SHA-256 of the same text (VA retro section, Sources).",
