    bodyText:
      "After an Alabama eviction judgment for the landlord, the writ of possession issues on the landlord's application once the automatic seven-day stay ends. A sheriff or constable who neglects or refuses to execute the writ owes the landlord $200. A tenant who comes back in without just cause after being removed can be held in contempt, and further writs may issue; going back into possession after being put out under court process is also a crime, punished by a fine of $200 to $1,000 (half paid to the landlord) and at least six months in jail. No Alabama statute sets duties for a tenant's belongings or animals when the writ is executed, or gives landlords immunity for it. The Act lets a landlord dispose of property left in the unit more than 14 days after a lawful termination, with no duty to store or protect it. Removing belongings or changing the locks before the writ is executed is self-help the Act forbids.",
