    bodyText:
      "Alabama's Deceptive Trade Practices Act defines 'goods' to include real property and 'sale' and 'buying' to include leasing and renting, so on its face it reaches a residential lease with a tenant renting for personal, family or household use. Its list of unlawful practices includes misrepresenting what is offered (its characteristics, benefits, standard or quality), advertising with intent not to rent as advertised, and false statements about the need for repairs, plus a catch-all for any other unconscionable, false, misleading or deceptive act. The list does not name leaving blanks to be filled in after signing or failing to give a copy at signing. A consumer may recover actual damages or $100, whichever is greater, up to three times actual damages at the court's discretion, and attorney's fees, after a written demand at least 15 days before suing; consumers may not sue as a class. Whether the Landlord and Tenant Act's statement that it is the exclusive remedy for rights under a rental agreement limits these claims is not settled by statute.",
