    bodyText:
      "If an Alabama tenant, a member of the tenant's family, or someone on the property with the tenant's consent deliberately or negligently causes a condition, the tenant's repair, termination and essential-service remedies do not arise for it. A fire or casualty caused by the tenant gives the tenant no right to end the lease or pay reduced rent; that exception names only 'the tenant', so a fire caused by a guest may still give the tenant those rights. The tenant must not damage the property, or knowingly, recklessly or negligently let anyone else do so. The landlord may recover actual damages and apply the deposit, and for a health or safety problem that repair, replacement or cleaning can fix, may do the work after seven days' written notice and bill it as rent. The tenant may not withhold rent. If the landlord ends the lease for the breach, the landlord has a claim for rent and actual damages, subject to a duty to mitigate. How Alabama courts measure lost future rent is case law not read here. The library offers an optional clause (`tenant-caused-damage-al`).",
