    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "landlord-self-cure",
    bodyText:
      "If an Alabama tenant breaks the tenant's statutory upkeep duties in a way that materially affects health and safety, and the problem can be fixed by repair, replacing a damaged item or cleaning, the landlord may give written notice describing it and asking the tenant to fix it within seven days (or as promptly as conditions require in an emergency). If the tenant does not, the landlord may enter, have the work done in a workmanlike manner, and bill the tenant for the actual and reasonable cost (or its fair and reasonable value) as rent on the next rent due date, or for immediate payment if the lease has ended. No lease clause is needed for this right, and because the bill is rent it can go in a nonpayment notice once it is due.",
    notes: "AL retro 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.8, rule 27). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from the official Code of Alabama 1975 on ALISON (alison.legislature.state.al.us; whole Code reloaded, 49,638 section versions, control term 0; section text saved and SHA-256 matched to the browser, AL retro sources). Controlling text: Ala. Code § 35-9A-422; Ala. Code § 35-9A-301; entry without consent, Ala. Code § 35-9A-303(b)(3) (already in landlords-access-al); rent definition, Ala. Code § 35-9A-141(12). Present by statute; education, not a clause (the right needs no lease text).",
