    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "for-cause-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "South Carolina has no just-cause or good-cause eviction rule. A landlord may decline to renew a fixed-term lease when it ends, or end a week-to-week or month-to-month tenancy by written notice at least 7 or 30 days before the termination date, without giving a reason, however long the tenant has lived there; the end of the term is itself a ground for ejectment. Situational limits still apply: a landlord may not raise rent above fair-market value, cut essential services or sue for possession in retaliation for a tenant's code complaint to a government agency or a complaint to the landlord about a violation of the Act, and a retaliatory refusal to renew can delay recovery of possession for 75 days; a protected domestic-violence tenant may not be made to leave before 60 days after the incident or be retaliated against for ending the lease; and an owner converting a building to condominiums must give tenants written notice and let them stay until the lease expires or for 120 days (90 if under 60), whichever is longer, except for a lease violation. Paying rent into court lets a tenant keep defending a possession case but does not bar eviction. No statute protects tenants' associations. Fair housing law applies everywhere; federal rules for subsidized housing are not covered here.",
    notes: "SC: SC retro 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.9, rule 41 and 41b). Records the verdict under for-cause-eviction; the situational limits live in their own rows and are only pointed to here. CONFIRMED ABSENT (just-cause rule): Chapter 40 read whole in phase 1 (SC log §0) and the sections below re-read section-open; whole-Code search for tenant organizing (/tenants?'? (association|union|organization|council)|organiz/ over Title 27 Chapters 33-40): no tenant-association protection (definitions of 'organization' only); SC log §17 searches found no good-cause renewal rule. Controlling text: S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-770(a)-(b) (7 and 30 days' written notice, no cause stated); S.C. Code Ann. § 27-37-10(A)(2) (tenant may be ejected when 'the term of tenancy or occupancy has ended'). Situational limits: S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-910(a)-(h) (retaliation; 75 days in (g); `edu-retaliation-sc`); S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-350(D)(1), (E) (2026 Act No. 184; `edu-dv-lease-termination-sc`); S.C. Code Ann. § 27-31-420(A), (E) (failure to give the notice is a defense to possession for 120 or 90 days; `edu-condo-conversion-notice-sc`); S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-790 (rent into court is a condition of defending, not a bar). Rule 41 wording screen: no just-cause rule, so wording that the end of the Term ends possession (`security-deposit-use`, `no-alterations`, `keys`, `early-termination-ks`, `holdover-ca`, `holdover-rate-sc`, `abandoned-property-sc`) is consistent with South Carolina law. Boundary: federal law (LIHTC, Section 8, CARES Act), local ordinances and case law not searched. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from the official South Carolina Code of Laws on scstatehouse.gov (the site states the Code is current through the 2025 session; whole Code reloaded, 1,304 chapter pages, control term 0; 2026 Act Nos. 184 and 252 read from the ratified acts; section text saved and SHA-256 matched to the browser, SC retro sources).",
