    bodyText:
      "If a South Carolina tenant breaks the tenant's statutory upkeep duties in a way that materially affects health and safety, and the problem can be fixed by repair, replacing a damaged item or cleaning, the landlord may give written notice specifying the breach and asking the tenant to fix it within 14 days (or as promptly as conditions require in an emergency). If the tenant does not, the landlord may enter, have the work done in a workmanlike manner, and the tenant must reimburse the cost; the landlord also keeps the Act's other remedies. No lease clause is needed for this right. The statute calls the payment reimbursement, not rent, and the Act's definition of rent excludes charges other than late charges, so whether an unpaid repair bill can support a nonpayment eviction is not settled by the statute. For a health-and-safety breach that repair, replacement or cleaning cannot fix, the landlord may instead end the lease if the tenant does not comply within 14 days after written notice.",
    notes: "SC: SC retro 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.9, rule 27). PRESENT: S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-720(a) (enter, do the work, 'the tenant shall reimburse the landlord for the cost and, in addition, the landlord shall have the remedies available under this chapter'); S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-720(b) (termination for other health-and-safety breaches); S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-510 (tenant duties); S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-210(11) (rent excludes 'other charges'). No clause: the right exists by statute for every South Carolina landlord, so a lease clause would only restate it (as VA and AL decided). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from the official South Carolina Code of Laws on scstatehouse.gov (the site states the Code is current through the 2025 session; whole Code reloaded, 1,304 chapter pages, control term 0; 2026 Act Nos. 184 and 252 read from the ratified acts; section text saved and SHA-256 matched to the browser, SC retro sources).",
