    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "rent-control",
    bodyText:
      "Indiana cities, towns and counties may not regulate rents, and may not regulate tenant screening, security deposits, lease applications, lease terms, disclosures, the parties' rights or landlord fees, unless the General Assembly authorizes it; an ordinance that does is void. This does not apply to housing that receives government funds or benefits to provide reduced rents. Since July 1, 2026, a unit also may not ban or restrict using private homes as rentals, except through general health and safety rules, building and fire codes, reasonable occupancy standards and the permitted registration and inspection programs; units with such rules adopted before 2026 have until January 1, 2028, and certain short-term rental ordinances adopted before 2018 are exempt. Local governments also may not regulate landlord retaliation.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code § 32-31-1-20(a)-(c); Ind. Code § 36-1-20-3.6 (P.L. 157-2026, SEC.229; effective-date clause read in the act, July 1, 2026; IN log §1.2); Ind. Code § 32-31-8.5-6. Registration and inspection limits: edu-rental-registration-inspection-in. Local mandates to join Section 8: edu-source-of-income-in. Municipal ordinances (Indianapolis, Bloomington and others) flagged, not read (rule 3). Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
