    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "rental-inspection",
    bodyText:
      "Indiana limits local rental programs. A city, town or county may not require you to take a class or join a program in order to lease, and may require a rental permit only if it is free, lasts until the property changes hands, and is one permit for a community of five or more units. A registration fee may not exceed $5 a year. A unit managed by a professional manager that passed a qualifying independent inspection in the past 12 months, with the report delivered on time, is exempt from routine local inspections and inspection fees, though the locality may still inspect on a complaint or reason to believe the code is violated. A penalty for a code violation requires notice and a stated reasonable time to cure first. A political subdivision whose rental registration or inspection program was created before July 1, 1984 is exempt from the permit, inspection and registration-fee limits. You may pass a local fee (other than a construction fee) through to tenants (optional clause government-fee-reimbursement-in).",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code §§ 36-1-20-1.2, 36-1-20-1.5, 36-1-20-2, 36-1-20-3, 36-1-20-3.5, 36-1-20-4.1 (HUD-prepared reports valid up to 36 months; mobile home communities inspected by the Indiana Department of Health excluded), 36-1-20-5, 36-1-20-6. Ind. Code ch. 36-1-20 read whole. Which units run pre-July-1984 programs was not determined (IN log §7; rule 3). Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
