    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "term-change-notice",
    bodyText:
      "Before modifying a rental agreement, an Indiana landlord must give the tenant at least 30 days' written notice, unless a written rental agreement provides otherwise. The statute sets the notice; it does not say a landlord may change a lease without the tenant's agreement, and whether it allows that is case law. The rest of this chapter of Indiana law cannot be waived.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code § 32-31-5-4; Ind. Code § 32-31-5-1(a), (c). Whether Ind. Code § 32-31-5-4 permits unilateral modification, or only sets notice for agreed or lease-authorized changes, is case law, not read. Lease-authorized changes in the library: rules-amendment-in and midterm-rent-increase-in (both 30 days). Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
