    bodyText:
      "Under Indiana's security deposit law, and the landlord-tenant chapters that adopt its definitions, 'rent' includes every payment made to the landlord under the rental agreement except a security deposit. The 10-day nonpayment-notice statute sits outside those chapters and does not adopt that definition, so whether a late fee or other charge counts as unpaid 'rent' for that notice is not settled by statute.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code § 32-31-3-6. Chapters adopting the Ind. Code ch. 32-31-3 definitions: Ind. Code §§ 32-31-2.9-1, 32-31-5-2, 32-31-6-1, 32-31-7-2, 32-31-8-2, 32-31-8.5-1, 32-31-9-2, 32-31-10-2, 32-31-11-2, 36-1-20-1; Ind. Code § 32-31-1-22(a) adopts them for that section only. Ind. Code § 32-31-1-6 is in Ind. Code ch. 32-31-1. No 'additional rent' statute (IN battery 106: public-finance leases only). Case law not read. Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
