    bodyText:
      "Every Indiana dwelling must have at least one functional smoke detector outside each sleeping area and on each additional story, including basements, installed as the statute describes; a totally sprinkled building that met building code standards when built is exempt from the smoke detector chapter. The owner, or its manager or rental agent, must install the required detectors and repair or replace one within seven working days after written notice. When you deliver the unit you must get the tenant's written acknowledgment that it has a functional smoke detector, and the occupant must test detectors at least every six months. A landlord who violates the rule at delivery, or does not fix a hard-wired detector within seven days after certified-mail notice, commits a Class B infraction (Class A for a repeat). These requirements cannot be waived, and local codes may be stricter. Indiana has no statute requiring carbon monoxide alarms in rentals.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code §§ 22-11-18-2(b) (sprinkled buildings exempt), 22-11-18-3.5, 22-11-18-3.6 (an insurer may not reduce a claim for a violation), 22-11-18-5(c) (others: Class D infraction), 22-11-18-5.5, 22-11-18-6 (amended by P.L. 28-2026; IN log §1.2); Ind. Code §§ 32-31-5-7, 32-31-7-5(6). Carbon monoxide: CONFIRMED ABSENT as a rental duty (IN batteries 16 and 79; the only 'carbon monoxide detector' chapter, Ind. Code ch. 36-8-24, is fire-department vehicle testing). Separately, fire-safety rules under Ind. Code ch. 22-11-16 require an emergency sign in covered apartments (Ind. Code § 22-11-16-3, read; the chapter's scope and the commission's rules not read; IN battery 121). Local fire and building codes not read (rule 3). Clause: smoke-detector-acknowledgment-in. Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
