    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "assistance-animal-accommodation",
    bodyText:
      "Under Indiana's emotional support animal law, if a person's disability is not readily apparent you may require written verification from a health service provider who treats the person (not one who only sells verification letters) that the person has a disability, has a disability-related need for the animal, and is helped by it; a person who moves from another state may provide documentation from a physician, psychiatrist, social worker or other mental health professional licensed there with whom the person has an ongoing treatment relationship. You may evaluate the documents, but may not charge any fee to keep an emotional support animal. You may require the tenant to follow the lease and rules like other residents, pay for damage the animal causes as other pet owners would, and sign an addendum setting out the owner's responsibilities. A landlord who allows an emotional support animal as a reasonable accommodation is not liable for injuries it causes to others, subject to any other federal, state or local law. The law does not apply to dwellings exempt from Indiana's fair housing act.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code ch. 22-9-7 read whole (P.L. 162-2018): Ind. Code §§ 22-9-7-1, 22-9-7-4, 22-9-7-9 to 22-9-7-11, 22-9-7-13 to 22-9-7-15. Guide dogs: edu-guide-dogs-in. Federal law not read (rule 21). Shared clause assistance-animal-accommodation (tagged IN). Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
