    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "lead-based-paint",
    bodyText:
      "If you renovate, remodel or maintain target housing built before 1960 that is occupied by someone other than you or your immediate family, and the work disturbs more than 20 square feet of exterior paint, more than 2 square feet in any one room, or more than 10% of the painted surface area, Indiana bans open-flame burning, machine sanding, grinding or abrasive blasting without HEPA exhaust control, heat guns above 1,100 degrees or that char paint, and most dry scraping and dry sanding; bars volatile chemical strippers in unventilated spaces; and requires visible exterior paint chips to be cleaned up within 48 hours. Paint is presumed to be lead-based unless a lead-based paint inspection shows otherwise. The federal lead disclosure also applies to pre-1978 housing.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code § 16-41-39.8-13(a)-(g); Ind. Code § 16-41-39.8-1 (chapter does not require abatement); Ind. Code § 16-18-2-198.7. The definition of 'target housing' that Ind. Code § 16-41-39.8-13 uses was not read (IN log §7). Federal renovation rules not read (rule 21). Shared clause lead-based-paint (tagged IN). Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
