    title: "Eviction Record Sealing",
    group: "Default & Termination",
    states: ["IN"],
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "eviction-record-sealing",
    bodyText:
      "Indiana courts must, on their own, seal the records of an eviction case that is dismissed (including when the landlord settles or stops prosecuting), that ends in the tenant's favor, or whose judgment against the tenant is overturned on appeal. If a judgment was entered against the tenant, the tenant may move to seal the records once any money judgment is paid, or after seven years if there was no money judgment. Sealed records are open only under a court order, to law enforcement, or as the law requires. If you file an eviction and then settle, you must dismiss the case unless you still seek damages, and a case you leave idle for 180 days may be dismissed and sealed.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code ch. 32-31-11 read whole (amended by P.L. 128-2025, effective July 1, 2025; IN log §1.2): Ind. Code §§ 32-31-11-1, 32-31-11-3, 32-31-11-4; Ind. Code §§ 32-31-10-3, 32-31-10-4. Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
