    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "post-eviction-property",
    bodyText:
      "After a court awards you possession, you may ask the court for an order allowing removal of the tenant's property. Property not removed by the date in the order may be delivered to a warehouse or a court-approved storage facility, but only after the order and the facility's name and location are personally served on the tenant. Medically necessary items, items used in the tenant's trade or business, and a week's seasonal clothing, blankets and items needed for the care and schooling of a minor child must be released to the tenant free; the facility has a lien on other property for its charges and may sell it if not claimed within 45 days after notice. You are not liable for loss of property a reasonable person would conclude the tenant abandoned, and a lease may not define abandonment differently.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code ch. 32-31-4 read whole: Ind. Code §§ 32-31-4-1 to 32-31-4-5 (45 days since P.L. 154-2025, effective July 1, 2025; IN log §1.2); waiver of the exempt-property protections is void (Ind. Code § 32-31-4-3(c)). Drug nuisance possession orders also use this chapter (Ind. Code § 32-30-8-12(c)). Clause: abandoned-property-in. Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
