    topicKey: "consumer-protection-act",
    bodyText:
      "Indiana's Deceptive Consumer Sales Act covers leases of real property for personal or household purposes, so a landlord who regularly leases is a 'supplier'. Soliciting a tenant to sign terms that are oppressively one-sided, unduly limit remedies or set an unduly excessive price, where unequal bargaining power led the tenant to sign unwillingly or unknowingly, is an unconscionable act treated as a deceptive act; signing a written lease creates a rebuttable presumption that the tenant knew its terms. A tenant's private damages and class actions under the Act do not apply to consumer transactions in real property; how the Act is otherwise enforced was not reviewed here. The Act's list of deceptive acts has no item on leaving blanks at signing or giving a copy at signing.",
    notes: "IN: Ind. Code § 24-5-0.5-2(a)(1), (3) (read in part); Ind. Code § 24-5-0.5-10(b); Ind. Code § 24-5-0.5-4(a)-(b) (read in part: the real-property exclusion from private and class actions); Ind. Code § 24-5-0.5-3(b) (current version from July 1, 2026, list read, no blank-space or copy-at-signing item). Attorney General enforcement provisions not read (IN log §7). Rule 51. Rule 15: written section-open. Read section-open 2026-09-30 in the built-in browser from iga.in.gov (Indiana General Assembly, 2026 Indiana Code, which prints each section with its history line and any future-dated version): Ind. Code art. 32-31 read whole (chapters 1, 2, 2.9, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 8.5, 9, 10, 11 and 12); the whole 2026 Indiana Code (37 title files, 93,246 sections, checked against the site's title index) and the Indiana Constitution loaded for full-text search, control terms 0 hits (IN log §1, IN log §17).",
