    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "Arizona's Rules of Procedure for Eviction Actions put several duties on a landlord who files an eviction. The complaint must state the specific reason for the eviction and when and how the notice was served, attach the notice, and be verified. A money claim must state the rent, the due dates, how late fees are calculated, the amounts due and any rent concession claimed. A nonpayment complaint must tell the tenant how to reinstate before judgment, and the landlord must serve the lease provisions the eviction relies on and, in a nonpayment case, the last six months of the tenant's charges and payments. A landlord who accepted a partial payment must show the court the partial-payment agreement the tenant signed, or the case is dismissed. The court awards late fees and other periodic fees only if a written lease specifies them, and attorney fees only where a statute or written contract provides for them, never more than the landlord paid or agreed to pay. A property manager may not represent the owner in court unless the manager is a lawyer. After a default judgment the landlord must mail or deliver a copy to the tenant. The landlord has 45 days after a judgment for possession to apply for a writ without explaining the delay. If the landlord later concludes the eviction was not valid, it must promptly move to set the judgment aside. Once a judgment is paid in full, the landlord must file a satisfaction of judgment within 30 days under the court rule (the statutes say 40 days) and serve a copy on the tenant; a landlord who does not answer a tenant's motion to compel satisfaction within 15 days risks having the case sealed.",
