    topicKey: "notice-delivery-methods",
    bodyText:
      "Arizona's Electronic Transactions Act lets an electronic record or signature satisfy a law that requires a writing or a signature, but only between parties who have agreed to do business electronically, and either party may refuse to do a later transaction electronically. A notice the law requires in writing can be sent electronically only in a form the recipient can print and store; a record the sender keeps the recipient from printing or storing cannot be enforced against the recipient. Where a law sets a delivery method, the electronic record must still go by that method: an abandonment notice, for example, must be sent by certified mail and posted. Arizona's landlord-tenant act treats a notice as received when it comes to the tenant's attention or is delivered in hand or mailed by registered or certified mail; email is not one of the listed methods, so an emailed notice rests on proof that it reached the tenant. A lease signed on paper may authorize doing business electronically only through the tenant's separate and express agreement that says which parts will be electronic and how, gives an email address, and promises to report a change. The Act has no special exclusion for eviction, default or cure notices.",
    notes: "AZ: NEW 2026-09-30 (SOP 1.13 retro, rule 45: the electronic-transactions act read itself). Controlling text: A.R.S. §44-7003 (scope: exclusions only for wills, initiative and referendum petitions and parts of Title 47; no landlord-tenant or eviction exclusion), §44-7005(B)-(C) (agreement required; right to refuse later transactions cannot be waived), §44-7008(A)-(D) (capable of retention; method prescribed by other law still applies; not variable), §44-7051(C) (paper consumer contract: separate and express assent), §33-1313(B), §33-1370(A). Federal E-SIGN's exclusion of default and eviction notices for a primary residence (15 U.S.C. §7003(b)) was not read. No AZ clause relies on electronic delivery of a default, cure or eviction notice: notices defers to law-prescribed methods; bedbug-obligations-az's electronic notice tracks §33-1319(B)(2). Read section-open 2026-09-30 in Taylor's built-in browser from azleg.gov (official compilation; each section fetched twice, heading number checked); saved to the AZ retro source folder with a SHA-256 match to the browser text (AZ retro log, sources).",
