      "If a tenant, or someone on the premises with the tenant's consent, creates a clear and present danger to the health or safety of other tenants, you, your staff, or others on or within 1,000 feet of the property (for example, assault or threats, illegal use or threatened use of a weapon, or illegal possession of a controlled substance; drug possession by someone other than the tenant counts only if the tenant knew of it), you may serve a single three days' notice of termination and notice to quit and then file for eviction. The notice must state the specific activity and set out the statute's exemption language word for word. A tenant is exempt where someone else did it and the tenant sought a protective order, reported it to police or the county attorney, or sent the statutory trespass letter, and gave you written proof before you sued. Simply keeping a firearm in the unit is never a clear and present danger.",
