    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "Iowa evictions are 'forcible entry and detainer' actions, usually filed in small claims court in the county where the property is. Give a three days' written notice to quit before filing, except after a three-day nonpayment notice has already ended the tenancy or where the occupant entered by force, intimidation, fraud or stealth. The court sets a hearing within 8 days of filing (up to 15 if you ask or agree), and the tenant must be served at least three days before the hearing. If you win, the order directs removal within three days, carried out in the daytime by the sheriff. Only a claim for rent, or for other recovery the statute lists (such as your damages after the lease is terminated), may be joined with the case. An employee of an individual owner, or an officer or employee of the property manager, may bring the small claims case without a lawyer. Waiting 30 days of peaceable possession after the cause arises, with your knowledge, bars the action.",
    notes: "IA: Iowa Code §§ 648.1 (grounds), 648.3 (three-day notice to quit and its service), 648.5 (venue, hearing within eight days or fifteen with plaintiff's consent, service of original notice, continuance), 648.18 (thirty days' peaceable possession bar), 648.19 (joinder limited to rent and listed recovery claims), 648.20 (daytime), 648.22 (removal within three days); small claims, Iowa Code §§ 631.1(2), 631.14(2); Small claims Form 3.6 (Original Notice and Petition for Forcible Entry and Detainer), Iowa Court Rules ch. 3 (read). Procedure is out of scope for clauses (rule 39); duties screened: no post-writ property or animal duty (`edu-no-post-eviction-property-rule-ia`). Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from legis.iowa.gov (Iowa Code 2026 chapter XML; the whole Code and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, IA log §1); no 2025 or 2026 act amends ch. 562A or ch. 648 (IA log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
