      "If you materially fail to comply with the lease or your maintenance duties, the tenant may give a written seven-day notice and end the lease if you do not fix it, and may recover damages. If you deliberately or negligently fail to supply heat, running water, hot water or essential services, the tenant may, after written notice, buy them and deduct the cost from rent, or recover reduced rental value. In an eviction for unpaid rent, a tenant who told you at least seven days before rent was due, and then in good faith fixed a condition costing no more than one month's rent before receiving your termination notice, can deduct the cost. In a rent case the tenant may counterclaim, and the court may order rent paid into court. A tenant may also repair or replace a smoke or carbon monoxide alarm you did not fix within 30 days of written notice and deduct the cost.",
