      "Iowa's consumer fraud statute defines 'merchandise' to include real estate, and the private-action consumer fraud act covers merchandise 'offered for sale or lease'. Deceptive or unfair practices in advertising or leasing a rental can therefore expose a landlord to claims by the Attorney General and possibly by the tenant. Whether a residential lease is 'consumer merchandise' under the private-action act has not been settled in any case read for this library.",
    notes: "IA: Iowa Code § 714.16(1)(e) ('merchandise' includes real estate), Iowa Code § 714.16(2)(a); Iowa Code § 714H.2(4) ('Consumer merchandise' means merchandise offered for sale or lease, or sold or leased, primarily for personal, family, or household purposes), Iowa Code § 714H.3, Iowa Code § 714H.4(1)(a)(4) (excludes merchandise offered by persons licensed under ch. 543B, real estate brokers and salespersons, acting under that license). Application to residential leases: case law not searched (IA log §1.4). IA battery 41: 8 hits, control 0; known positives passed. Rule 51: the consumer act's unfair-practice list has no lease-specific blank-space or copy-at-signing rule (IA battery 108: 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed). Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from legis.iowa.gov (Iowa Code 2026 chapter XML; the whole Code and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, IA log §1); no 2025 or 2026 act amends ch. 562A or ch. 648 (IA log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
