    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "landlord-entry",
    bodyText:
      "The tenant may not unreasonably refuse you entry to inspect, make necessary or agreed repairs or improvements, supply services, or show the unit to buyers, lenders, prospective tenants, workers or contractors. Except in an emergency or where it is impracticable, give at least 24 hours' notice and enter only at reasonable times. You may enter without consent in an emergency, to fix a health-and-safety condition the tenant failed to remedy, during an absence of more than 14 days, or after abandonment or surrender; otherwise only with a court order. Abusing entry lets the tenant get an injunction or end the lease and recover actual damages of at least one month's rent plus attorney fees; a tenant's refusal of lawful entry lets you get an injunction or end the lease and recover your actual damages and attorney fees. An entry without consent can also be a trespass: the Iowa Supreme Court upheld per-entry damages, judging consent by what the tenant's conduct led the landlord to believe.",
    notes: "IA: Iowa Code § 562A.19(1)-(4); Iowa Code § 562A.28; Iowa Code § 562A.29(2); Iowa Code § 562A.35(1)-(2). Butter v Midwest Property Management IC, LLC, ___ N.W.3d ___ (Iowa 2025) (No. 24-1752, filed Dec. 31, 2025, amended Jan. 6, 2026) (read: tenants recovered common law trespass damages for showings without notice and consent; 'Consent is based on what the tenants' actions led the landlord to believe, not the tenants' intent'). Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from legis.iowa.gov (Iowa Code 2026 chapter XML; the whole Code and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, IA log §1); no 2025 or 2026 act amends ch. 562A or ch. 648 (IA log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
