      "Ohio has no statute on setting rents with algorithmic or revenue-management pricing software, and none on sharing rent or occupancy data with other landlords or with a pricing vendor. Federal and state antitrust law and the general law on unfair competition still apply, and Ohio's rent-control preemption (R.C. 5321.19 and 5321.20) says nothing either way.",
    notes: "OH: CONFIRMED ABSENT (statutes), SOP 1.23 rule 27 retro 2026-10-02. Instrument: the official Ohio Revised Code keyword search at codes.ohio.gov, driven in the built-in browser with Taylor's approval 2026-10-02 (quoted phrases; control term 'zqxvbnmwt' returned 0 results, 'radon' returned 23, so the instrument was validated in both directions). This is a stronger instrument than the site-restricted queries used for Ohio's 2026-09-27 backfill, which could not drive the JavaScript-rendered results page. Terms run: 'algorithmic' (5 hits: 1347.12, 1349.19 data-breach notification, 5162.19, 5162.90 and 5164.11 Medicaid automated review tools - none on pricing), 'algorithm'. No rent-setting or data-sharing provision exists. NOT SEARCHED: Ohio's antitrust act (the Valentine Act, R.C. ch. 1331) was not read, so this row states the absence of a RENT-SPECIFIC rule only, not that Ohio antitrust law is silent on landlord data sharing. Flagged. 16 other states carry a row on this topic_key; Ohio had none.",
