    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "quiet-possession",
    bodyText:
      "No Ohio statute states a covenant of quiet enjoyment or quiet possession for a residential lease. The protection a tenant actually has in Ohio comes from two other places. R.C. 5321.04(A)(1) to (6) sets out your repair and habitability duties and R.C. 5321.13(A) makes them non-waivable, and R.C. 5321.15 bars you from excluding the tenant from the premises, cutting off utilities, or threatening either, outside a court process. Ohio courts also treat a lease as carrying an implied covenant of quiet enjoyment as a matter of common law rather than statute. So a clause promising quiet enjoyment adds little in Ohio, and leaving it out takes nothing away.",
    notes: "OH: CONFIRMED ABSENT (statutes), SOP 1.23 rule 27 retro 2026-10-02. Instrument: the official Ohio Revised Code keyword search at codes.ohio.gov, driven in the built-in browser with Taylor's approval 2026-10-02 (quoted phrases; control term 'zqxvbnmwt' returned 0 results, 'radon' returned 23, so the instrument was validated in both directions). This is a stronger instrument than the site-restricted queries used for Ohio's 2026-09-27 backfill, which could not drive the JavaScript-rendered results page. Terms run: 'quiet enjoyment' (1 hit: 1309.610, UCC 9-610 disposition of collateral after default - a warranty of quiet possession on a secured-party sale of goods, not real property), 'quiet possession'. Nothing in ch. 5321 or ch. 5301. 15 other states carry this topic_key; Ohio had none. THE COMMON-LAW SENTENCE IS UNREAD AUTHORITY: no Ohio opinion on the implied covenant was read for this row, so it is stated as the general position and not cited to a case. Flagged per rule 76. The statutory substitutes (5321.04, 5321.13(A), 5321.15) were read section-open on 2026-09-18 and 2026-09-27.",
