    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "cure-and-eviction-grounds",
    bodyText:
      "For a first material breach other than rent (or a breach of the tenant's statutory duties materially affecting health and safety), give written notice describing the breach, with dates and specific facts, stating that the lease will end on a date at least 7 days after the tenant receives it unless the breach is remedied in 7 days, and stating that the lease will end on a second material breach within 6 months. Give it within 30 days of the breach or of learning of it. For a second material breach within 6 months, give a written notice with the same detail stating that the lease will end at least 7 days after receipt; no cure is allowed. A court can grant relief only on the grounds stated in the notice.",
    notes: "NM: NMSA 1978, § 47-8-33(A) (initial material noncompliance with the rental agreement 'or any separate agreement', or noncompliance with NMSA 1978, § 47-8-22 materially affecting health and safety; written notice 'specifying the acts and omissions constituting the breach, including the dates and specific facts describing the nature of the alleged breach'; seven days to remedy); (B) (second material noncompliance within six months: written notice, termination not less than seven days after receipt; a breach more than six months later is a new initial breach); (C) (the initial notice must state that the agreement will terminate on a second noncompliance within six months, and 'to be effective, any notice pursuant to this subsection shall be given within thirty days of the breach or knowledge thereof'); (G) (relief only on grounds in the written notice); (H) (weekend or federal holiday extension). Form 4-902 NMRA (seven-day notice of noncompliance). A late security deposit and late rent caused by delayed assistance checks were not a 'serious violation' under a Section 8 lease (Serna v. Gutierrez, 2013-NMCA-026, as summarized in the compilation's annotation; opinion not read, NM log §1.4). Tagged `default-by-tenant`; rule 43 carve-out: its no-cure exception reaches the second-noncompliance and substantial-violation grounds. Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
