    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "expedited-criminal-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "If a tenant knowingly commits, or consents to another person in the unit or on the premises knowingly committing, a 'substantial violation', you may give a written notice specifying the time, place and nature of the act and stating that the lease will end at least 3 days after receipt. Only the conduct listed in the statute counts: controlled-substance offenses other than misdemeanor possession and use; unlawful use of a deadly weapon; unlawful action causing serious physical harm; sexual assault or molestation; entering another's home or vehicle to commit theft or assault; theft by force or threat of force; and intentional or reckless property damage over $1,000, in the unit, on the premises or within 300 feet. The tenant has defenses for domestic violence victims, for lack of knowledge, and for reasonable self-defense, and a frivolous or bad-faith action costs the landlord two times the monthly rent plus damages and costs.",
    notes: "NM: NMSA 1978, § 47-8-3(V) (definition; 'which shall be the sole grounds for a substantial violation'); NMSA 1978, § 47-8-22(I); NMSA 1978, § 47-8-33(I)-(M); NMSA 1978, § 47-8-40(A)(4) (action not barred by the retaliation section). Form 4-901A NMRA (three-day notice of substantial violation). Domestic violence defense: `edu-dv-eviction-protection-nm`. Optional lease clause for other crimes: `criminal-activity-nm`. Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
