      "In an eviction based on a substantial violation, it is a defense that the tenant is a victim of domestic violence. If the tenant has filed for or obtained a temporary domestic violence restraining order because of the incident behind the notice, or an earlier incident, the court may not issue a writ of restitution. In other cases where domestic violence is raised, the court has discretion to evict only the resident accused while letting the other residents' tenancy continue.",
    notes: "NM: NMSA 1978, § 47-8-33(J) (quoted in substance). The defense applies to actions under NMSA 1978, § 47-8-33(I) only. NM battery 24 (domestic violence / abuse and lease or tenancy): 9 hits, control 0; known positives passed (no other housing protection for domestic violence victims; hits include the confidential substitute address program, NMSA 1978, § 40-13B-3, which makes the secretary of state the participant's agent for mail and service but imposes no landlord duty). Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
