    topicKey: "for-cause-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "New Mexico has no just-cause or good-cause eviction statute: a fixed term ends on its end date, and a periodic tenancy can be ended by the required written notice without stating a reason. Limits still apply: you may not end a tenancy, raise rent or cut services in retaliation for protected tenant activity within the last 6 months; a condominium conversion requires 60 days' notice and an offer to sell the unit to the tenant; a lease term letting a converting declarant end the lease early is unenforceable; and terminations for breach must follow the statutory notices.",
    notes: "NM: CONFIRMED ABSENT (whole-NMSA battery; boundary: NMSA 1978 and the Constitution searched; administrative rules (NMAC), case law and local codes not searched). Battery 95 recorded as failed; NM battery 126 (just cause: whole-code rerun of B105 (B95 failed)): 61 hits, control 0; known positives passed (whole-code rerun of battery 105): hits are hospital, licensing, school, guardianship, investigative-demand and motor-carrier sections and NMSA 1978, § 47-8-43 (continuance for good cause); none limits nonrenewal of a residential tenancy. Situational limits: retaliation (NMSA 1978, § 47-8-39; `edu-retaliation-nm`); condominium conversion (NMSA 1978, § 47-7D-12; `edu-conversion-notice-nm`); notice requirements (NMSA 1978, §§ 47-8-33, 47-8-37). Fixed-term ending: `edu-end-of-term-nm`. Local ordinances not read (rule 3). Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
