    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "holdover",
    bodyText:
      "If a tenant stays after the lease ends without your consent, you may bring an action for possession. Only if the holdover is willful and not in good faith may you also recover the damages you suffer and reasonable attorney fees. If you consent to the tenant staying, the tenancy continues month to month (week to week for weekly rent). If the tenant appeals an eviction judgment, the writ is stayed only if the tenant pays the rent into escrow or to you within 5 days of the appeal and keeps paying monthly.",
    notes: "NM: NMSA 1978, § 47-8-37(C); NMSA 1978, § 47-8-15(C); NMSA 1978, § 47-8-47(A) (appeal stay conditioned on rent payments; failure leads to a three-day notice and a hearing within ten days). The forcible-entry double-rent measure (NMSA 1978, § 35-10-5) does not apply (NMSA 1978, § 35-10-2). Clause: `holdover-nm`. Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
