    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "After the required notice expires, an owner files a petition for restitution in magistrate or metropolitan court (a metropolitan court has the magistrate courts' jurisdiction) or district court, stating the facts, describing the premises and showing the notice was given. Trial is set 7 to 10 days after the summons is served, and the court may continue it up to 7 days for good cause. If the owner wins, the court issues a writ directing the sheriff to restore possession on a date 3 to 7 days after judgment. An owner or the property manager may appear without a lawyer in magistrate and metropolitan court. The Supreme Court's eviction prevention and diversion program was withdrawn for new cases in January 2023, but some of its forms remain in the rules.",
    notes: "NM: NMSA 1978, §§ 47-8-10(A), 34-8A-3(A), 47-8-41, 47-8-42, 47-8-43(A)-(B), 47-8-44, 47-8-45, 47-8-46(A), 47-8-47; NMSA 1978, § 47-8-30(A)-(C) (in a nonpayment possession action the resident may counterclaim but remains responsible for the rent specified during possession; fees and costs for a defense, counterclaim or reply that is without merit and not in good faith); Rule 2-107(B)(2) NMRA and Rule 3-107(B)(2) NMRA (owner or person authorized to manage the premises may appear in actions under the act); current forms 4-904, 4-905, 4-906, 4-906A, 4-907, 4-909, 4-913, 4-913A and 4-914 NMRA (4-908, 4-909A and 4-910 to 4-912 are withdrawn). The forcible-entry article does not apply (NMSA 1978, § 35-10-2). Eviction Prevention and Diversion Program: Supreme Court Order No. 22-8500-012 (program) and Order No. 23-8500-001 (January 9, 2023, withdrawing it for new cases), read and saved (`nm-sc-orders-epdp.json`, NM log §1); Forms 4-904A and 4-905A (post-judgment application for a writ and its summons, from Order No. 22-8500-012), 4-904B and 4-905B (petition and summons 'for use only with' or 'with the Eviction Prevention and Diversion Program'), 4-908A to 4-908D (facilitation referral, stipulated dismissal, notice of payment, dismissal) and 4-923A NMRA remain published (NM log §19, rule 39). No statutory rental assistance or pre-filing diversion condition (NM battery 102 (rental assistance / eviction prevention / diversion (statute)): 0 hits, control 0; known positives passed; everyday-word rerun NM battery 130 (eviction prevention / rental assistance everyday-word rerun of B102): 11 hits, control 0; known positives passed: hits are apprenticeship, solar-fund and utility sections). Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
