    bodyText:
      "In a suit by an applicant or a party to enforce the lease or the Owner-Resident Relations Act, the winning side is entitled to reasonable attorney fees and court costs. Fees also follow from specific violations, such as failing to return a deposit properly, a willful holdover, refusing or abusing access, a bad-faith counterclaim, or knowingly using prohibited lease terms. A lease may provide for prevailing-party fees, which matches the statute.",
    notes: "NM: NMSA 1978, § 47-8-48(A) ('If suit is brought by an applicant or any party to the rental agreement to enforce the terms and conditions of the rental agreement or to enforce any provisions of the [act], the prevailing party shall be entitled to reasonable attorneys' fees and court costs'); NMSA 1978, §§ 47-8-18(D)(3), 47-8-30(B)-(C), 47-8-37(C), 47-8-38, 47-8-17; NMSA 1978, § 57-12-10(C). The 'prevailing party' wins on the merits or the main issue (Hedicke v. Gunville, 2003-NMCA-032); a fee award must consider the act's policy goals and an objective analysis such as lodestar (Behrens v. Gateway Court, L.L.C., 2013-NMCA-097); a landlord prevailing on a damages cross-claim recovered fees (Stodgell v. Weissman, 2025-NMCA-003) (all as summarized in the compilation's annotation to NMSA 1978, § 47-8-48; opinions not read, NM log §1.4). Stale cross-reference: NMSA 1978, § 47-8-35 refers to fees 'as provided in Subsection C of Section 33', which the compiler notes should now be Subsection F and no longer authorizes fees (NM log §10). Rule 49: no New Mexico ban on a lease's 'costs and expenses' language. Tagged `default-by-tenant`. Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
