    topicKey: "notice-delivery-methods",
    bodyText:
      "A notice of nonpayment of rent is effective only if handed to the tenant, mailed, or posted on an exterior door of the unit. Any other written notice to a tenant may be posted only if it is also mailed first class or handed to the tenant. Every notice must state the posting date, which is its effective date; a posted notice must be taped on all sides or placed in a mail or notice receptacle. If a mailed notice comes back or the tenant's last known address is the vacated unit, serve one more notice at any other address the tenant gave you. E-mail does not replace these methods: New Mexico's electronic transactions law does not apply to default, eviction or right-to-cure notices for a primary residence.",
    notes: "NM: NMSA 1978, § 47-8-13(A)-(F); NMSA 1978, § 14-16-3(B)(2)(b); NMSA 1978, §§ 14-16-5(b)-(c), 14-16-8(b)-(d). A resident receives written notice when it is delivered in hand or 'mailed to him at the place held out by him as the place for receipt of the communication, or in the absence of such designation, to his last known place of residence' (NMSA 1978, § 47-8-13(C)(3)); an owner receives it when mailed or delivered at its place of business or any place held out for receipt (NMSA 1978, § 47-8-13(C)(2)). If the owner fails to give the NMSA 1978, § 47-8-19 disclosure, the resident is relieved of the obligation to give the owner notice (NMSA 1978, § 47-8-19(D)). Rule 45: the electronic transactions act read itself, including its variation section (NM log §19). Clauses: tagged `notices`; optional `electronic-notice-nm`. Read section-open 2026-10-02 in the built-in browser from nmonesource.com (NMSA 1978 annotated chapter PDFs, 2026 compilation; the whole NMSA 1978 and the Constitution loaded, saved and hash-matched, NM log §1); 2025 N.M. Laws ch. 122 read as enrolled (NM log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
