    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "fees-as-rent",
    bodyText:
      "No Kansas statute says whether a late fee, a utility charge or any other fee counts as rent, so the lease decides, and the courts decide what the lease means. That matters because Kansas's three-day pay-or-quit notice runs on unpaid rent (K.S.A. 58-2564(b)): calling fees 'additional rent' is how some leases try to reach them, and an eviction resting on unpaid fees rather than unpaid rent is untested in Kansas. This library's clauses do not label fees as rent, and the Tenant Default clause says plainly that failing to pay an assessed late fee, apart from the rent itself, is not by itself a ground to terminate or evict.",
    notes: "KS: NEW ROW 2026-10-02, SOP 1.26 rule 27 retro. CONFIRMED ABSENT (statutes). 'deemed to be rent', 'considered rent', 'as additional rent' and 'shall be rent' return 0 hits in the corpus. K.S.A. 58-2543(j) defines 'rent' as 'all payments to be made to the landlord under the rental agreement, other than the security deposit' — broad enough on its face to swallow fees, which is the argument on the other side and the reason this is a lease-drafting question rather than a settled one. Not researched: Kansas case law on whether a fee designated 'additional rent' supports a 58-2564(b) nonpayment termination. Read section-open 2026-10-02 from a full-text corpus built in the built-in browser from ksrevisor.gov: every section of K.S.A. chapters 12, 16, 16a, 31, 44, 50, 58, 60 and 61 (5,906 sections, 16,345,503 characters, 0 fetch failures), control term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 hits. Boundary: chapters outside that list were not loaded, and the Kansas Legislature's own statute search indexes section titles and a leading snippet only, so its clean negatives prove nothing about body text (verified: 'periodic rent-paying date' returns 0 results though it is verbatim in K.S.A. 58-2570(b)).",
