    group: "Landlord Responsibilities",
    states: ["KS"],
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "landlord-self-cure",
    bodyText:
      "Kansas gives you no statute letting you fix a tenant's breach — damage, a maintenance failure, an unremoved hazard — and add the cost to the next month's rent as rent. Several URLTA states have that right; Kansas did not adopt it. What you have instead is the notice-and-cure route in K.S.A. 58-2564(a), your claim for actual damages under K.S.A. 58-2568, and the security deposit under K.S.A. 58-2550. The practical difference matters: a cost you bill as rent can support a three-day pay-or-quit notice, while the same cost as damages cannot, so doing the work yourself and charging it does not give you an eviction route for nonpayment.",
    notes: "KS: NEW ROW 2026-10-02, SOP 1.26 rule 27 retro. CONFIRMED ABSENT (statutes). No URLTA sec. 4.105 analog in the KRLTA: 'cause the work to be done', 'charge ... as rent', 'cost ... as rent' and 'enter ... and cause' return no hit anywhere in K.S.A. 58-2540 through 58-2573. NAMING TRAP: the one hit in Article 25's range is K.S.A. 58-2531, in the pre-1975 block, which is a preamble about FARM land leases adding unpaid taxes to money rent — not a residential self-cure right, and deprioritized by product decision. See also edu-habitability-duty-delegation-ks for the one thing Kansas does allow, which is shifting duties forward by agreement rather than curing after the fact. Read section-open 2026-10-02 from a full-text corpus built in the built-in browser from ksrevisor.gov: every section of K.S.A. chapters 12, 16, 16a, 31, 44, 50, 58, 60 and 61 (5,906 sections, 16,345,503 characters, 0 fetch failures), control term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 hits. Boundary: chapters outside that list were not loaded, and the Kansas Legislature's own statute search indexes section titles and a leading snippet only, so its clean negatives prove nothing about body text (verified: 'periodic rent-paying date' returns 0 results though it is verbatim in K.S.A. 58-2570(b)).",
