    bodyText:
      "Kansas has no statute requiring every blank in a residential lease to be filled in before signing, and no statute making a blank a breach or a deceptive practice. Fill them in anyway. Two Kansas rules bite on an incomplete lease from different directions: a court may refuse to enforce an unconscionable lease or term (K.S.A. 58-2544), and where a lease is never signed and delivered but the parties act on it, K.S.A. 58-2546 gives it effect for no longer than one year whatever term the document states. A blank left for later also invites the K.S.A. 58-2570(e) problem, where a document you hand the tenant to sign adds terms the lease does not contain.",
    notes: "KS: NEW ROW 2026-10-02, SOP 1.26 rule 27 retro. CONFIRMED ABSENT (statutes). 'blank space', 'blanks in the lease' and 'all blanks' return one corpus hit, K.S.A. 50-6,147, which is a credit-services organization rule and not a lease rule. The Kansas Consumer Protection Act carries no enumerated unfair-practice for blank spaces or for failing to give a copy at signing (see edu-consumer-protection-act-ks and SOP rule 51): Kansas is absent on the enumerated-list question and live on the general unconscionability standard at K.S.A. 50-627. Read section-open 2026-10-02 from a full-text corpus built in the built-in browser from ksrevisor.gov: every section of K.S.A. chapters 12, 16, 16a, 31, 44, 50, 58, 60 and 61 (5,906 sections, 16,345,503 characters, 0 fetch failures), control term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 hits. Boundary: chapters outside that list were not loaded, and the Kansas Legislature's own statute search indexes section titles and a leading snippet only, so its clean negatives prove nothing about body text (verified: 'periodic rent-paying date' returns 0 results though it is verbatim in K.S.A. 58-2570(b)).",
