    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "tenant-security-cameras",
    bodyText:
      "No Kansas statute says whether a tenant may install a security camera or a video doorbell, so the lease controls, including its alterations terms where mounting hardware is involved. Two Kansas points are worth knowing. A device that records sound is a different question from one that only records pictures, and Kansas's eavesdropping statute reaches recording a private conversation without the consent of a party to it, so a doorbell capturing audio in a shared hallway or a neighbour's doorway can raise an issue a video-only device does not. And nothing here limits your own entry rules: you still owe reasonable notice at reasonable hours under K.S.A. 58-2557, and a tenant's camera does not change that.",
    notes: "KS: NEW ROW 2026-10-02, SOP 1.26 rule 27 retro. CONFIRMED ABSENT (statutes). 'security camera', 'video doorbell', 'surveillance camera' and 'recording device' return two corpus hits, K.S.A. 31-172 and 60-4118, neither about tenants or dwellings. FLAGGED, NOT READ: the Kansas eavesdropping statute sits in chapter 21 (crimes), which was NOT loaded into the corpus, so the audio sentence is stated from general knowledge of K.S.A. 21-6101 and has not been read section-open this pass — confirm before this row goes in front of a customer. Read section-open 2026-10-02 from a full-text corpus built in the built-in browser from ksrevisor.gov: every section of K.S.A. chapters 12, 16, 16a, 31, 44, 50, 58, 60 and 61 (5,906 sections, 16,345,503 characters, 0 fetch failures), control term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 hits. Boundary: chapters outside that list were not loaded, and the Kansas Legislature's own statute search indexes section titles and a leading snippet only, so its clean negatives prove nothing about body text (verified: 'periodic rent-paying date' returns 0 results though it is verbatim in K.S.A. 58-2570(b)).",
