    bodyText:
      "Montana sets no cap on application fees. Since October 1, 2025, a property manager of four or more dwelling units who requires an application fee must refund it within a reasonable time if the applicant does not become a party to the rental agreement, keeping only the out-of-pocket costs of services actually performed that were itemized in writing when the fee was collected (such as a credit check). The manager's own time can't be kept as a cost. Wrongful withholding makes the manager liable for the amount withheld, and attorney fees may be awarded. 'Property manager' is defined broadly, so owners who manage four or more units themselves should follow this rule too.",
    notes: "MT: Mont. Code Ann. § 37-56-109, enacted by 2025 Mont. Laws ch. 254, § 1 (HB 311; effective October 1, 2025, the default date in Mont. Code Ann. § 1-2-201, MT log §1.2): (1) 'A property manager of four or more dwelling units that requires an application fee prior to the rental of a dwelling unit shall refund the application fee within a reasonable period of time if the applicant does not become a party to a signed rental agreement for the dwelling unit or if the applicant does not become a party to a rental agreement that has the same effect as if it had been signed pursuant to 70-24-204'; (2) written notice of the portion allocated to each cost 'at the time the application fee is collected', retaining 'only the costs specified in the written notice for services actually performed'; (3) liability for the sum wrongfully withheld, discretionary attorney fees, burden on the manager; (4)(b) '\"Cost\" means the out-of-pocket expense ... The term does not include a fee for the property manager's time or effort for arranging or performing the service.' 'Property manager' means 'an individual who engages in the business of leasing, renting, subleasing, or other transfer of possession of real estate located in this state without transfer of the title to the property' (Mont. Code Ann. § 37-56-101(2)); the exemptions in Mont. Code Ann. § 37-56-104(1), including a person who leases no more than four residential units, are from 'The property manager licensing provisions of this part', and whether they reach Mont. Code Ann. § 37-56-109 is not settled by its text, so the row tells self-managing owners of four or more units to follow it (independent check round 1). 2025 Mont. Laws ch. 254, § 3: '[This act] applies to an application fee collected by a property manager from an applicant on or after [the effective date of this act].' No cap (MT battery 12 (application fee cap): 0 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); MT battery 178 (application fee everyday rerun (fee to apply / screen / credit check for rental)): 6 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-02 to 2026-10-03 from the whole Montana Code Annotated 2025 and the Montana Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from mca.legmt.gov, saved and hash-matched (MT log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
