    topicKey: "holdover-rate",
    bodyText:
      "Montana lets you recover up to the greater of three months' periodic rent or treble damages when a tenant purposefully holds over after you end a month-to-month tenancy without cause by a lawful 30-day notice, or after you end a longer tenancy for cause and the holdover is purposeful and not in good faith. Outside those cases you recover actual damages, including the rental value for each day of holdover. A lease can lawfully set a holdover rate, but the library doesn't offer a premium daily rate: Montana voids damages fixed in advance unless actual damage would be impracticable or extremely difficult to fix, and holdover loss usually isn't. A landlord who wants a premium rate can add their own clause and should take advice first.",
    notes: "MT: Mont. Code Ann. § 70-24-429(2)-(3) (quoted in `holdover-mt`); Mont. Code Ann. § 70-24-422(5) (actual damages for noncompliance); Mont. Code Ann. § 28-2-721 (liquidated damages void unless 'impracticable or extremely difficult to fix the actual damage'). Rule 54: the statute's enhanced measure covers only two cases, leaving the expiry of a fixed term without a termination 'for cause' to actual damages; a stipulated rate for that gap is lawful only if it approximates actual loss, which `holdover-mt`'s daily-rent sentence does; a premium is declined (MT log §6.1). The ch. 27 treble-rent holdover statutes (Mont. Code Ann. §§ 70-27-207, 70-27-208) do not apply (Mont. Code Ann. § 70-27-101(1)). No Montana case on stipulated holdover rates was searched (MT log §1.4). Read section-open 2026-10-02 to 2026-10-03 from the whole Montana Code Annotated 2025 and the Montana Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from mca.legmt.gov, saved and hash-matched (MT log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
