      "A tenant with a disability may ask to keep an emotional support animal as a reasonable accommodation. Unless federal law says otherwise, you may deny an animal that poses a direct threat to others' health or safety, or of physical damage to the property, that another accommodation can't reduce or eliminate. If the need isn't readily apparent, you may ask for supporting information from a Montana-licensed mental health practitioner who has known the tenant at least 30 days and has done a clinical evaluation (including the practitioner's license type, number and effective date), or from another source you find reliable under fair housing law, and you may require proof of licensing and vaccinations. You may not ask for a diagnosis, its severity or medical records, and an online registration or certificate alone isn't enough. You must issue a written determination by a notice method the Act allows. The tenant is liable for damage the animal does. A person with a service animal is entitled to full and equal access to housing and can't be charged extra, but is liable for damage.",
