      "Texas statutes do not regulate using algorithmic or software pricing tools to set residential rents, or sharing rent data with other landlords. Texas's general antitrust law, which makes any contract, combination or conspiracy in restraint of trade unlawful, may still apply, as may federal antitrust law.",
    notes: "TX: NEW 2026-10-03 (SOP 1.29 retro, rule 27 topic `algorithmic-rent-setting`). CONFIRMED ABSENT (statute). Battery over the whole Property, Business & Commerce, Civil Practice & Remedies and Finance Codes: 'algorithm' PR 0, BC/CP/FI 9 (positives: Bus. & Com. Code §§4A.201, 120.051, 322.002, 509.053, 509.056 - none about rent or pricing); 'pricing (software|tool|service)|rent[- ]setting|revenue management|coordinated pricing' 0; heading-only screen: 3 hits, all ch. 509 (digital services to minors). Antitrust: Tex. Bus. & Com. Code §15.05(a). Boundary: Government and Penal Codes, federal law and case law not read. Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the official Texas statutes (chapter HTML served by tcss.legis.texas.gov behind statutes.capitol.texas.gov; site current through the 89th Legislature, 2nd Called Session, 2025); the whole Property, Business & Commerce, Civil Practice & Remedies and Finance Codes and the Constitution were loaded, proved complete against the site's chapter lists, saved and hash-matched (TX log, 'Retro checks (SOP 1.29)').",
