      "Texas's landlord-tenant statutes have no separate quiet-enjoyment covenant; any covenant comes from the common law and the lease. The statutes protect the tenant's possession directly instead: you may change the locks only in the narrow cases the statute allows, may interrupt utilities only as it allows, and may not, in retaliation for a protected act, engage in bad faith in a course of conduct that materially interferes with the tenant's rights under the lease. Each carries money penalties.",
