      "Florida prescribes many forms a residential landlord uses, most of them 'in substantially the following form'. In or with the lease: the security deposit disclosure (landlords with five or more units), the fee-in-lieu-of-deposit disclosure, the e-mail notice addendum, the flood disclosure (separate document, leases of a year or more), the early termination fee addendum (separate, signed at signing), the abandoned-property legend, and the radon notice (exact words). During and after the tenancy: the deposit claim notice, the 3-day notice for rent, the 7-day cure and 7-day termination notices, the abandoned-property notices, and the bounced-check demand. Others concern associations and squatters: a condominium or homeowners' association's demand that the tenant pay rent to it, and an owner's sworn complaint to have the sheriff remove unauthorized occupants. Court forms for eviction (summons, complaint and answer) are set by the Florida Supreme Court.",
