    topicKey: "for-cause-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "California has one of the strongest just-cause rules in the country, and it is the single thing most likely to catch out a landlord who has rented elsewhere. Once a tenant has continuously and lawfully occupied the home for 12 months, you may not end the tenancy without a just cause, and the cause must be stated in the written notice. If you added another adult to the lease before the original tenant reached 24 months, the rule applies once all tenants have reached 12 months or any one tenant has reached 24. The end of a fixed term is not itself a reason to recover possession: a covered tenancy continues, and a tenant's refusal to sign a renewal on similar terms is itself one of the listed at-fault causes. At-fault causes include nonpayment, breach of a material lease term, nuisance, waste, criminal activity, unlawful subletting and refusing lawful entry. No-fault causes include an owner or close relative moving in, withdrawing the home from the rental market, complying with a government order, and demolition or substantial remodel; a no-fault termination requires relocation assistance or a waiver of the final month's rent, and getting that wrong voids the notice. Several kinds of property are exempt, and a local just-cause ordinance can displace the state scheme entirely.",
    notes: "CA: NEW 2026-10-03, SOP 1.31 rule 41b retro. **California was the only one of 31 states with no row carrying the `for-cause-eviction` topic_key**, even though it has the project's strongest just-cause rule - the verdict was spread across tpa-notice-ca, edu-tpa-sunset-ca, month-to-month-notice-ca, edu-termination-notice-periods-ca and early-termination-ca and stated nowhere as the topic's answer. Written per rule 41b as a short row recording the verdict and pointing at the rows that carry the detail, rather than moving content. PRESENT: Civ. Code s1946.2, subdivisions (a) and (b)(1)(A)-(K) read section-open on leginfo 2026-10-03; s1946.2(a)'s 12-month and 24-month thresholds and the (b)(1)(E) refusal-to-renew cause quoted from that read. (b)(2) no-fault causes, (d) relocation assistance or final-month rent waiver, (g) failure to comply voids the notice, (e) exemptions and (i) local-ordinance displacement are as recorded in tpa-notice-ca and edu-tpa-sunset-ca from the original pass. RULE 41 SCREEN, run the same day on the four clauses the prompt named plus the surrender clause: `early-termination-ca` PASSES - it says Landlord may terminate early 'only as permitted by law, including, where this property is subject to Civil Code section 1946.2, only for a just cause stated in the notice of termination'. `holdover-ca` PASSES - it claims no possessory right at the end of the Term, only damages and an alternative month-to-month continuation 'terminable only as provided by law'. `keys` and `no-alterations` PASS - neither speaks to possession. `surrender-end-of-term` (shared, 12 states) is the one with a real tension and it is recorded in that row's CA segment: it tells the tenant to surrender possession 'upon the expiration' of the Lease, which for a s1946.2-covered tenancy overstates the position, since expiry of the term does not entitle the owner to possession. Not edited: shared text, rule 62 - proposed for review instead. SUNSET: s1946.2 is repealed 2030-01-01 (edu-tpa-sunset-ca).",
