    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "algorithmic-rent-setting",
    bodyText:
      "California is one of the few states with a statute aimed at algorithmic pricing, and it took effect on January 1, 2026. It is antitrust law rather than landlord-tenant law, and it does not ban revenue-management software. Two things are unlawful: using or distributing a common pricing algorithm as part of an agreement or conspiracy to restrain trade, and using or distributing one to coerce someone else into adopting a price or commercial term the algorithm recommends for the same or similar product or service. A common pricing algorithm is any method, including software, used by two or more people that uses competitor data to recommend, align, stabilise, set or otherwise influence a price or term, and 'price' is defined broadly. The practical reading for a landlord: using pricing software on your own data is not what this targets; feeding or drawing on competitors' non-public rent data through a shared tool, or pressing another owner to follow its numbers, is where the risk sits. Federal and California antitrust and unfair-competition law apply as well.",
    notes: "CA: PRESENT, SOP 1.31 rule 27 retro 2026-10-03, and a different answer from the 24 states that recorded this topic absent. Bus. & Prof. Code s16729, read section-open on leginfo 2026-10-03: ADDED BY STATS. 2025, CH. 338 (AB 325), EFFECTIVE 2026-01-01, inside the Cartwright Act. (a) conspiracy limb; (b) COERCION limb; (c) does not limit other antitrust law; (d)(3) 'common pricing algorithm' defined as a methodology used by TWO OR MORE PERSONS that uses COMPETITOR DATA; (d)(6) 'price' broadly defined; (d)(5) 'person' excludes the end consumer. The statute names neither rent nor housing, so the row says so rather than implying a housing-specific ban - it reaches rental housing as a service like any other. Official leginfo Code Search (All codes), run 2026-10-03; rule 11 control tests passed the same day - nonsense term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 sections, and the phrase 'shall not constitute rent', read verbatim in the body of Civ. Code s1954.213, returns that one section, so the index covers section body text and honours phrases. METHOD LIMIT, stated: the results page returns ten sections at a time and its paging postback could not be driven reliably, so an absence is claimed only from terms returning 0 or at most 10 hits, every one reviewed; for a higher-count term the count is given and no absence rests on it. Boundary: statutes only - the California Code of Regulations, local ordinances and case law were not searched unless stated. The full term battery with counts is tabled in the CA retro decision log under 'Retro checks (SOP 1.31)', rule 27. There is no battery JSON of the Pennsylvania kind because the corpus here is the official leginfo search index, queried live, not a local statute dump that can be re-run offline. Terms: 'algorithmic' 26 sections (count given, no absence claimed on it); 'pricing algorithm' 1 (BPC 16729); 'common pricing algorithm' 1 (BPC 16729); 'algorithmic device' 0; 'rent-setting' 8, all reviewed and all affordable-housing program sections (HSC 53559.4, 50517.11, 53567, 53601, 50675.16, 50199.24, 53545.11; MVC 987.011), none a private-market rule. NOT READ: the Cartwright Act's remedies sections (Bus. & Prof. Code s16750 and following), so this row states no penalty figure; no case law.",
