    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "tenant-security-cameras",
    bodyText:
      "No California statute says whether a tenant may install a security camera or a video doorbell, so the lease controls, through its rules on alterations and on attaching things to the building. Two things make this more sensitive in California than almost anywhere else. Privacy is an inalienable right under the state constitution, and California courts apply it to private conduct, not only to the government - so a camera covering a shared hallway, a neighbour's door or a yard is a live issue even between neighbours. And California requires the consent of everyone involved before a confidential conversation is recorded, so a device that captures audio outside the tenant's own space raises a criminal-law question, not just a lease one. A reasonable approach is to allow a device that views only the tenant's own entry and requires no drilling, and to ask for written consent otherwise.",
    notes: "CA: CONFIRMED ABSENT, SOP 1.31 rule 27 retro 2026-10-03, re-run and matching the 2026-09-28 result. Official leginfo Code Search (All codes), run 2026-10-03; rule 11 control tests passed the same day - nonsense term 'zqxvbnmwt' 0 sections, and the phrase 'shall not constitute rent', read verbatim in the body of Civ. Code s1954.213, returns that one section, so the index covers section body text and honours phrases. METHOD LIMIT, stated: the results page returns ten sections at a time and its paging postback could not be driven reliably, so an absence is claimed only from terms returning 0 or at most 10 hits, every one reviewed; for a higher-count term the count is given and no absence rests on it. Boundary: statutes only - the California Code of Regulations, local ordinances and case law were not searched unless stated. The full term battery with counts is tabled in the CA retro decision log under 'Retro checks (SOP 1.31)', rule 27. There is no battery JSON of the Pennsylvania kind because the corpus here is the official leginfo search index, queried live, not a local statute dump that can be re-run offline. Terms: 'security camera' 3 sections, all reviewed and none landlord-tenant (Educ. Code s49005.8; Rev. & Tax. Code ss17053.64, 23664); 'video doorbell' 0; 'surveillance' 154 sections, count given and NO absence claimed from it. Constitution screened by DIRECT READ, not by search: the Code Search's CONS option indexes nothing (control terms 'Legislature', 'Governor' and even 'the' each return 0 sections), so a negative from it would prove nothing (rule 35c). California Constitution article I (Declaration of Rights) was loaded and read from leginfo 2026-10-03, 41,093 characters. Article I section 1 quoted from that read: inalienable rights include 'pursuing and obtaining safety, happiness, and privacy' (added Nov. 5, 1974, by Proposition 7). LABELLED UNREAD, per rule 76: that California's constitutional privacy right reaches PRIVATE conduct is doctrine from case law this project has not read, and the row's wording attributes it to the courts rather than to the text. The audio limb is Pen. Code s632, NOT READ at this retro - the row describes it in general terms only and names no penalty. No CA-tagged clause addresses tenant cameras; `no-alterations` and `common-area-use-ca` are the clauses that bear on installation. effective_from is the screen date, not a statute date, following edu-no-radon-disclosure-ca - the CA convention where the topic has no statute at all.",
