    topicKey: "cure-and-eviction-grounds",
    bodyText:
      "New York gives tenants several chances to cure. For unpaid rent: the 14-day rent demand, payment before the hearing ends the case, and the court must vacate a warrant if all rent due is paid before it is executed (unless the rent was withheld in bad faith). For a lease violation: where the Good Cause Eviction Law applies you must give written notice to cure within 10 days before the breach is good cause, and in a holdover case based on a lease breach the court must give the tenant 30 days to cure before the warrant issues (except where the case is brought because the tenant is objectionable and you prove it, and except for hotel, lodging-house or rooming-house rooms). No statute sets a cure notice for lease violations outside those rules; the lease's own cure terms apply.",
    notes: "NY: N.Y. Real Prop. Acts. Law §§ 711(2), 731(4), 749(3) (quoted in `edu-rent-demand-ny` and `edu-eviction-process-ny`); N.Y. Real Prop. Law § 216(1)(b) ('failed to cure such violation after written notice that the violation cease within ten days of receipt of such written notice'); N.Y. Real Prop. Acts. Law § 753(4) (30-day cure stay; quoted in `edu-eviction-stay-ny`). Rule 43: `default-by-tenant-co` ties the rent cure to the period 'specified by applicable law' after written notice, which in New York is the statutory 14-day demand. For non-rent breaches it promises a cure after written notice; New York requires no pre-suit notice for non-rent breaches outside article 6-A, so there the promise is the lease's own, subject to the clause's no-cure carve-out (Claude's reading; tag note). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the Consolidated Laws of New York and the New York Constitution as published on nysenate.gov (The Laws of New York), crawled whole into the built-in browser, with every relied-on section saved and hash-matched (NY log §1); session laws read as enrolled on nysenate.gov (NY log §1.2). Rule 15: written section-open.",
