    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "late-fee",
    bodyText:
      "Wisconsin sets no cap or grace period for late fees, but you may charge one only if the lease specifically provides for it. Before charging a late fee you must apply any rent the tenant prepaid to the rent owed, and you may never charge a fee for not paying a late fee. Keep the fee in line with your real cost of late payment. To be safe, list unpaid late fees in a NONSTANDARD RENTAL PROVISIONS document if you want to deduct them from the security deposit.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 134.09(8)(a): 'No landlord may charge a late rent fee or late rent penalty to a tenant, except as specifically provided under the rental agreement.' ATCP § 134.09(8)(b): 'Before charging a late rent fee or late rent penalty to a tenant, a landlord shall apply all rent prepayments received from that tenant to offset the amount of rent owed by the tenant.' ATCP § 134.09(8)(c): 'No landlord may charge any tenant a fee or penalty for nonpayment of a late rent fee or late rent penalty.' No cap or grace period: CONFIRMED ABSENT (whole-code battery; boundary: the Wisconsin Statutes, the Wisconsin Administrative Code and the Wisconsin Constitution searched; case law and local ordinances not searched). (WI battery 1 (late fee (any)): 78 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic)) (WI battery 2 (late fee cap / limit (amount or percent)): 21 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic)) (WI battery 3 (late fee cap everyday rerun (reasonable late fee; dwelling)): 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic)). The only other late-fee standards found are a presumption of reasonableness for self-service storage space not rented for residential use (Wis. Stat. § 704.90(1)(b), (4b)(b)) and the Consumer Act's delinquency-charge cap for consumer credit transactions (Wis. Stat. § 422.203), which on the reasoning of Koble Investments v. Marquardt, 2026 WI 19, ¶¶ 15-16 does not reach a lease with monthly rent (Claude's reading; `late-fee`) (WI battery 206 (delinquency / late charge caps in consumer law (wider rerun for late fee)): 38 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic)). Late fees count as 'rent' only 'In this section' (Wis. Stat. § 704.17(1g)); whether they are 'Unpaid rent' that may be withheld from a deposit under Wis. Stat. § 704.28(1)(b) is not settled by the text, so the row recommends listing them as a nonstandard rental provision (`nrp-deposit-withholding-wi`; Claude's reading). Penalty doctrine for residential late fees not searched (WI log §1.4). `late-fee` tagged. Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
