      "For a Wisconsin pay-or-vacate notice, rent expressly includes past-due rent and late fees owed on that past-due rent; the statute does not say other charges count, so treat unpaid non-rent charges as a breach under the notice-to-cure rules rather than adding them to a pay-rent notice. State the amount due carefully: a notice with a wrong amount is still valid unless the error was intentional or the tenant paid or offered what the tenant believed was due. Accepting past-due rent after the notice or after filing does not by itself end an eviction case.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 704.17(1g): 'In this section, “rent” includes any rent that is past due and any late fees owed for rent that is past due.' Wis. Stat. § 704.17(4m): a notice 'that includes an incorrect statement of the amount due is valid unless' the statement is 'intentionally incorrect' or 'The tenant paid or tendered payment of the amount the tenant believes to be due.' Wis. Stat. § 799.40(1m): the action 'may not be dismissed because the landlord accepts past due rent or any other payment from the tenant after serving notice of default or after commencing the action.' Other fees: no Wisconsin definition makes them rent (WI battery 15 (fees as rent / definition of rent): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
