    topicKey: "rent-increase-notice",
    bodyText:
      "Wisconsin has no statute setting a notice period for a rent increase. During a fixed-term lease you can raise rent only as the lease itself provides. For a month-to-month tenancy, the safe course is to give written notice of the new rent at least 28 days before the end of a rental period, the same notice that ends a periodic tenancy. You may not raise rent in retaliation for a tenant's good-faith complaint or for exercising a legal right, and a lease that lets you raise rent because a tenant contacted police, health or safety services is void. (Manufactured home community operators follow separate DATCP rules: no increases during the term (with limited exceptions) and 28 days' written notice of changes at renewal.)",
    notes: "WI: No rent-increase or term-change notice statute (WI battery 8 (rent increase notice): 12 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic)); the hits are the retaliation sections (Wis. Stat. §§ 704.44(1m)(a), 704.45(1); Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 134.08(1)(a)); the manufactured home community rules (rent 'may not be increased during the term of the rental agreement', Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 125.03(4); new terms 'at least 28 days prior to the date on which the proposed new agreement is to take effect', ATCP § 125.05(1)); Wis. Stat. § 823.23(4)(b) (a landlord may not raise rent or take other listed action against the tenant of a unit subject to a pending or anticipated nuisance action if the tenant proves the action 'would not have occurred but for' the nuisance action and that no nuisance was found, the tenant did not cause it, or the conduct is not necessary to abate it); and relocation, tax and ATCP § 134.09(9) rules. Retaliation basis: Wis. Stat. § 704.45(1)(c) ('Exercising a legal right relating to residential tenancies'); § 704.44(1m) voids a lease that allows the listed acts but does not itself bar them. A periodic tenancy ends only on written notice 'at the end of a rental period' and at least 28 days' notice (Wis. Stat. § 704.19(2)(b)1., (3)); using that notice for an increase is Claude's recommendation (no case searched, WI log §1.4). Retaliation: `edu-retaliation-wi`. Rent control preempted: `edu-rent-control-wi`. Also answers `term-change-notice` and `rent-escalation` (WI log §18). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
