    topicKey: "municipal-utility-lien",
    bodyText:
      "A tenant's unpaid bill to a city, village or town utility (water and sewer, and electric where the statute allows) can become a lien on your property: each October the utility gives notice to the owner or occupant, a 10% penalty is added if not paid by November 1, and unpaid amounts after November 15 go on the tax roll as a special charge. For water or electric service, you can shift collection toward the tenant by notifying the utility in writing of your name and address and the responsible tenant's name and address (and giving it the lease if asked); when the tenant moves out, give the utility the tenant's forwarding address within 21 days. Even then, the utility can still put the arrears on your tax bill if it notifies you within 14 days after the tenant's charges become past due; you may, at least 14 days after that notice, ask it to shut off electric service. You may also ask the utility whether a prospective tenant owes it past-due charges at another address.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 66.0809(3)(a)-(c) (October 15 notice 'to the owner or occupant'; 'unless the amount is paid by November 1 a penalty of 10 percent of the amount of arrears will be added'; lien and special charge after November 15). § 66.0809(5)(a)2.-4. (owner's written notices and lease copy if requested), (am)2. (forwarding address 'no later than 21 days after the date on which the tenant vacates'), (b) (utility must serve the owner within 14 days after the tenant's charges become past due to keep using the lien), (bm) (owner may then ask the utility to end electric service), (3m) (lien on the tenant's assets). § 66.0809(8): 'A municipal public utility shall disclose to the owner of a rental dwelling unit, upon the owner’s request, whether a new or prospective tenant has outstanding past-due charges for utility service to that municipal public utility in that tenant’s name at a different address.' Electric arrears may be collected by lien only as § 66.0809(4) allows. Deposit: Wis. Stat. § 704.28(1)(d). (WI battery 41 (municipal utility lien / tenant utility arrears): 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)) Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
