    topicKey: "tenant-repair-remedies",
    bodyText:
      "If the unit becomes untenantable from fire, water or other casualty or a health hazard, or you substantially violate your repair duties in a way that materially affects health or safety, the tenant may move out unless you promptly repair (or even if you do, when the repair would impose undue hardship), owes no rent after the unit became untenantable, and gets back rent paid in advance for that period. A tenant who stays has rent reduced to the extent they lose normal use, but may not withhold all rent. Wisconsin has no repair-and-deduct statute, and these remedies do not apply if the tenant caused the condition.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 704.07(4): the tenant 'may remove from the premises unless the landlord proceeds promptly to repair or rebuild'; 'rent abates to the extent the tenant is deprived of the full normal use of the premises. This section does not authorize rent to be withheld in full, if the tenant remains in possession'; 'This subsection is inapplicable if the damage or condition is caused by negligence or improper use by the tenant.' Undue hardship and repayment of advance rent: same subsection. Local abatement ordinances limited: § 704.07(5). No repair-and-deduct or rent-escrow statute (WI battery 51 (repair and deduct / rent withholding / escrow): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic)). Receivership for public nuisance buildings: Wis. Stat. § 823.23 (not a tenant remedy). Also answers `landlord-breach-remedy` (WI log §18). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
