    topicKey: "nonpayment-notice",
    bodyText:
      "For a month-to-month or week-to-week tenant, a year-to-year tenant, or a lease of one year or less, you may give a 5-day notice to pay or vacate. A month-to-month tenant in default may instead get a 14-day notice to vacate with no chance to pay. Under a lease of one year or less, or a year-to-year tenancy, if the tenant was given a 5-day notice and then pays late again within one year, a 14-day notice to vacate ends the tenancy. A lease of more than one year needs a 30-day notice to pay. The notice must be in writing and served by a method the statute allows; a wrong amount does not invalidate it unless it was intentionally wrong or the tenant paid or offered what they believed was due.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 704.17(1p)(a) (5 days to pay; month-to-month 14 days to vacate 'while the tenant is in default in payment of rent'), (2)(a) (lease of one year or less: 5 days; repeat 'within one year of any prior default in payment of rent for which notice was given', 14 days), (3)(a) (lease over one year: 30 days), (4) (writing; Wis. Stat. § 704.21). 'rent' includes late fees on past-due rent (§ 704.17(1g)). Contrary lease terms invalid except in leases for more than one year (§ 704.17(5)(a)). Also answers `redemption` and `forfeiture-redemption`: the tenant's right to cure is paying within the notice period; no statute lets a tenant pay and stay after the tenancy is terminated (WI battery 216 (tenant reinstatement / redemption after termination): 29 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic)) (WI log §18). Incorrect amount: § 704.17(4m). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
