    topicKey: "cure-and-eviction-grounds",
    bodyText:
      "For a breach other than rent, a tenant under a lease of one year or less, or a year-to-year tenant, gets a 5-day notice to remedy the default or vacate; a tenant is complying if they promptly take reasonable steps to fix it or offer to pay adequate damages. A second breach within a year allows a 14-day notice to vacate with no cure. A month-to-month tenant can be given either the 5-day notice to remedy or a 14-day notice to vacate. Leases over one year need a 30-day notice to comply. Criminal activity and law-enforcement nuisance notices have their own 5-day no-cure rules.",
