    topicKey: "notice-delivery-methods",
    bodyText:
      "A landlord's notice under Wisconsin's landlord-tenant chapter must be served by one of these methods: personal delivery, or leaving it at the tenant's home with a competent family member at least 14 who is told what it says; leaving it with a competent person in charge of the premises and mailing a copy; posting it conspicuously and mailing a copy, if the first two cannot be done with reasonable diligence; registered or certified mail; or service like a summons. Certified mail is the simplest to prove: the post office's proof of certified mailing is enough in an eviction case. Notice to one cotenant counts as notice to all, and a notice actually received counts even if not served properly, if you can prove receipt by clear and convincing evidence.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 704.21(1)(a)-(e), (4), (5). Wis. Stat. § 799.40(1g): for notices under §§ 704.16, 704.17 or 704.19 sent by certified mail, 'proof of certified mailing from the United States post office shall be sufficient'. Electronic notice does not substitute for termination notices (Wis. Stat. § 137.12(2r)(b); `electronic-delivery-wi`). Also answers `eviction-service-party` (WI log §18). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
