    topicKey: "for-cause-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "Wisconsin does not require a reason to end a periodic tenancy with proper notice or to decline to renew a lease (manufactured home community operators are different: they may end or refuse to renew a resident's tenancy only on listed grounds, including other good cause). But you may not end or refuse to renew a tenancy in retaliation or for a discriminatory reason, a lease that lets you do so because the tenant called police, health or safety services is void in its entirety, and a tenant has a defense if you knew or should have known the tenant is a victim and the eviction is based on conduct related to domestic abuse, sexual assault or stalking by a non-guest or a guest they have excluded. Cities, villages, towns and counties may not impose eviction moratoriums.",
    notes: "WI: No just-cause statute for ordinary rentals (WI battery 85 (just / good cause eviction): 9 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic)); the battery's relevant hits are Wis. Stat. § 254.595(8) (a tenant's health-hazard action 'is not just cause for eviction') and the manufactured home community rule, Wis. Stat. § 710.15(5m) (termination or nonrenewal only on listed grounds, '(k) Other good cause'; notice, § 710.15(5r)); its other seven hits use 'good cause' or 'just cause' in unrelated settings (child placement, public assistance, sheriff's office discipline, motor vehicle dealers, juvenile placement, driver licensing). Limits: Wis. Stat. §§ 704.45, 704.44(1m), 106.50(2), (5m)(dm); Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 134.09(5). Moratoriums: Wis. Stat. § 66.1010(2) (any political subdivision) (WI battery 102 (eviction moratorium (local)): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). Also answers `good-cause-notice` and `owner-move-in-reservation` (WI log §18). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
