    title: "The Wisconsin Eviction Process",
    group: "Default & Termination",
    states: ["WI"],
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "After the notice period ends, you file a small claims eviction. The summons return date is 5 to 25 days after issuance (at least 20 days for a nonresident), and if the tenant appears and contests, a trial must be held within 30 days after the return date. If you win, the court orders judgment and a writ of restitution, which only the sheriff executes; a writ received by the sheriff more than 30 days after issuance cannot be executed. Either side may demand a jury by the time issue is joined, and appeals must be started within 15 days. Accepting rent after filing does not by itself require dismissal. The court must pause the case for up to 10 working days if the tenant applies for emergency assistance before a writ is issued.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 799.05(3)(b) (eviction return date 'not less than 5 days nor more than 25 days from the issue date'); § 799.206(3) (hearing 'not more than 30 days after the return date'); § 799.44(1)-(2) ('No writ shall be executed if received by the sheriff more than 30 days after its issuance'); § 799.45 (sheriff); § 799.21(3)(a) (jury demand 'at or before the time of joinder of issue'); § 799.445 (appeal 'within 15 days'); § 799.40(1m); emergency-assistance stay, § 799.40(4)(a); the tenant may counterclaim 'any claim related to the rented property' (§ 799.43). Small claims jurisdiction for money claims rises to $15,000 on January 1, 2027 (2025 Wis. Act 105; WI log §1.2) (WI battery 82 (eviction process (any)): 16 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). No statute requires a bond or rent deposit as a condition of defending an eviction (WI battery 105 (rent into court / counterclaim deposit): 8 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic)) (the battery searched deposit and payment wording; the bond and undertaking provisions in §§ 799.44(3) and 799.445 were read directly); an appeal does not stay restitution unless the undertaking in § 799.445 is given, and a hardship stay of the writ is conditioned on paying rent due and may also be conditioned on a bond (§ 799.44(3)). Nonresident return date: § 799.05(3)(c). Also answers `rent-into-court-counterclaim` and `possession-bond` (WI log §18). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
