      "DATCP's residential rental rules are orders under Wisconsin's trade-practices law. A tenant who suffers a money loss because you violated them, including the deposit, disclosure and prohibited-term rules, can recover twice the loss plus costs and reasonable attorney fees, and the State can seek forfeitures. The tenant must show the violation actually caused a money loss. In 2026 the Wisconsin Supreme Court held that the Consumer Act's debt-collection prohibitions do not govern a residential lease with rent payable monthly; for other payment arrangements, get advice before collecting arrears.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 100.20(5) (twice the pecuniary loss, costs and reasonable attorney fee); § 100.26(6) (forfeitures of $100 to $10,000 for each violation of an order issued under § 100.20); Wis. Stat. § 704.95 (violations of §§ 704.28 and 704.44 may be unfair trade practices). Koble Investments v. Marquardt, 2026 WI 19: 'any tenant suffering a pecuniary loss because of a violation of Ch. ATCP 134 may' recover twice the loss plus costs and a reasonable attorney fee (¶ 26), but the tenant must show the violation caused a pecuniary loss (¶¶ 4, 25); Wis. Stat. § 427.104 'does not govern a residential lease under which rent is payable on a monthly basis' (¶ 4), because such a lease is not an agreement to defer payment (¶ 16), and 'Chapter 704 of the Wisconsin Statutes governs residential leases' (¶ 22). The site's annotations to Wis. Stat. §§ 421.301 and 427.104 still report the reversed court of appeals decision (2024 WI App 26; WI log §10). The Act's credit-sale and 'Consumer lease' definitions do not cover ordinary real-property leases (Wis. Stat. § 421.301(9), (11)) (WI battery 149 (consumer act: residential leases / real property): 0 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic)) (WI battery 176 (consumer act scope over leases (everyday words, rerun of 149)): 31 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic)). Also answers `knowing-use-penalty` and `unconscionability` (Wis. Stat. § 402.302 covers sales of goods) (WI battery 167 (unconscionability (rerun of 148 with a known positive)): 26 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)) (battery 148 had no known positive; rerun as 167). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
