      "You may end a tenancy on a 5-day written notice, without a chance to cure, if the tenant, a household member or a guest engages in criminal activity threatening other tenants, neighbors or you and your staff, or in drug manufacturing or distribution on or near the premises. The notice must state its basis, describe the activity, its date and the people involved, tell the tenant they may seek help from a lawyer, a volunteer legal clinic or a tenant resource center, and state their right to contest the allegations in court. No arrest or conviction is needed, but the rule does not apply to a tenant who is the victim. A property owner may also give a 5-day notice to vacate after police or the district attorney give written notice that a drug or criminal-gang nuisance exists in the unit or was caused by the tenant on the owner's property; that notice must state its basis and the tenant's right to contest the termination in an eviction action. A lease that allows termination for a crime committed in relation to the rental property is void in its entirety unless it includes the Notice of Domestic Abuse Protections.",
    notes: "WI: Wis. Stat. § 704.17(3m)(b)1.-2., (c); nuisance notices: § 704.17(1p)(c), (2)(c), (3)(b), referring to Wis. Stat. § 823.113(1), (1m)(b). Contrary lease terms invalid: § 704.17(5)(b) for (3m) terminations; for the nuisance terminations, § 704.17(5)(a) (except in leases for more than one year) (WI battery 99 (criminal activity termination): 3 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)) (WI battery 98 (nuisance (drug / criminal) and tenancy): 49 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic)). Void without the notice: Wis. Stat. § 704.44(10); on an unlawful-use clause, the court of appeals held void under Wis. Stat. § 704.44(10) a lease that lacked the Wis. Stat. § 704.14 notice and barred knowingly permitting use of the premises for an unlawful purpose, together with a general termination-for-breach term (Koble Investments v. Marquardt, 2024 WI App 26, as the site's annotation and 2026 WI 19, ¶ 23 report), and the supreme court reversed on other grounds without deciding that point: 'We do not need to resolve this issue' (Koble Investments v. Marquardt, 2026 WI 19, ¶¶ 24-25). The question is open. `criminal-activity-wi`, `dv-protections-notice-wi`. Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Wisconsin Statutes (2023-24, updated through 2025 Wis. Act 247), the Wisconsin Administrative Code (current through Register September 2026, No. 849) and the Wisconsin Constitution, loaded in the built-in browser from docs.legis.wisconsin.gov, saved and hash-matched (WI log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
