    bodyText:
      "Wisconsin imposes no statutory requirement that a written residential lease have every blank completed. Several Wisconsin rules still turn on what the lease and the papers given at signing say: the domestic abuse protections notice must be in the lease or an addendum (Wis. Stat. § 704.14); the names and addresses of the person who manages the premises and of the owner or person authorized to accept legal papers must be disclosed in writing at or before signing (Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 134.04(1)); and a written lease and rules must be shown to the prospective tenant before signing and before any earnest money or deposit is accepted, with copies given at signing (Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 134.03(1)). A blank left in a required statement is a missing statement. Fill in every blank before signing, as good practice rather than a statutory duty.",
    notes: "WI: Added 2026-10-03 at sync (SOP rules 27 and 51; the WI log §18.2 recorded `lease-completeness` CONFIRMED ABSENT with no row). CONFIRMED ABSENT: WI battery 110 (lease blanks / completeness: 17 hits, control 0; known positives passed; the hits are consumer-credit and motor-vehicle lease notices; WI log §17, §18.2). Required statements: Wis. Stat. § 704.14 (`dv-protections-notice-wi`), Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 134.04(1) (`landlord-disclosure-wi`), Wis. Admin. Code ATCP § 134.03(1) (`edu-lease-copy-wi`); ATCP §§ 134.03(1) and 134.04(1) read section-open at sync on docs.legis.wisconsin.gov (2026-10-03). Model: `edu-no-lease-completeness-rule-mi`. Rule 15: written section-open.",
