      "Give each affected tenant at least 90 days' written notice of any rent increase, and do not make an increase effective before the current fixed term ends. Use the state's Rent and Fee Increase Notice form and serve it the way eviction notices are served (personal delivery, or leaving it with a suitable person and mailing, or posting and mailing). 'Rent' here includes every recurring or periodic charge in the lease for use and occupancy, such as pet rent or parking, so a new or higher recurring fee is a rent increase. If rent is set by the tenant's income in a subsidized tenancy, 30 days' notice applies and the form is not required (a tenant paying with a portable voucher still gets the form). New rules other than rent changes take effect only after 30 days' written notice and at the end of the term, unless the tenant agrees sooner.",
