    topicKey: "rent-control",
    bodyText:
      "Washington limits rent increases for every type of tenancy, month-to-month or fixed-term: no increase at all during the first 12 months of a tenancy, and in any 12-month period no more than 7% plus the Seattle-area CPI, or 10%, whichever is less. The Department of Commerce publishes each year's maximum. Rent includes recurring fees. You may reset rent freely only after a tenant vacates. Exempt: units whose first certificate of occupancy is 12 years old or less at the date of the notice; units owned by public housing or development authorities or by rent-regulated nonprofits; qualifying tax-credit housing; and, if the owner is not a REIT, a corporation or an LLC with a corporate member, rooms sharing a kitchen or bath with the owner, owner-occupied single-family homes renting up to two units or bedrooms, and owner-occupied duplexes to fourplexes. Claim an exemption by stating its supporting facts in the increase notice. If you overcharge, the tenant must first give you a written demand to cure, and may end the lease on 20 days' written notice before the increase takes effect, owing rent for the full month of moving out but no fee; courts award the excess, up to three months of the unlawful amount, and fees, and the Attorney General may seek civil penalties of up to $7,500 per violation. Do not report unpaid unlawful increases to a screening service. No city or county may otherwise regulate the amount of rent, but check local notice and fee ordinances (Seattle, Tacoma, Spokane and others).",
    notes: "WA: 'a landlord may not increase the rent for any type of tenancy, regardless of whether the tenancy is month-to-month or for a term greater or lesser than month-to-month' (RCW 59.18.700(1)(a)): 'During the first 12 months after the tenancy begins' and 'in an amount greater than seven percent plus the consumer price index, or 10 percent, whichever is less'; reset after vacancy RCW 59.18.700(1)(b); Commerce publication and CPI definition RCW 59.18.700(1)(c); exemption facts in the notice RCW 59.18.700(2); cure demand and 20-day termination RCW 59.18.700(3); remedies RCW 59.18.700(5); no screening report RCW 59.18.700(7). Exemptions: RCW 59.18.710(1)(a)-(g), with the owner-type limit in RCW 59.18.710(2). Laws of 2025, ch. 209, §§ 101-102; the act's § 303 made it null and void unless funded, and Laws of 2025, ch. 424, § 130(30) appropriates the funds (WA log §1.2). Expires 'This section expires July 1, 2040' (RCW 59.18.700(8)). Preemption: 'No city or town of any class may enact, maintain, or enforce ordinances or other provisions which regulate the amount of rent to be charged for single-family or multiple-unit residential rental structures or sites' (RCW 35.21.830) (counties: RCW 36.01.130); local notice, relocation and fee ordinances are flagged, not resolved (rule 3). [BUILDER: `{{renewal_rent_increase_cap}}` must not exceed the RCW 59.18.700(1)(a)(ii) limit for a non-exempt WA unit; WA log §10.] (WA battery 11 (rent increase notice and limit): 17 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic)) (WA battery 12 (rent control preemption): 2 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Revised Code of Washington and the whole Washington Administrative Code (app.leg.wa.gov, every chapter page loaded in the built-in browser) and the Washington Constitution, saved and hash-matched (WA log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
