    topicKey: "lease-type-parity",
    bodyText:
      "Washington bars terms of payment or other material conditions that are more burdensome for a month-to-month tenant than for a fixed-term tenant, or the reverse. For any one unit, the rent you offer on different lease types may differ by no more than 5%, and that difference may not push the rent past the statewide increase limit. Offering a lower rent only for a 12-month lease, or a fee only for month-to-month, can therefore break the rule if the gap is larger than 5% or the burden is material.",
    notes: "WA: 'a landlord may not include terms of payment or other material conditions in a rental agreement that are more burdensome to a tenant for a month-to-month rental agreement than for a rental agreement where the term is greater or lesser than month-to-month, or vice versa' (RCW 59.18.700(4)(a)); 'the landlord may not charge a tenant more than a five percent difference in rent depending on the type of lease or rental agreement offered' (RCW 59.18.700(4)(b)) and 'This five percent difference may not cause the rent charged for a specific dwelling unit to exceed the rent increase limit in subsection (1) of this section'. New subject, new topic_key (rule 58). Expires with RCW 59.18.700 on July 1, 2040. (WA battery 167 (lease type parity / month-to-month burdens): 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Revised Code of Washington and the whole Washington Administrative Code (app.leg.wa.gov, every chapter page loaded in the built-in browser) and the Washington Constitution, saved and hash-matched (WA log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
