    bodyText:
      "You may charge an applicant for a tenant screening report, or your own actual screening costs up to what local screening services customarily charge, only if before getting any information you told the applicant in writing (or by posting) what you will check, what criteria can lead to denial, the consumer reporting agency's name and address and the applicant's rights to a free copy and to dispute it, and whether you accept a comprehensive reusable screening report. If you accept a reusable report, you may run your own report but may not charge the applicant for it. You may never charge a fee or deposit just to put someone on a waiting list.",
    notes: "WA: 'The landlord may charge a prospective tenant for costs incurred in obtaining a tenant screening report only if the prospective landlord provides the information as required in (a) of this subsection' (RCW 59.18.257(1)(b)(i)); own screening 'The amount charged may not exceed the customary costs charged by a screening service in the general area' (RCW 59.18.257(1)(b)(ii)); reusable report RCW 59.18.257(1)(a)(iv), (2) and the definition in RCW 59.18.030; waiting lists 'It shall be unlawful for a landlord to require a fee or deposit from a prospective tenant for the privilege of being placed on a waiting list to be considered as a tenant for a dwelling unit' (RCW 59.18.253(1)). Penalty up to $100 (RCW 59.18.257(3)). (WA battery 10 (fee caps and bans (application, screening, holding, fee for)): 23 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic)) (WA battery 126 (tenant screening): 17 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Revised Code of Washington and the whole Washington Administrative Code (app.leg.wa.gov, every chapter page loaded in the built-in browser) and the Washington Constitution, saved and hash-matched (WA log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
