    bodyText:
      "If you offer a monthly fee instead of a deposit, keep it strictly optional, offer it to every approved applicant and never use the choice in deciding an application; any nonrefundable part must be disclosed in the lease and separately acknowledged by the tenant; the fee is not rent, and nonpayment is not a cause for eviction, though you may sue for it; give the statutory disclosure form with the lease and any renewal; disclose in writing the terms of the insurance you buy with the fees (coverage, exclusions and caps) and, if the insurer requires you to try the tenant first, that the fee doesn't stop you or the insurer pursuing the tenant; use the fee only to buy that insurance, charging no more than its cost and administration; still give the move-in checklist; and let the tenant switch to a deposit at any time. Once the insurer pays a valid claim you may not seek those amounts from the tenant, and if it denies a claim as not a loss under the lease or for insufficient documentation you may not invoice or pursue the tenant for it; wait 60 days after notifying the tenant and sending documentation (and 60 days after filing the claim) before any collection; sue within one year after the tenancy ends. If you stop offering the option, give 60 days' notice before the end of the term, credit the fees paid against the deposit and offer a three-month installment plan.",
