    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "Only a court can evict. After the notice period runs, file an unlawful detainer case in superior court with the statutory Eviction Summons (Residential). You may ask for a show cause hearing 7 to 30 days after service, where the tenant may answer orally or in writing; if you prevail, the court issues a writ of restitution that the sheriff executes. If you can't serve the tenant personally after three attempts over at least two days at different times, you may post and mail (regular and certified) at least nine days before the return date, but then the court can only restore possession, not enter a money judgment. Subject to state funding, the court must appoint a lawyer for an indigent tenant, and hearings may be continued for that. Some superior courts add local rules: for example, requiring a copy of the lease with the complaint, pleading the just-cause basis, serving a local notice with the show cause order, or (in Skagit County) notifying the local legal aid office before filing. Check the county's local court rules.",
    notes: "WA: Summons form RCW 59.18.365(3); show cause 'which shall not be less than seven nor more than thirty days from the date of service of the order upon defendant' (RCW 59.18.370); hearing and writ RCW 59.18.380; alternative service RCW 59.18.055(1)-(4) and 'the court's jurisdiction is limited to restoring possession of the premises to the landlord and no money judgment may be entered against the tenant or tenants until such time as jurisdiction over the tenant or tenants is obtained' (RCW 59.18.055(2)); counsel 'the court must appoint an attorney for an indigent tenant in an unlawful detainer proceeding under this chapter and chapters 59.12 and 59.20 RCW' (RCW 59.18.640(1)) (subject to appropriation) and SPR 98.24W (appointment protocols). Local rules read (rule 39): San Juan County LCR 98-100 (only an owner, lessor or attorney in fact may bring the action; the complaint must attach the rental agreement and plead the RCW 59.18.650 basis for non-rent cases; no default or writ before the hearing), Skagit County SCLCR 8 (attach the lease; plead the just-cause basis with facts; certify that before filing you e-mailed or mailed notice of the intended filing and the tenant's contact information to Skagit Legal Aid (SCLCR 8(a)(1)(viii), Attachment B); serve the notices in the tenant's language where the Attorney General publishes them (SCLCR 8(a)(1)(ix)); attach the tenant notice to the show cause order), King County LCR 40 and LCR 41 (unlawful detainer calendars and administrative closure), Grant County LCR 80 and Island County LSPR 95 (continuances for counsel). Six rule sets hosted off-site (seven counties, since Benton and Franklin share one) were not fetched (WA log §7). (WA battery 83 (right to counsel / appointed attorney in eviction): 2 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)) (WA battery 111 (service of notices (unlawful detainer)): 6 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Revised Code of Washington and the whole Washington Administrative Code (app.leg.wa.gov, every chapter page loaded in the built-in browser) and the Washington Constitution, saved and hash-matched (WA log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
