      "In a nonpayment eviction, the tenant can end the case by paying the rent due, court costs, up to $75 in late fees and any awarded attorneys' fees before judgment or within five court days after it (longer with a rental assistance pledge letter), plus $50 for each prior post-judgment reinstatement in the last 12 months. Even after judgment the court may stay the writ for up to 90 days on a payment plan, weighing factors such as willfulness, hardship and payment history; the tenant must pay one month's rent within five court days. A tenant served three or more pay-or-vacate notices in the past 12 months generally can't get this stay. If the tenant later defaults on the plan, serve the statutory notice of default; the tenant then has three calendar days before the sheriff may execute the writ. The court may find the tenant eligible for the state landlord mitigation program to pay you.",
