      "Never remove or exclude a tenant without a court order: no lock changes, removal of doors or belongings, or utility shutoffs. A tenant locked out may recover possession or end the lease, plus damages and fees. Intentionally causing termination of water, heat, electricity or gas (other than for a reasonable time to make repairs) costs actual damages plus up to $100 a day. Taking or keeping a tenant's belongings without specific written consent, after a written demand for their return, can cost up to $500 a day (capped at $5,000) if intentional. The police may remove a squatter on the owner's sworn declaration, but never a tenant or a tenant's guest.",
    notes: "WA: 'It is unlawful for the landlord to remove or exclude from the premises the tenant thereof except under a court order so authorizing' (RCW 59.18.290(1)); utilities 'It shall be unlawful for a landlord to intentionally cause termination of any of his or her tenant's utility services, including water, heat, electricity, or gas, except for an interruption of utility services for a reasonable time in order to make necessary repairs' (RCW 59.18.300); property RCW 59.18.230(4); squatters RCW 9A.52.105(6) (the declaration acknowledges RCW 59.18.230 and 59.18.290, RCW 9A.52.105(4)(i)). Heat-alert shutoff limits: RCW 59.18.060(11)(a)-(c) (`edu-heat-alert-utility-wa`). (WA battery 79 (self-help eviction / lockout / utility shutoff): 177 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Revised Code of Washington and the whole Washington Administrative Code (app.leg.wa.gov, every chapter page loaded in the built-in browser) and the Washington Constitution, saved and hash-matched (WA log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
