    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "retaliation",
    bodyText:
      "While a tenant complies with the landlord-tenant act, you may not retaliate (by eviction, a rent increase, reduced services or added obligations) for good-faith complaints to a government agency about code violations that may endanger health or safety, or for asserting rights under the act. Acting within 90 days after such a complaint or the resulting inspection creates a presumption of retaliation; a tenant behind on rent or in breach when you give a termination notice gets the opposite presumption; a rent increase notice that states reasonable grounds avoids the presumption; impracticality of repairs with the tenant in place can rebut it for an eviction, and a tenant complaint made within 90 days after your notice of a rent increase is presumed not made in good faith. Do not retaliate against a tenant who lets a code official inspect.",
