    bodyText:
      "If a tenant who owes rent has shown by words or actions an intent not to return, you may enter, take any property left, and store it somewhere reasonably secure. Mail a notice by first-class mail to the last known address and any other known address giving your name and address, where the property is, the sale or disposal date, and the tenant's right to get it back first. Return it if the tenant asks in writing before the sale and pays the lesser of actual or reasonable moving and storage costs. You may sell or dispose of it 45 days after the notice (7 days if its total value is $250 or less, except personal papers, family pictures and keepsakes). Apply proceeds to what you're owed and hold any excess for one year. A sole tenant's death follows a separate procedure.",
    notes: "WA: Abandonment RCW 59.18.310(1); notice and storage RCW 59.18.310(2); 'After forty-five days from the date the notice of such sale or disposal is mailed or personally delivered to the tenant, the landlord may sell or dispose of such property, including personal papers, family pictures, and keepsakes'; $250 rule and excess proceeds RCW 59.18.310(2); deceased tenant RCW 59.18.310(3), 59.18.595. Shared `abandoned-property` texts are not tagged (they rest on other states' periods; WA log §2.3). (WA battery 73 (abandonment of premises): 18 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic)) (WA battery 74 (tenant personal property left behind / storage / sale): 11 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic)). Read section-open 2026-10-03 from the whole Revised Code of Washington and the whole Washington Administrative Code (app.leg.wa.gov, every chapter page loaded in the built-in browser) and the Washington Constitution, saved and hash-matched (WA log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
