    topicKey: "dv-lease-termination",
    bodyText:
      "A tenant who gives written notice that the tenant or a household member was a victim of domestic violence, sexual assault, unlawful harassment or stalking, and provides a protection order or a qualified third party's signed report, may end the lease without further obligation if the request is made within 90 days of the incident. The tenant owes no rent after the month of moving out, and owes that month's rent unless the termination also met the 20-day periodic notice rule, and gets the full deposit back, subject to lawful deductions (documented damage beyond ordinary wear, unpaid rent or charges), whatever the lease says about forfeiture. If you claim damages from the state landlord mitigation program, you may not keep any of that tenant's deposit or pursue the tenant for property damage beyond the deposit. Other co-tenants remain bound unless they are victims. If the tenant or a household member is a victim of sexual assault, stalking or unlawful harassment by the landlord (including the landlord's employees), the tenant may leave first and deliver the order or report within seven days of moving out, owing rent only until the later of moving out or delivering the report and notice, with a pro rata refund of prepaid rent. The tenant may instead change or add locks at the tenant's expense, giving you written notice and the documents within seven days; the lease then ends 90 days after that notice unless within 60 days the tenant tells you in writing they want to stay or ends it under the leave-first route, and rent continues until the lease ends and the tenant vacates; once you receive that notice your entry is restricted (emergencies with police or fire officials, or at least two days' written notice of date, time and purpose under RCW 59.18.150), including if the tenant stays.",
