      "Beyond the military and victim rights, a Washington tenant may end the lease without further obligation if: you fail to remedy a defective condition within the statutory period to start repairs after written notice and a reasonable time to finish; a court or arbitrator finds a defect too substantial to fix in time; you threaten the tenant with a firearm or deadly weapon and are arrested; another tenant threatens them with a firearm or deadly weapon, is arrested, the tenant notifies you, and you don't file an eviction within seven calendar days after law enforcement notifies you of the arrest; or you raise rent unlawfully above the statewide limit (after a written cure demand, on at least 20 days' written notice, ending before the increase takes effect, owing rent for the full month of moving out). If you knowingly rent a unit an agency has condemned or declared unlawful to occupy, the tenant recovers the greater of three months' rent or up to three times actual damages, plus costs and attorneys' fees, and, on leaving, all deposits and prepaid rent; relocation assistance is owed where you knew or should have known of the conditions, with exceptions for tenant or third-party illegal conduct you didn't know of, natural disasters and eminent domain.",
