    bodyText:
      "Washington sets no statutory holdover measure for residential tenancies (the general double-damages rule for unlawful detainer doesn't apply to them), so a lease could set a stipulated daily charge for a tenant who stays after the tenancy lawfully ends. The library doesn't offer a premium rate: because a fixed term usually becomes month-to-month rather than ending, a holdover only arises after a just-cause ending, often for nonpayment, where the tenant can still pay and be reinstated and late fees in judgment are capped at $75, so a premium could be read as a penalty or a second late charge. The library's holdover clause charges daily rent instead. If you want a premium, add your own clause and take advice first.",
