    bodyText:
      "Washington has no statute written for fire or casualty damage to a rental. If the unit becomes defective, the tenant uses the repair-notice remedies: if you don't start repairs within the statutory period after written notice and finish within a reasonable time, the tenant may end the lease, and a court may end it if the defect can't feasibly be fixed in time. You may end a tenancy for a damaged unit only on a just cause, for example a unit certified or condemned as uninhabitable by a local agency where continued occupancy would expose you to civil or criminal penalties (30 days' notice, or as much as the order allows) or demolition or substantial rehabilitation (120 days; not before a fixed term ends unless the tenant agrees in writing and gets at least 60 days). If a code agency condemns the unit and you knew or should have known of the conditions, you owe relocation assistance (not for natural disasters or tenant or third-party illegal conduct you didn't know of). A lease clause letting you end the lease after a casualty isn't offered: it would add a cause the statute doesn't list. If the tenant, family, guest or anyone under the tenant's control caused the damage, you have no repair duty for it and the tenant has no remedy for it under the landlord-tenant act.",
