      "Any lease term waiving a section of the landlord-tenant act is unenforceable (the narrow exemption agreement aside), and a lease may not provide that the tenant: waives rights or remedies under the act; gives up the right to sue or join a class action against you or your agents; signs a nondisclosure agreement about the lease or the offer (rent, deposits, fees, concessions, move-in gifts, specials); authorizes a confession of judgment; agrees to pay your attorneys' fees except as the act allows and a court awards; exculpates or limits your liability under law, or indemnifies you for it; agrees in advance to a particular arbitrator, or to arbitrate unless you pay all its cost and the agreement is notarized; pays late fees on rent paid within five days of its due date; or must pay rent electronically only. A lease term creating a lien on the tenant's belongings is void. Agreements made in an eviction case that make the tenant pay more than the act allows, or waive the tenant's rights, are void too. Knowingly using a lease with a prohibited term exposes you to statutory damages of up to twice the monthly rent.",
