      "A lease may not make the tenant pay your attorneys' fees except as the landlord-tenant act authorizes and a court awards. Any lease clause giving one party fees is read to give fees to whichever party prevails, and can't be waived. In an eviction, a court may award you fees, but not after a default judgment, and not where the rent awarded is two months' rent or $1,200 or less, whichever is greater; if the tenant has moved to stay the writ, the court may award you fees only if the tenant is reinstated under the hardship-stay rules, and those fees are then subject to repayment under the stay order. Many sections (deposits, retaliation, lockouts, entry) give the prevailing party fees directly.",
