    bodyText:
      "A week-to-week rent increase needs written notice at least seven days before it takes effect. Any other tenancy needs written notice at least 90 days before the effective date, and no increase is allowed in the first year. The notice must state the amount of the increase, the new rent, the effective date and, if the increase is above the statewide cap, the facts supporting the exemption you rely on. The terms of a fixed term tenancy, including the rent, can't be changed by either party alone during the term.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.323(1), (2)(a)-(b), (3)(a)-(d) ('The notices required under this section must specify' (ORS 90.323(3))); fixed term: 'The terms of a fixed term tenancy, including the amount of rent, may not be unilaterally amended by the landlord or tenant' (ORS 90.220(2)); rent increases must comply with ORS 90.323 (ORS 90.220(7)(a)). Notice by a method in ORS 90.155 (personal delivery, first class mail, mail and attachment if the lease allows, or e-mail if a written addendum under ORS 90.155(1)(d) allows it; `edu-notice-delivery-or`). Adding three days for mail is cautious practice: ORS 90.155(2) adds them to 'the minimum period for compliance or termination of tenancy' and doesn't name rent-increase notices (Claude's reading). Present (rule 27). (OR battery 110 (rent increase limits and notices): 23 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
