    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "rent-control",
    bodyText:
      "Oregon bars cities and counties from enacting rent control on residential property, with exceptions: rent limits a state agency, city, county or urban renewal agency reserves on property it has contracted to benefit (for example with tax exemptions, financing or subsidies) to provide reduced rents for low-income tenants; condominium conversion ordinances limiting increases during the conversion notice period; temporary controls after a natural or man-made disaster that materially eliminates a significant part of the rental housing supply, removed once supply is substantially normal again; and inclusionary-zoning requirements that set the price of new multiunit housing. ORS 91.225 preempts only rent control; some cities, such as Portland, have other rental ordinances (relocation assistance, screening, notice rules), so check local law.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 91.225(2) ('a city or county shall not enact any ordinance or resolution which controls the rent that may be charged for the rental of any dwelling unit' (ORS 91.225(2))), (3) (low-income benefit contracts), (4) (condominium conversion, within the ORS 90.493 limit), (5) (temporary disaster controls), (7); ORS 197A.465(4) ('Notwithstanding ORS 91.225, a city or county may adopt a land use regulation or functional plan provision' (ORS 197A.465(4)); restated in Or. Laws 2026, ch. 79, §§ 2-3, operative January 1, 2028 under § 14(1)). Whether a given local ordinance is preempted is flagged, not resolved (rule 3). The statewide cap is `edu-rent-increase-limit-or`. ORS 90.465 (read) gives a city of more than 300,000 people a right to recover its tenant-relocation costs from an owner whose code violations cause condemnation; it gives tenants no claim. (OR battery 111 (local rent control preemption): 17 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
