    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "required-fees",
    bodyText:
      "Oregon lets you charge only the fees its fee statute lists, and each must be described in your written rental agreement. Per-occurrence fees: late charges (as the late-charge rules allow); a dishonored check (up to $35 plus your bank's charge); removing or tampering with a working smoke or carbon monoxide alarm (up to $250, unless the State Fire Marshal fines the tenant); and, for a tenant who leaves a fixed term early without cause, a fee of no more than one and one-half times the monthly rent (not allowed when the tenant leaves under the victim or servicemember termination rights, or when, after you give a qualifying-reason notice ending a fixed term, the tenant ends the tenancy early with the tenant's own written notice of at least 30 days), in which case you give up rent after the date you knew or reasonably should have known of the departure, and reletting costs. Rule-violation fees: after a written warning notice, for the second violation of the same kind within a year (up to $50) and later ones (up to $50 plus 5% of that period's rent), but only for late utility charges, pet or service-animal waste or garbage left outside the unit, parking violations and improper vehicle use, smoking in a clearly designated nonsmoking area (up to $250, 24 hours after the warning) or an unauthorized pet capable of causing damage (up to $250, 48 hours after the warning). You may not charge a fee at the start of the tenancy for an expected expense, any other liquidated damages, or both a fee and a termination for the same violation. Outside the fee rules: attorney fees awarded by a court, screening charges, charges for improvements or other actions the tenant asks for that the lease or law doesn't require of you (including replacing a lost key), processing fees passed through under the payment rules, renter's insurance requirements and association assessments passed through as the statute allows. A fee not allowed by the statute costs twice your tenant's actual damages or $300, whichever is greater (this penalty doesn't apply to the per-occurrence fees listed above).",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.302(1) ('A fee must be described in a written rental agreement' (ORS 90.302(1))), (2)(a)-(e), (3)(a)-(b) (warning notice, 30-day timing, no fee plus termination, no deduction from rent), (5) ('a landlord may not charge a tenant any form of liquidated damages, however designated' (ORS 90.302(5))), (7)(a)-(f), (8). ORS 90.302(2)(d) (a written pet agreement or facility pet rule 'pursuant to ORS 90.530') was read as a facility fee and is not used for ordinary tenancies (Claude's reading of the cross-reference; `edu-pet-fees-or`). ORS 90.302(2)(e) (no fee for departures under ORS 90.453(2), 90.472 or 90.475); ORS 90.302(2)(e)(A); ORS 90.372(1)-(2); ORS 90.302(8) (penalty does not apply to fees described in subsection (2)). ORS 90.302(7)(d) as rewritten by Or. Laws 2026, ch. 23, § 5 (effective June 5, 2026, § 8; applies to agreements entered into before, on or after that date, § 7(1)). Library clauses: `late-fee-limit-or`, `nsf-fee-limit-or`, `alarm-tampering-fee-or`, `early-termination-or`, `rule-violation-fees-or`. Collection of an unlawful fee can also be an unlawful collection practice (`edu-collection-practices-or`). Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
