      "Oregon has no general sales tax, so long-term residential rent is not taxed. The state transient lodging tax doesn't apply to a dwelling the same person rents for 30 consecutive days or more; shorter stays may be taxed, and vacation and transient occupancies fall outside the landlord-tenant act anyway.",
    notes: "OR: Present or absent (rule 27): no state tax on residential rent found (OR battery 69 (tax on rent or lodging): 42 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142). State transient lodging tax: ORS 320.305(1)(a) (1.5 percent on transient lodging), with an exemption for 'A dwelling unit that is leased or otherwise occupied by the same person for a consecutive period of 30 days or more during the year' (ORS 320.308(6)); local lodging taxes flagged, not resolved. Vacation occupancy exclusion: ORS 90.110(6) and the 'vacation occupancy' definition in ORS 90.100 (no more than 45 days, not a principal residence). Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
