      "You may charge an applicant a screening charge only to cover the cost of screening, no more than your average actual cost or the customary charge of a screening company or credit agency for comparable screening, and only after you adopt written screening criteria and give the applicant written notice of the matters the statute lists (the charge, your criteria and process, dispute and appeal rights, nondiscrimination, the rent and deposits, any renter's insurance requirement and refund rights) and an estimate of available units and applications already under consideration. Give a receipt and confirmation of any outside screening; charge only one screening charge in any 60-day period across all your units; don't charge when you know no unit is or will soon be available; and refund within 30 days if you fill the unit before screening or haven't screened before the applicant withdraws in writing. Violations cost twice the screening charge plus $250.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.295(1)-(6) read section-open: 'The amount of any applicant screening charge must not be greater than the landlord’s average actual cost of screening applicants or the customary amount charged by tenant screening companies or consumer credit reporting agencies for a comparable level of screening' (ORS 90.295(2)); one charge 'within any 60-day period' (ORS 90.295(1)(b)); notice items ORS 90.295(3)(a)-(c); no charge when no units are available (ORS 90.295(4)); refunds ORS 90.295(5); 'twice the amount of any applicant screening charge paid, plus $250' (ORS 90.295(6)(b)). Screening charges are outside the ORS 90.302 fee rules (ORS 90.302(7)(b)). Applicants' rights in evaluation and denial: `edu-tenant-screening-or`. (OR battery 129 (screening charges and application fees): 72 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
