    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "tenant-screening",
    bodyText:
      "In evaluating an applicant you may not consider an eviction action that was dismissed or ended in the applicant's favor, an eviction judgment entered five or more years before the application, judgments for claims that arose between April 1, 2020 and March 1, 2022, arrests unless they led to charges in the listed categories and the applicant was convicted, or the charges are pending and the applicant isn't in a diversion, conditional discharge or deferral program; convictions or charges outside the listed categories (drug-related crimes other than marijuana use or possession alone, person crimes, sex offenses, financial fraud, and crimes affecting property, health, safety or peaceful enjoyment) or for conduct no longer illegal; possession of a medical marijuana card or status as a medical marijuana patient; or rent unpaid during the emergency period; you may not ask about immigration status, and you must accept the identification documents the statute lists in place of a Social Security number. If you deny an applicant after applying your criteria, give a written statement of one or more reasons within 14 days (a $100 remedy if you don't); before denying on criminal history, let the applicant submit supplemental evidence and make an individualized assessment. From January 2, 2028, the emergency-period limits drop out of the screening statute.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.303(1)-(6) and ORS 90.304 read section-open, current versions ((2)(a)-(b), (3)(a)-(e), (4) as described; (1)-(5) in the version operative January 2, 2028); the dated version of ORS 90.303 operative January 2, 2028 (Or. Laws 2021, ch. 39, §§ 10, 12; Or. Laws 2025, ch. 226, § 5) read beside it (kickoff lead 1; OR log §1.2, §10): it removes the emergency-period limbs. Identification: ORS 90.306 (`edu-immigration-status-or`). Remedy: 'If a landlord fails to comply with this section, the applicant may recover from the landlord $100' (ORS 90.304(6)); ORS 90.303 states no remedy of its own. Individualized assessment: ORS 90.304(5). Local screening ordinances (Portland) flagged, not resolved. (OR battery 100 (screening criminal history and applicant evaluation): 55 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
