      "Every rental dwelling must have approved smoke alarms or detectors, and you must supply, install and maintain them and give written testing instructions when the tenant first takes possession. Your maintenance duty, including working batteries, arises before each new tenancy and during the tenancy only when the tenant gives written notice of a deficiency other than dead batteries; the tenant tests at least every six months and replaces dead batteries. If the unit contains a carbon monoxide source, or is in a structure with one and connected to its room by a door, ductwork or ventilation shaft, you may not start a new tenancy without properly installed, working carbon monoxide alarms, and the same instruction, battery and repair rules apply. Both are habitability items. Removing or tampering with a working alarm is prohibited; you may charge a fee of up to $250 for it if your lease describes the fee, unless the State Fire Marshal has assessed the tenant a civil penalty for the same conduct. Placement and type follow State Fire Marshal rules and the state building code.",
