    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "for-cause-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "Either side may end a week-to-week tenancy on 10 days' written notice at any time. During the first year of occupancy you may end a month-to-month tenancy without cause on 30 days' written notice, and a fixed term ending within the first year on 30 days' written notice before its end date. The first year of occupancy lasts as long as any tenant has lived in the unit for one year or less. After the first year of occupancy, you may end a month-to-month tenancy only for a tenant cause under the notice statutes or for a qualifying landlord reason, and a fixed term ending after the first year becomes month-to-month unless you agree a new term, the tenant gives 30 days' notice, or you have a qualifying reason. The qualifying reasons, each needing at least 90 days' written notice stating the reason and supporting facts: demolition or conversion to a non-residential use; repairs or renovations within a reasonable time where the premises are unsafe or unfit for occupancy, or the unit will be unsafe or unfit during the work; you or an immediate family member moving in as a primary residence when you have no comparable vacant unit in the same building; or a buyer who will live there and whose accepted offer you document. With a qualifying-reason notice you must also pay the tenant one month's rent, unless you own an interest in four or fewer dwelling units. For the buyer reason you may give 60 days' notice instead of 90 if you pay the tenant one more month's rent with the notice, on top of any payment already required. A fixed term also doesn't convert if the tenant had three violations in 12 months, each with a written warning in the statutory form, and you give 90 days' notice. In a building or property with no more than two units where you live, you may end a month-to-month tenancy after the first year on 60 days' notice without cause (30 days for a documented owner-occupant buyer) and a fixed term at its end on 30 days' notice. Terminating in violation of the qualifying-reason, payment or three-violation rules costs three months' rent plus actual damages and gives the tenant a defense to possession; claims must be brought within one year. Some cities add relocation-payment and notice rules.",
    notes: "OR: Rule 41: this is Oregon's `for-cause-eviction` verdict: a for-cause rule after the first year of occupancy, with listed landlord reasons. ORS 90.427(1)-(9), (12) read whole ('“First year of occupancy” includes all periods in which any of the tenants has resided in the dwelling unit for one year or less' (ORS 90.427(1)(a)); week-to-week ORS 90.427(2); buyer 60-day option ORS 90.427(5)(b)(A)-(B); 'at any time after the first year of occupancy, the landlord may terminate the tenancy only' (ORS 90.427(3)(c)) for a tenant cause under ORS 86.782(6)(c), 90.380(5), 90.392, 90.394, 90.396, 90.398, 90.405, 90.440 or 90.445, or a qualifying landlord reason; payment 'equal to one month’s periodic rent' (ORS 90.427(6)(a)(B)) and the four-unit exception (ORS 90.427(6)(b)); remedy 'three months’ rent in addition to actual damages' (ORS 90.427(9)(a)(A)) and a defense to possession; claims within one year (ORS 90.427(9)(b))). As amended by Or. Laws 2025, ch. 291, § 1 (history line). The shared end-of-term, holdover, early-termination and surrender clauses that assume a landlord can simply end a tenancy are replaced by Oregon rows (OR log §2.2). A tenant who receives a qualifying-reason notice during a fixed term may leave early on 30 days' notice without a fee (ORS 90.372). Rent for the next tenancy after a first-year no-cause termination: ORS 90.323(4). Local ordinances (Portland's relocation assistance) flagged, not resolved (rule 3). (OR battery 140 (week-to-week and month-to-month termination notice): 32 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
