      "Without cause: week-to-week, 10 days' written notice by either side; month-to-month, the tenant may give 30 days' written notice at any time, and you may give 30 days' notice only in the first year of occupancy (after that, see the for-cause rules); a fixed term ending after the first year continues month to month unless ended as the statute allows, and a tenant who wants it to end must give 30 days' written notice before the end date. For cause: 30 days for a material violation with a cure period of at least 14 days (7 and 4 days in a week-to-week tenancy; the cure date can be the delivery date for a separate, completed act that isn't ongoing), 10 days for a repeat within six months (4 in a week-to-week tenancy), 72 hours or 10 or 13 days for nonpayment, 24 hours for serious threats or violence, intentional substantial damage, certain false application information, or acts outrageous in the extreme, 10 days for an unpermitted pet capable of causing damage to persons or property (curable by removing it; 10 days without cure for a repeat within six months), and the special 24- and 48-hour notices for a household member's violence, a unit posted unsafe, drug and alcohol free housing and group recovery homes. Every termination notice must include the veterans' information the statute requires, a nonpayment notice must come with the court's rental-assistance notice, and a notice served by first class mail alone adds three days.",
