    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "cure-and-eviction-grounds",
    bodyText:
      "For a material violation of the lease or of the tenant's statutory duties, your 30-day notice must describe the violation and, if it can be cured, say so, describe at least one way to cure it and set a cure date at least 14 days after delivery; if the tenant cures by that date the tenancy continues. For a separate, completed act that isn't ongoing, the cure date can be the date of delivery, so the act can't be cured by waiting. If substantially the same violation recurs within six months after a notice, a 10-day notice with no right to cure is allowed (its termination date can't be earlier than the one in the earlier notice), but not when the only violation is failing to pay the current month's rent. In a week-to-week tenancy the 30, 14 and 10 days become 7, 4 and 4 days. Nonpayment of a late charge, fee or utility charge is a for-cause matter, not a nonpayment-of-rent matter. A 24-hour notice has no cure, except that a pet-based notice is cured by removing the pet. Accepting rent with knowledge of a violation during three or more separate rental periods, or accepting performance that varies from the lease, waives termination for that violation unless an exception applies, such as a written warning with the statutory content given before the third period; 24-hour causes are not waived.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.392(1)-(6) ('If the violation is conduct that was a separate and distinct act or omission and is not ongoing, no earlier than the date of delivery of the notice as provided in ORS 90.155' (ORS 90.392(4)(a)(B)); 'A landlord may not terminate a rental agreement under this subsection if the only violation is a failure to pay the current month’s rent' (ORS 90.392(5)(b))); late charges, fees, utility charges ORS 90.260(6), 90.302(6), 90.315(4)(e); 24-hour causes and pet cure ORS 90.396(1)-(2); waiver ORS 90.412(2), (4)(b)-(c), (4)(e) (`edu-waiver-by-acceptance-or`). Rule 43: `default-by-tenant-or` leaves the cure to these rules. Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
