    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "nonpayment-notice",
    bodyText:
      "For week-to-week tenancies, give at least 72 hours' written notice, no sooner than the fifth day of the rental period (counting the day rent is due). For other tenancies, give either at least 10 days' written notice no sooner than the eighth day, or at least 13 days' notice no sooner than the fifth day. The notice must state the amount of rent that must be paid and the date and time by which it must be paid to cure, must include the veterans' information, and must be delivered with a copy of the Oregon Judicial Department's rental assistance notice (also required with any summons for nonpayment). A tenant's payment mailed within the notice period is timely unless you served the notice personally, by mail and attachment or by mail and e-mail, both the written rental agreement and the notice name a payment location on the premises or where the tenant has always paid in person, and that place is available to the tenant throughout the notice period. Since June 5, 2026, you must let tenants pay by check or another commercially reasonable method and may not require electronic payment; if you refuse a payment made that way, you may not charge a late fee or terminate for nonpayment. A court will dismiss a nonpayment case if the rental-assistance notice wasn't delivered, if you caused the tenant not to tender rent (including by not reasonably cooperating with a rental assistance program), or if the tenant tenders rental assistance or other payment covering the amount owed; after a dismissal for tender you may charge the tenant the court filing fees, though paying them isn't a condition of the dismissal. Only rent supports this notice: late charges, fees and utility charges don't.",
