      "Every nonpayment termination notice and every summons in a nonpayment case must be delivered with the rental assistance notice the Oregon Judicial Department posts on its website. 'Nonpayment' for this purpose includes rent, late charges, utility charges and other fees, but not payments owed for damage to the premises. The court must dismiss the case if you didn't deliver the rental assistance notice, if you caused the tenant not to tender rent (for example by not reasonably participating in a rental assistance program), or if the tenant has tendered rental assistance or other payment covering the amount in the notice. Victims' calls for police or emergency help are protected separately (`edu-dv-eviction-protection-or`).",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.395(1)-(5) ('The landlord caused the tenant to not tender rent, including as a result of the landlord’s failure to reasonably participate with a rental assistance program' (ORS 90.395(3)(b))); ORS 105.136. A tenant's right to call police or emergency assistance: no Oregon landlord-tenant statute addresses it separately; victims' calls are protected by ORS 90.449(1)(c) (OR battery 214 (police or emergency response and tenants (rerun of 65)): 70 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) (the battery's only chapter 90, 91 or 105 hit is ORS 90.449; battery 65 was narrower). Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
