    topicKey: "expedited-criminal-eviction",
    bodyText:
      "You may end a tenancy on at least 24 hours' written notice, specifying the cause and the date and time of termination, if the tenant, someone in the tenant's control or the tenant's pet seriously threatens or inflicts substantial personal injury on someone other than the tenant on the premises (or injures a neighbor living nearby); the tenant or someone in the tenant's control recklessly endangers others; the tenant or someone in the tenant's control intentionally causes substantial damage (a pet, on more than one occasion); the tenant intentionally gave substantial false information on the application within the past year about the tenant's own criminal conviction that would have been material to your acceptance of the application, and you act within 30 days of discovering it; or there is an act outrageous in the extreme, on the premises or in the immediate vicinity, such as prostitution, unlawful manufacture, delivery or possession of a controlled substance, unlicensed cannabis extract manufacture, a bias crime or burglary. A pet-based notice can be cured by removing the pet. Four more 24-hour notices: (1) to a tenant who commits a criminal act of physical violence related to domestic violence, sexual assault, bias crime or stalking against a household member who is a tenant (only the perpetrator's tenancy ends); (2) to an unauthorized person in possession after the tenant has vacated, if the rental agreement prohibited subletting or other occupants without your written permission and you haven't knowingly accepted rent from that person; (3) to a tenant after an agency posts the unit unsafe for conditions you didn't cause; and (4) in group recovery homes. Someone is in the tenant's control if the tenant lets them enter or stay after the tenant reasonably knows or should know of their act or likelihood to commit such an act. Drug and alcohol free housing programs have a separate 48-hour rule for tenants there less than two years (curable within 24 hours), with a 24-hour notice and no cure for a repeat within six months.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.396(1)-(5) ('An act that is outrageous in the extreme is more extreme or serious than an act that warrants a 30-day termination under ORS 90.392' (ORS 90.396(1)(f))); ORS 90.398 (drug and alcohol free housing). Victims: a landlord may not terminate because a tenant is a victim or for an incident against the tenant (ORS 90.449; `edu-dv-eviction-protection-or`). ORS 90.445(1)-(2); ORS 90.403(1)(a)-(c); ORS 90.380(5)(b); ORS 90.440(2), (6); ORS 90.398(1)-(3); ORS 90.396(1)(e)(A)-(C), (3). Gambling: a lease made knowing or having reason to believe the place will be used for gambling is void (ORS 91.240(1)-(2)); if the lessee or occupant, or another person with their knowledge or consent, uses it for gambling, the lessor may avoid the lease on discovery and recover possession (ORS 91.240(3)); ORS 91.245 (penalty) (OR battery 85 (gambling and leases): 20 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142). Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
