    bodyText:
      "You may not end or refuse to renew a tenancy, serve a termination notice, sue or threaten to sue for possession, raise rent, cut services or refuse to rent because a tenant or applicant is or was a victim of domestic violence, sexual assault, bias crime or stalking, because of a lease violation that consists of an incident against them, or because of criminal activity or a police or emergency response in which they are the victim; nor may you apply different rules to them. The one exception: after a prior written warning about the perpetrator's conduct, you may end the victim's tenancy if the tenant lets the perpetrator on the premises and the perpetrator is an actual and imminent threat to others, or lets the perpetrator live there as an unauthorized occupant. Violations cost up to two months' rent or twice actual damages, whichever is greater, and give the tenant a defense. A tenant isn't responsible for damage caused by the perpetrator, though you may require verification of victim status as ORS 90.453 provides. If one tenant commits physical violence related to these crimes against a household member who is also a tenant, you may end the perpetrator's tenancy alone on 24 hours' written notice, without new rent, deposits or fees for the others.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.449(1)-(5) ('A landlord may not impose different rules, conditions or standards or selectively enforce rules, conditions or standards against a tenant or applicant on the basis that the tenant or applicant is or has been a victim of domestic violence, sexual assault, bias crime or stalking' (ORS 90.449(2))); ORS 90.325(3)(b), (4); ORS 90.445(1)-(4); ORS 105.128 (court removal of a perpetrator, read). (OR battery 123 (domestic violence, sexual assault, bias crime, stalking (victims)): 39 hits, control 0; known positives passed (3 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
