      "A tenant who gives you actual notice of being a victim of domestic violence, sexual assault, bias crime or stalking may ask you to change the locks; no verification is needed. You must promptly change them at the tenant's expense or let the tenant do it; if you don't act promptly, the tenant may change them and must give you a key. If the perpetrator is a tenant of the same unit, the tenant must first give you a court order requiring the perpetrator to move out; you then owe the perpetrator no access or keys, the perpetrator stays jointly liable for rent and damages incurred before exclusion, you may not charge the others extra rent, deposits or fees because of the exclusion, and the perpetrator's tenancy ends when the order becomes final.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.459(1)-(3) ('A tenant is not required to provide verification of the domestic violence, sexual assault, bias crime or stalking to initiate the changing of the locks' (ORS 90.459(1))). `keys` (tagged) is read with this rule. (OR battery 123 (domestic violence, sexual assault, bias crime, stalking (victims)): 39 hits, control 0; known positives passed (3 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
