    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "abandoned-property",
    bodyText:
      "When a tenancy has ended and you reasonably believe the tenant left personal property with no intention of claiming it, or the tenant has been absent seven days after an unexecuted court order, or the sheriff has given you possession, you must store, sell or dispose of the property only as the statute provides. First give written notice, personally delivered or by first class mail to the tenant at the premises, any known post office box and any forwarding address, saying the property is considered abandoned and must be claimed by a date at least five days after personal delivery or eight days after mailing (45 days for a manufactured dwelling or floating home), where it is stored and how to arrange removal. Store it with reasonable care in a place of safekeeping; you may require payment of removal and storage charges before releasing it, except where the sheriff gave you possession. If the tenant responds by the date, you must make it available for removal by appointment during the next 15 days (30 for a dwelling or home); otherwise it is conclusively presumed abandoned and you may sell it at a public or private sale, or dispose of it if its reasonable value is $1,000 or less or so low that storage and sale costs would probably exceed what a sale would bring, deducting notice, storage and sale costs and unpaid rent from any proceeds. Special rules apply to a sole tenant's death, drug-lab sites and vehicles. A rental agreement may not waive these rights. If you don't comply, the tenant is relieved of liability for unpaid rent and for damage that wasn't deliberate, intentional or grossly negligent and may recover up to twice actual damages; a deliberate and malicious violation also costs twice actual damages.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.425(1)-(27) read whole (notice and timing ORS 90.425(3), (5)-(6); storage ORS 90.425(7); removal period and presumption ORS 90.425(8)-(9); sale or disposal ORS 90.425(10)-(11); notice dates ORS 90.425(6)(a)-(b); storage charges ORS 90.425(5)(f)-(g), (8); disposal ORS 90.425(10)(b)(C); proceeds ORS 90.425(13)(a); liability and noncompliance ORS 90.425(15)-(17); death ORS 90.425(20)-(21); drug sites ORS 90.425(23); vehicles ORS 90.425(25), ORS 98.830; post-termination agreements ORS 90.425(26)(a); no waiver 'A landlord may not, as part of a rental agreement, require a tenant, a personal representative, a designated person or any lienholder or owner to waive any right provided by this section' (ORS 90.425(26)(b))). Liens: no landlord lien on household goods (ORS 90.420). Figures read from ORS 90.425 as printed in the 2025 edition. (OR battery 121 (abandoned personal property after tenancy): 12 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
