    bodyText:
      "If a tenant abandons the unit, you must make reasonable efforts to rent it at a fair rental. Renting it to a new tenant for a term starting before the old agreement would have ended terminates the old agreement as of the new tenancy's start. If you don't make reasonable efforts, or you accept the abandonment as a surrender, the agreement is treated as ended by you as of the date you knew or should have known of the abandonment. For a month-to-month or week-to-week tenancy, the remaining term is treated as one month or one week. Taking back the keys, or trying to re-rent, is not by itself acceptance of a surrender. If you charge the early-termination fee Oregon allows for a fixed term, these rules don't apply and you can't also recover later rent or re-renting costs.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.410(3) ('If the tenant abandons the dwelling unit, the landlord shall make reasonable efforts to rent it for a fair rental' (ORS 90.410(3))); ORS 90.148; ORS 90.125(1); rent liability ending ORS 90.220(8); ORS 90.302(2)(e)(C). (OR battery 133 (abandonment, reletting and mitigation): 18 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
