    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "After a valid termination notice expires, you may recover possession only through a court action for possession (forcible entry and detainer). You may not file until the notice period (or fixed term) has run. The complaint and summons follow statutory forms; the summons must include the veterans' information and, for nonpayment, the rental-assistance notice. The clerk sets the first appearance seven days after the judicial day following payment of the filing fee (15 days for nonpayment cases), and may delay it up to seven days; if the tenant contests, trial is usually set within 15 days (15 to 30 days for nonpayment claims), and a continuance of more than two days requires the tenant to give security for or pay accruing rent into court. Until September 28, 2027, a nonpayment trial can be rescheduled to 90 to 104 days out for a tenant who is the parent of a child under 12 months living in the unit, receives medical assistance and has been approved for health-related social needs housing supports. If you win, the sheriff executes a writ after a four-day notice of restitution; the judgment can't be enforced if you later sign a new agreement or accept rent for occupancy after the judgment. The court may award attorney fees to the prevailing party, but not to a landlord whose tenant does not contest. The filing fee is set by statute. Some counties' courts have their own forms and procedures.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 105.100-105.168 read whole (ORS 90.255 (prevailing-party attorney fees); ORS 105.113(1)-(2) (summons form; ORS 408.515 information); ORS 105.115(2) (filing timing), 105.124 (complaint form), 105.135 and 105.136 (summons, first appearance, notice), 105.137 (appearance and trial; attorney fees not to a plaintiff where the defendant doesn't contest, ORS 105.137(3)), 105.140 (rent into court on a continuance), 105.151 and 105.159 (notice of restitution and writ), 105.161(4) (new agreement or rent after judgment), 105.130 (filing fee)). ORS 105.135(2)(a)-(b). Medicaid new-parent rescheduling: Or. Laws 2025, ch. 598, § 2, printed as a note after ORS 105.137 (read; repealed September 28, 2027 per the note). (OR battery 236 (stays, continuances and postponement of evictions (rerun of 228)): 216 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Self-help: ORS 90.435. Court rules (UTCR and supplementary local rules) were not loaded: OR log §7 (open item, rule 39 boundary). Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
