    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "casualty-termination",
    bodyText:
      "Oregon has no general casualty-termination statute. If a government agency posts the unit as unsafe and unlawful to occupy after the tenancy begins, a tenant who didn't cause the conditions may end the tenancy immediately, and you may end it on 24 hours' written notice if you and your failure to maintain didn't cause them; you must return deposits and prorated prepaid rent within 14 days. If the unit is destroyed in a declared natural disaster (wildfire, flood, earthquake and similar events, even if begun by negligent or intentional acts), the tenancy ends immediately unless you and the tenant agree otherwise afterward: return deposits and prepaid rent (prorated from the disaster), don't charge the tenant for cleanup unless the tenant was responsible for the disaster, and let the tenant search for valuables after the emergency; no rent is owed while the unit is inaccessible or destroyed. Otherwise, an uninhabitable unit triggers the tenant's repair and essential-service remedies. A lease clause letting you end the tenancy after a casualty would add a cause the termination statute doesn't list, so the library doesn't offer one; repairs that make the unit unsafe are a qualifying landlord reason after the first year, with 90 days' notice and a relocation payment where required.",
