      "If an agency posts a unit as unsafe and unlawful to occupy, you may not continue or start a tenancy there until you correct the conditions; knowingly doing so lets the tenant leave immediately and recover two months' rent or up to twice actual damages, whichever is greater. If an agency notifies you that a unit is unlawful but safe for the existing tenant, you may not start a new tenancy until you correct it; a written pre-tenancy disclosure of the determination (with the agency's notice attached) protects you from the tenant's damages claim but not from the agency's penalties or your habitability duty, and the library doesn't offer it as a clause. You may not take a deposit to secure a rental agreement for a posted or noticed unit without that disclosure; if you knowingly do, or don't correct the conditions before the tenancy is to start, the applicant may end the agreement and recover twice the deposit. A city of more than 300,000 people may recover its tenant relocation costs from an owner whose multiple and material code violations for more than 30 days, through the owner's action or inaction, endanger tenants or the public and cause condemnation; as to a tenant's relocation costs, it is a defense that the condition was caused by that tenant's action or negligence.",
