      "Before signing a lease for premises with four or fewer units, you must disclose in writing any outstanding notice of default or trustee's sale, pending foreclosure suit, pending declaration of forfeiture or suit for specific performance of a contract of sale, or pending tax-lien foreclosure. During a foreclosure, a tenant with actual notice of it may apply the deposit or prepaid rent to its obligation to you after written notice without that being grounds for termination, and a fixed-term tenant may end the tenancy on 60 days' notice unless within 30 days you give written evidence from a lender or trustee that the property is no longer in foreclosure, or that a court has appointed a receiver. After a trustee's sale (nonjudicial foreclosure), the purchaser must give the occupants written notice of the sale containing the information the statute lists (a notice in the statutory form complies) within 30 days after the sale, and before or with any 30-day termination notice; a bona fide tenant may stay for 60 days after a written termination notice under a fixed term lease, or at least 30 days for a periodic tenancy, though a purchaser who will live there may end even a fixed term on 30 days' notice. If the purchaser accepts rent, signs a new agreement or fails to terminate the tenancy as the statute allows within 30 days after the sale, the purchaser becomes the landlord. The purchaser's responsibility for deposits follows the foreclosure statute. After an execution sale on a judgment with priority over the lease, a tenant under an unexpired lease may stay until the redemption period expires by paying the purchaser (or redemptioner) each month the greater of the value of the use and occupancy or the lease payments.",
