    bodyText:
      "Written notices under the landlord-tenant act must be served by personal delivery, by first class mail (adding three days to the notice period, and the notice must say so), or by first class mail plus attachment to the main entrance if the written rental agreement provides for it both ways. E-mail service is allowed only under a written addendum both parties sign after the tenant has moved in, listing each side's e-mail addresses, letting either side end it or change the address on three days' written notice, and containing the statute's notice about receiving legal notices by e-mail; even then, a termination notice sent by e-mail must also go by first class mail. Other methods may be used only in addition to these. 'Actual notice' (for entry, for example) may be given in person or by voicemail, by a written notice left as the statute describes, or by another method agreed and described in the written rental agreement. Days are counted as consecutive calendar days, excluding the day of service and including the last day until 11:59 p.m. Deposit refunds, utility bills and abandoned-property notices have their own delivery rules. Oregon's electronic transactions act applies only between parties who agreed to transact electronically, doesn't override a statute's required delivery method, and itself doesn't authorize electronic notice of default, eviction or a right to cure under a rental agreement for a primary residence; e-mail service rests only on the landlord-tenant act's addendum rule.",
