    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "landlord-entry",
    bodyText:
      "Except in an emergency, a written repair request, an agreed yard-work entry, an agreement with the tenant about a specific entry, a court order, abandonment, or an absence of the tenant of more than seven days (entry then only at times reasonably necessary), you must give at least 24 hours' actual notice and enter only at reasonable times. After receiving your notice, the tenant may deny consent (by actual notice, or by a written notice securely attached to the main entrance), but may not unreasonably withhold it; an unreasonable refusal lets you seek a court order or end the tenancy for cause, plus damages. After an emergency entry in the tenant's absence, give actual notice within 24 hours of the fact, date and time, nature of the emergency and the names of those who entered. Entry without notice to show the unit to a buyer requires a separate signed writing, made while you are actively trying to sell, with separate consideration. You may not abuse entry or use it to harass; unlawful or harassing entry lets the tenant seek an injunction or end the tenancy and recover at least one month's rent (one week's for week-to-week). A good-faith attempt to comply protects you if an agency requires entry and the tenant refuses.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.322(1)-(8) (agreement about a specific entry, ORS 90.322(1)(f); court order and abandonment, ORS 90.322(5)(a), (c); 'Is reflected in a writing separate from the rental agreement and signed by both parties' (ORS 90.322(1)(d)(B))); ORS 90.410(2). Rule 48: the showing agreement can't be a lease clause. Clauses: `landlords-access-or`, `yard-maintenance-entry-or`. (OR battery 137 (landlord access with tenant consent): 10 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
