    bodyText:
      "Keep the manager and owner information you disclosed current; the duty binds any successor landlord, owner or manager. Whoever holds the landlord's interest when the tenancy ends is responsible for the security deposit and prepaid rent, except a trustee's-sale purchaser, as the foreclosure statute provides. A sale doesn't end a tenancy. After the first year of occupancy you may end a month-to-month tenancy, or a fixed term at its end, for a sale only where the buyer will live in the unit as a primary residence and you document the accepted offer, with the statutory notice and payment (the one-month payment isn't required if you own four or fewer units, except on the 60-day sale option); in an owner-occupied building or property with two units or fewer, 30 days' notice is enough for such a sale (or 60 days without cause). Showing the unit to buyers needs 24 hours' actual notice and the tenant may not unreasonably withhold consent, unless the tenant signs a separate written agreement for showings without notice, made while you are actively selling and supported by separate consideration.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.305(2); ORS 90.300(2)(b) (except as provided in ORS 86.782(10)); 'A sale doesn't end a tenancy' is Claude's reading of these successor provisions and the ORS 90.427 limits; ORS 90.427(5)(a)(D), (5)(b)(B), (6)(a)-(b), (8)(a)(B)-(C); ORS 90.322(2); ORS 90.322(1)(d)(A)-(C), (1)(f). (OR battery 94 (notice to tenants of sale or new owner): 12 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
