    bodyText:
      "You may require renter's liability insurance only in a written rental agreement, only if you carry comparable liability insurance and give documentation to a tenant who asks, and only up to $100,000 per occurrence or the customary amount for similar rentals, whichever is greater. Before a new tenancy, tell the applicant in writing about the requirement, the amount and a summary of the exceptions. You may not require it of a household at or below 50% of area median income or for a unit subsidized with public funds (vouchers don't count as a subsidy). To add it to an existing month-to-month tenancy, give 30 days' written notice with the summary; a tenant who doesn't comply can be given a for-cause notice and cure by getting insurance. You may require only interested-party status, not a particular insurer, additional-insured status or a subrogation waiver, and you may claim against the policy only for damage the tenant is liable for (not wear and tear, acts of God or your conduct), only if the claim is greater than the tenant's security deposit and only with a copy to the tenant. Your written agreement must describe your own insurance requirements. Knowing violations cost actual damages or $250, whichever is greater; a frivolous claim costs actual damages plus $500.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.222(1)-(12) (ORS 90.222(5), last sentence: the agreement describes the (5) requirements; ORS 90.222(11)(a); 'A landlord may not require a tenant to obtain or maintain renter’s liability insurance if the household income of the tenant is equal to or less than 50 percent of the area median income' (ORS 90.222(8))). Screening notice ORS 90.295(3)(b)(H). Not for facility tenancies (ORS 90.222(12)(b)). Clause: `renters-insurance-or`. (OR battery 127 (insurance required of tenants): 14 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
