      "Since June 5, 2026, you may not disclose a tenant's, former tenant's, applicant's or household member's confidential information, which includes date of birth, Social Security, taxpayer or government ID numbers, phone number and e-mail, banking information, tax returns, W-2 statements, and sources of income or financial assistance, employer, immigration status or membership in a protected class, records of victim protections they used, and medical or disability records. The exceptions are narrow: the individual's separate written consent; a court order, judicial warrant or subpoena (not an administrative one); communications needed for affordable-housing compliance; screening background and credit checks; giving a phone number or e-mail to someone who needs it for repairs, maintenance or utilities; answering a reference request from a prospective landlord; and your insurance claims, collections, administrative or court actions. A knowing violation costs twice the monthly rent. Because consent must be separate, a lease clause can't supply it.",
    notes: "OR: New topic `tenant-confidential-information` (OR log §10). Or. Laws 2026, ch. 61, § 2 (HB 4123; approved March 31, 2026; effective June 5, 2026; added to ORS chapter 90): the confidential-information list, the exceptions ('separate written consent' of the individual) and the twice-monthly-rent remedy, read from the enrolled act. Rule 48: the consent must be separate, so no clause. Victims' verification information also has its own rule (ORS 90.453(6); `edu-victim-confidentiality-or`). (OR battery 109 (confidential tenant information and disclosure): 25 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
