    bodyText:
      "Oregon's Unlawful Trade Practices Act largely does not reach residential tenancy conduct: its definition of 'real estate' excludes conduct covered by ORS chapter 90. So the act is generally not a separate route against a residential landlord for tenancy conduct, although its general standard's clause on collecting or enforcing an obligation may reach rent collection, though a tenant can't bring its own suit on that clause (only on the act's listed practices); a tenant the landlord sues may be able to counterclaim; otherwise only a prosecuting attorney enforces it (whether advertising before a tenancy is 'conduct covered by ORS chapter 90' is not settled by the text); the landlord-tenant act's own remedies, fair housing law and the unlawful collection practices statute apply instead.",
    notes: "OR: '“Real estate” does not cover conduct covered by ORS chapter 90' (ORS 646.605(6)(b)(A)). Rule 51: two questions asked: an enumerated list exists (ORS 646.608) and the general standard is in the same act; the exclusion is from 'real estate' only (ORS 646.605(6)(b)(A)); whether 'services' in a tenancy, or an unconscionable tactic in 'collecting or enforcing an obligation' (ORS 646.607(1)), reaches a residential landlord is not settled by the text (Claude's reading; whether pre-tenancy advertising is 'conduct covered by ORS chapter 90' was not resolved; case law not searched). Enforcement: ORS 646.632(1) (prosecuting attorney), ORS 646.638(1) (private action only for ORS 646.608 practices), (7) (lessee counterclaim for violations of ORS 646.605 to 646.652 when the lessor sues; reach to chapter 90 tenancies unsettled given ORS 646.605(6)(b)(A); Claude's reading); ORS 646.607(6) makes an ORS 646.639 violation an unlawful trade practice. Unlawful collection practices: ORS 646.639 (no chapter 90 carve-out; `edu-collection-practices-or`). Blank spaces and copy at signing: no Oregon rule found (OR battery 58 (blank spaces in lease or contract): 16 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142); copy duty ORS 90.220(3). Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
