    bodyText:
      "In any action on a rental agreement or under the landlord-tenant act, the court may award reasonable attorney fees at trial and on appeal, with costs and disbursements, to the prevailing party (the party with the final judgment), whatever the lease says; a one-way fee clause works both ways. In an eviction, a landlord whose tenant does not contest the case doesn't get attorney fees, and some statutes deny fees in particular situations (for example a nonpayment case dismissed because the tenant tendered payment after filing, or a victim's successful defense in some cases).",
