    topicKey: "permitted-occupants",
    bodyText:
      "Oregon lets you, the tenant and a proposed occupant sign a written temporary occupancy agreement. The temporary occupant is not a tenant and has no tenant's rights; the tenant may end the agreement at any time, and you may end it only for a material violation, without a right to cure. You may screen the occupant for conduct or criminal record, but not credit or income. The agreement must expressly state that the occupant is not a tenant and has no tenant's rights and the termination rules, without a right to cure, may require compliance with the rules and may have an end date. If the occupant doesn't leave when it ends, you may give the tenant a for-cause notice (unless the victim protections apply), and an occupant who stays after the tenancy ends, or after the tenant ends the agreement, is treated as a squatter; no written termination notice to the occupant is required. You may not use it to evade your duties, and every tenancy needs at least one tenant.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.275(1)-(11) ((8) no written notice to the occupant; (10) squatter status; 'Shall expressly include the requirements of subsections (2) to (4) of this section' (ORS 90.275(6)(a))). Not offered as a clause: it is a three-party agreement separate from the lease (rule 48; Claude's call). (OR battery 131 (temporary occupants): 5 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
