      "If you materially fail to comply with the lease or the habitability duty, the tenant may give written notice ending the tenancy on a date at least 30 days later (seven days for week-to-week) unless you remedy the breach within seven days for an essential service or 30 days otherwise; a recurrence within six months allows termination on 14 days' notice. The tenant may also recover damages, but not for a condition you neither knew nor reasonably should have known of, if the tenant knew of it and failed to give you timely actual notice or it was caused after the tenancy began by the deliberate or negligent act of someone other than you or your agent. For an essential service you intentionally or negligently fail to supply, after written notice and a reasonable time the tenant may buy the service and deduct its cost, recover reduced-rental-value damages, or, if the unit is unsafe or unfit, get substitute housing and stop paying rent; if the failure poses an imminent and serious threat, the tenant may give 48 hours' notice ending the tenancy unless you remedy it within those 48 hours. For a minor defect costing $300 or less (not mold, radon, asbestos or lead-based paint), the tenant may give at least seven days' written notice and then have it repaired by someone other than the tenant (you may reasonably specify who) and deduct the cost with a written statement. In an eviction for nonpayment or an action for rent, the tenant may counterclaim if you knew or should have known of the problem before you filed, and the court may order rent paid into court. None of these remedies applies to a condition the tenant or a guest caused.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.360(1)-(5); ORS 90.365(1)-(7) ('shall not include the presence of radon, asbestos or lead-based paint or the future risk of flooding or seismic hazard' (ORS 90.365(2))); ORS 90.368(1)-(6); ORS 90.370(1)(a)-(b) (read: counterclaims and rent into court). Dormant row `habitability-timeline-or` is left switched off: its text restated these tenant remedies as a lease promise with figures that don't match Oregon's structure (OR log §5). (OR battery 138 (habitability, essential services, repair remedies): 31 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
