    bodyText:
      "If you're responsible for a utility under the lease and it's shut off for nonpayment, the tenant may pay the balance and deduct it from rent, or end the tenancy on 72 hours' actual notice unless you restore service in that time, and you must then return, within four days, rent prepaid for that month prorated from the later of termination or move-out and any other prepaid rent, and the deposit as the deposit statute requires; the tenant may also recover actual damages from the shutoff. If a tenant can't get service in the tenant's own name because of a previous tenant's or the owner's unpaid bill, the tenant may, before moving in, pay and deduct, agree a solution with you, or end the tenancy immediately; after moving in, pay and deduct or end the tenancy on 72 hours' notice, which fails if service becomes available within that time. If the tenancy ends before move-in, you must return all deposits, rent and fees within four days; if it ends after move-in, all rent and fees within four days and the deposit as the deposit statute requires, and the tenant may also recover actual damages from the shutoff. Failing to return money owed in these cases costs twice the amount. A utility company can't transfer a claim against a tenant to the property owner without the owner's written consent. A municipal utility can't transfer one unless it notified the tenant and mailed the owner or the owner's agent a copy of the delinquency notice within 30 days after the payment was due; the statute itself gives a municipal utility no authority to transfer.",
