    topicKey: "ev-charging",
    bodyText:
      "A tenant may apply to install an electric vehicle charging station for personal, noncommercial use in or near the tenant's assigned parking space or dwelling unit. You may prohibit it only if the premises don't have at least one parking space per dwelling unit; otherwise you must approve a completed application within 60 days (unless you reasonably ask for more information) and may require an application, architectural standards, reasonable review and permit charges and reasonable restrictions that don't significantly raise the cost or reduce performance. A licensed electrician (at least journeyman) must install and remove it; the tenant pays all costs, including electricity and damage; if the station's combined load requires electrical upgrades you may assess their cost to the tenants with stations; the station is the tenant's personal property unless you negotiate otherwise; and if it isn't a certified electrical product, its owner must carry renter's liability insurance of at least $100,000 covering it and name you as an additional insured.",
    notes: "OR: ORS 90.462 read whole (as noted in OR log §14). Not for facilities. `ev-charging-shared-area-co` and `ev-charging-end-of-tenancy-co` rest on the Colorado and Illinois statutes and are not tagged (rule 26); Oregon's rules on removal, damage and ownership are in this section. (OR battery 113 (electric vehicle charging): 34 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CONST 383, OAR 40864, ORS 39614, SL2026 142) Read section-open 2026-10-04 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole Oregon Revised Statutes (oregonlegislature.gov, 2025 edition, every chapter page with history lines and Notes), the Oregon Constitution, the 2026 session laws as enrolled and the Oregon Administrative Rules (secure.sos.state.or.us). Rule 15: written section-open.",
