    topicKey: "cannabis",
    bodyText:
      "Oregon's adult-use cannabis statutes expressly do not change landlord-tenant law, so a lease may restrict cannabis use and growing on the property like other conduct, and your lease's smoking policy can cover cannabis smoking (from January 1, 2027 the statutory smoking disclosure covers lit tobacco only, and a cannabis restriction added mid-tenancy that substantially modifies the bargain still needs the tenant's written consent). A tenant who wants the rental registered as a medical marijuana grow site or processing site needs the owner's notarized written consent (yours, if you own the property), and a renewal needs it again unless your earlier consent covers the renewal term (you may say how many annual terms it covers). When screening applicants you may not consider a medical marijuana card or a person's status as a patient, or convictions based only on marijuana use or possession. Making a cannabinoid extract without a license or registration is one of the 'outrageous in the extreme' acts that allow a 24-hour termination notice.",
