      "Refusing a reasonable accommodation, including an assistance animal a person with a disability needs, is housing discrimination in Oregon. You may not ask for verification when the disability and the need are obvious or already known; otherwise you may ask only for what's needed to evaluate the disability-related need. You may not charge any deposit or fee for keeping the animal. You may refuse a specific animal only if it poses a direct threat to others' health or safety that can't be eliminated or significantly reduced, based on an individualized assessment of objective evidence. The tenant remains responsible for damage, and the rule-violation fee for not cleaning up an animal's waste outside the unit can apply.",
