      "Oregon has no criminal penalty for a landlord who refuses a tenant's assistance animal; the refusal is an unlawful housing practice enforced through a civil rights complaint to the Bureau of Labor and Industries, which can assess civil penalties of up to $11,000 ($27,500 or $55,000 for repeat violators), or a lawsuit; a tenant may also raise it as a defense to a discriminatory eviction unless the tenant is in default in rent. There is no criminal penalty for the refusal itself, although willfully violating a commissioner's order is a Class A misdemeanor. Separately, it is a Class A misdemeanor to intentionally or knowingly injure, or attempt to injure, an assistance animal in the narrower sense of an individually trained dog or other animal designated by rule, or to interfere with one while it is assisting a person with a disability.",
