    id: "edu-alarm-duties-wv",
    title: "Smoke and Carbon Monoxide Detectors",
    group: "Landlord Responsibilities",
    states: ["WV"],
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "alarm-duties",
    bodyText:
      "In a one- or two-family dwelling, including a manufactured home, West Virginia requires an operational smoke detector near each sleeping area. The owner must provide, install and replace it, and where the owner does not live there the tenant must do routine maintenance on it. If a deaf or hard-of-hearing person lives in a dwelling you rent out, you must, on a written request by or for that person, install a smoke detector with a light signal; a sprinkler system installed under the State Fire Code and State Building Code may be provided instead of smoke detectors. An AC-powered carbon monoxide detector with battery backup is required in a newly built residential unit with a fuel-burning heating or cooking source, and in apartment buildings, boarding houses and one- and two-family dwellings intended to be rented, in a common area open to the public or in the sleeping rooms next to, above and below rooms with permanently installed fuel-burning appliances; one is also required in a unit connected to a newly built garage, shed or barn with a fuel-burning source, and in newly built apartment buildings and one- and two-family rentals detectors must be hardwired with battery backup. The statute does not say who maintains a carbon monoxide detector, so plan to do it yourself. A violation is a misdemeanor (fines of $250, then $750, then $2,000), and a city or county may impose greater duties. No statute sets smoke detector rules for apartment buildings or a fee for a tenant who disables a detector, so charge a tenant who damages or removes one only the actual cost of repair or replacement; building-wide rules come from the State Fire Code and State Building Code, legislative rules not read here.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 15A-10-12(a) (one- and two-family dwellings, including manufactured homes; 'in the immediate vicinity of each sleeping area'), (b) ('The owner of each dwelling described in subsection (a) of this section shall provide, install, and replace the operational smoke detectors required by this section'; where the dwelling 'is not occupied by the owner of the dwelling, the tenant in any dwelling shall perform routine maintenance on the smoke detectors within the dwelling'), (c) (light signal 'upon written request by or on behalf of the individual'), (d) (sprinkler 'in lieu of smoke detectors'), (f)(1)-(3) (carbon monoxide: newly constructed units with a fuel-burning source; units connected to a newly constructed garage, shed or barn with one; and 'all rooms in which a person will be sleeping that are adjoining to and directly below and above all areas or rooms that contain permanently installed fuel-burning appliances' in listed buildings including 'one- and two-family dwellings intended to be rented or leased'), (g) (hardwired in newly constructed apartment buildings and rental one- and two-family dwellings), (l) ('Any person who violates any provision of this section is guilty of a misdemeanor'; $250, $750, $2000), (m) (a violation 'may not be considered to constitute evidence of negligence' in a civil action), (o) (political subdivisions may impose a greater duty). Subsection (f) says detectors 'shall be installed, maintained, tested, repaired, or replaced' without naming who does it (Claude's reading: the owner should). Former § 29-3-16a prints [Repealed.]. Statutes searched (WV battery smoke-detector (smoke and carbon monoxide detectors): 3 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery a-alarm-ev (smoke or carbon monoxide detection and fire alarms generally (everyday rerun)): 3 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery a-alarm-ev-nc (smoke or carbon monoxide detection and fire alarms generally (everyday rerun) (no context limb)): 18 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205): the only other residential hit is bed and breakfast standards (§ 15A-10-14, transient lodging, out of scope). No tampering fee or tampering rule (WV battery a-alarm-tamper (tampering with or disabling smoke or carbon monoxide detectors): 0 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery a-alarm-tamper-nc (tampering with or disabling smoke or carbon monoxide detectors (no context limb)): 0 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (topic `alarm-tampering-fee` answered here). A flat tampering fee would be a charge added because of the tenant's breach, which W. Va. Code § 46A-2-128(d) bars collecting unless 'expressly authorized by the agreement creating or modifying the obligation and by statute or regulation', and no statute authorizes one; reimbursing the actual cost the lease makes the tenant's is part of the obligation (Claude's reading, the library-wide § 46A-2-128(d) reading; case law not searched). State Fire Code and State Building Code are legislative rules (W. Va. Code §§ 15A-11-3, 15A-11-5), not read; `edu-applicable-codes-wv`. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
