    bodyText:
      "No West Virginia statute lets a tenant repair and deduct, withhold rent, pay rent into escrow or end the lease because you did not make repairs, and no statute sets a notice procedure or deadline for repair requests. In an eviction for unpaid rent, though, the tenant may answer that you breached a material covenant on which the duty to pay rent depends, and the court must then hold a hearing on that issue; a tenant who asks for and gets a continuance must pay the rent coming due into court. If buildings are destroyed or damaged by fire or otherwise without the tenant's fault or negligence, rent is reasonably reduced until you rebuild unless the lease provides otherwise, and if you do not rebuild within a reasonable time the tenant may surrender possession and owe no further rent. A tenant who wins an eviction appeal after the lease term has ended gets money damages only, unless the case involves title, retaliatory eviction or breach of warranty. Other remedies for breach of your habitability duty, such as damages or rent abatement, depend on case law, which was not searched; the magistrate court's answer form lets the tenant check a defense that the premises are defective and violate the warranty of habitability. Ask tenants to put repair requests in writing and keep records.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(b) ('if the tenant files an answer raising the defense of breach by the landlord of a material covenant upon which the duty to pay rent depends, the court shall proceed to a hearing on such issues'), (d) (tenant granted a continuance pays 'any periodic rent becoming due during the period of such continuance' into court), (g) ('absent an issue of title, retaliatory eviction, or breach of warranty'); W. Va. Code § 55-3A-2 (all ejectment and unlawful detainer defenses); W. Va. Code § 37-6-28 (reasonable reduction 'unless the lease otherwise provides'; surrender after a reasonable time; `casualty-wv`); W. Va. Code § 37-6-30(c) (no repairs required while the tenant is in arrears). No repair-and-deduct, withholding or escrow statute (WV battery a-repair-deduct-r2 (tenant repair-and-deduct, rent withholding, rent escrow or abatement (rerun of repair-deduct)): 27 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery a-repair-deduct-r2-nc (tenant repair-and-deduct, rent withholding, rent escrow or abatement (rerun of repair-deduct) (no context limb)): 115 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (the first `repair-deduct` battery failed its positive and is superseded; residential hits are §§ 37-6-23, 37-6-28, 37-6-29, 55-3A-3, 8-12-16 and the manufactured-home § 55-3B-6, none a tenant self-help remedy); no repair deadline (WV battery a-repair-timeline (deadlines for a landlord to make repairs after notice): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205). Topics `repair-notice`, `designated-repairer`, `landlord-breach-remedy`, `habitability-presumption`, `rent-demand-bar`, `repair-cost-termination` and `repair-escrow-exemption-notice` answered here (no such West Virginia statutes). The court's answer form lists the defenses 'the premises are defective and violate the warranty of habitability as described below' and 'the duty to pay rent depended on an express or implied covenant or warranty described below which the landlord breached' (W. Va. Magistrate Court form MLTAWWO, revised 03/04/2026; saved sources/wv-court-forms-rules-20261005.json); the form is not a statute and the warranty's content is case law, not searched. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
