    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "disaster-duties",
    bodyText:
      "Once the Governor or the Legislature declares a state of emergency, it is unlawful in the area covered by the declaration to rent or offer rental housing leased on a month-to-month term for more than 10 percent above the price you charged on the 10th day before the declaration, unless the increase comes directly from added costs, and then only up to 10 percent above your cost plus your customary markup. The limit lasts for the whole emergency or 30 days after the declaration, whichever is longer; under a declared state of preparedness it applies only to items the proclamation names, and the Governor reviews the covered goods every 15 days. A violation is an unfair or deceptive act under the consumer protection act and a misdemeanor (fine up to $1,000, jail up to one year, or both). The statute's definition of housing names only month-to-month rentals, not week-to-week or fixed-term leases. No West Virginia statute sets other landlord duties after a disaster, such as debris removal or rent during an evacuation; the lease's casualty terms and the rent-reduction statute govern damage.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-2(g) ('Housing' means 'any rental housing leased on a month-to-month term or the sale of manufactured homes'), (k), (l) (state of emergency or preparedness declared by the Governor or the Legislature); W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-3(a) ('for a price greater than 10 percent above the price charged by that person for those goods or services on the 10th day immediately preceding the declaration'; cost proviso), (d) (preparedness: only items in the proclamation), (e) (15-day review); W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-5(a)-(c) (unfair or deceptive act; misdemeanor; cumulative); W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-6 (preempts local ordinances); W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-1 (liberal construction). Whether 'sell or offer to sell' reaches a renewal or a new month-to-month rental of the same unit, and what price applies to a unit not rented on the 10th day, is not stated (Claude's reading: treat a renewal or re-rental as covered). Not found by the `rent-control` or `rent-increase` batteries; see `edu-rent-control-wv` and `edu-rent-increase-notice-wv`. Other disaster duties: (WV battery a-disaster (declared disasters or states of emergency and housing or tenancies): 6 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits: emergency services and flood fund definitions, foreclosure of abandoned property, housing development fund; none sets a landlord duty). W. Va. Code § 37-6-28; `casualty-wv`. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
