    topicKey: "quiet-possession",
    bodyText:
      "No West Virginia statute reads a covenant of quiet enjoyment into every lease. If your lease contains one, the statute says it has the effect of a promise that a tenant who pays the rent and keeps the lease's covenants shall peaceably possess and enjoy the premises for the term without any interruption or disturbance from any person whatever; its legal scope is then decided by the rules of law and the parties' intent, which is case law that was not searched. Because those words are broad, think carefully before adding such a covenant. A tenant may enforce the lease's covenants against a later buyer of the property as against you. Whether a covenant of quiet enjoyment is implied without express words is a question of case law, which was not searched. Separately, a person turned out of possession by a forcible or unlawful entry may sue to recover possession within three years.",
    notes: "WV: A lessor's covenant for the lessee's 'quiet enjoyment of his term' has the effect of a covenant that the lessee 'paying the rent reserved, and performing his or their covenants, shall peaceably possess and enjoy the demised premises, for the term granted, without any interruption or disturbance from any person whatever' (W. Va. Code § 36-4-14); scope by the plain intent of the parties: 'The legal scope and effect of the covenants mentioned in this article, and the person or persons by and against whom such covenants may be enforced, shall be determined according to the rules of law applicable to such cases, and the plain intent and meaning of the parties' (W. Va. Code § 36-4-17); so whether the covenant reaches, for example, wrongful acts of strangers is case law, not searched. Against a transferee: W. Va. Code § 37-6-2. Forcible or unlawful entry: W. Va. Code § 55-3-1 ('the party so turned out of possession, no matter what right or title he had thereto' may sue within three years). Searched (WV battery a-quiet (covenant of quiet enjoyment or quiet possession): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery a-quiet-nc (covenant of quiet enjoyment or quiet possession (no context limb)): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits: §§ 36-4-5 deeds, 36-4-14, UCC § 46-9-610, manufactured-home § 55-3B-6). No lease clause in the library promises quiet enjoyment for West Virginia; proposal A-3 recommends not adding one. Self-help lockouts and utility shutoffs: `edu-self-help-eviction-wv`. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
