      "West Virginia has no general statute barring a residential landlord from raising rent, cutting services or evicting because a tenant complained about conditions or reported code violations. The eviction statute mentions retaliatory eviction only on appeal: a tenant who wins an appeal after the lease term has ended gets money damages, not possession, unless the case involves title, retaliatory eviction or breach of warranty. Fair housing law makes it unlawful to coerce, intimidate, threaten or interfere with anyone for exercising fair housing rights or helping someone else do so. The magistrate court's answer form for these eviction cases lets the tenant check a defense that the action was filed in retaliation for complaints the tenant made about the premises. How courts treat that defense is a question of case law, which was not searched, so document an independent reason for any action you take soon after a tenant complains.",
