    bodyText:
      "Cities and counties may adopt ordinances to make owners repair, close or demolish dwellings that are unsafe, unsanitary or unfit for human habitation, charge the costs to the owner, and record them as a lien on the property. A city's code enforcement officials must give the owner reasonable advance notice and ask permission before inspecting, except in exigent circumstances; if the owner cannot be found or refuses, they may get an administrative search warrant, a copy of which goes to the owner and any tenant in possession five days before entry. A city may let its municipal court place a structure in receivership where the owner cannot be found or refuses entry and the building is salvageable but needs prompt repair, and a city may charge a registration fee on uninhabitable property that violates its building code. No West Virginia statute gives tenants a specific right to have a receiver appointed or to pay rent into escrow for repairs of an occupied rental; the general equity receivership statute is not tenant-specific, and whether tenants could use it is case law that was not searched.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 8-12-16(a)(4)(I), (b), (e)(1)-(3), (g)(1)(A)-(D), (2) (receivership; owner may petition to end it); W. Va. Code § 7-1-3ff(a) (county ordinances); W. Va. Code § 8-12-16a(a), (n) (uninhabitable property registration fee; forfeiture after two years delinquent); W. Va. Code § 15A-10-9(b) (State Fire Marshal orders and lien). General receivers: W. Va. Code § 53-6-1 (notice to 'the owner or tenant' before a receiver of real estate is appointed); the commercial real estate receivership article reaches property with one to four dwelling units where 'The owner is collecting or has the right to collect rents or other income from the property from a person other than an affiliate of the owner' (W. Va. Code § 55-21-4(b)(4)), for example on a mortgagee's request (W. Va. Code § 55-21-6(b)). No tenant receivership or rent escrow (WV battery a-receivership (receivership, rent escrow or conservatorship for substandard rental buildings): 39 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits are bond, banking, conservatorship and the sections above) (WV battery a-repair-deduct-r2 (tenant repair-and-deduct, rent withholding, rent escrow or abatement (rerun of repair-deduct)): 27 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery a-repair-deduct-r2-nc (tenant repair-and-deduct, rent withholding, rent escrow or abatement (rerun of repair-deduct) (no context limb)): 115 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
