    bodyText:
      "West Virginia's deposit law says a landlord may use submetering equipment, energy allocation equipment or a ratio utility billing system if the rental agreement so provides, so put any such arrangement, and how charges are calculated, in the lease. No statute sets how a shared bill must be split, caps administrative fees, or requires a particular disclosure form. At the end of the tenancy you may apply the security deposit to utilities that were billed to and paid by you and that the lease makes the tenant's obligation. Whether a landlord who bills tenants for utilities is regulated by the Public Service Commission depends on commission rules and decisions, which were not read.",
    notes: "WV: 'If the rental agreement so provides, a landlord may use submetering equipment or energy allocation equipment, or a ratio utility billing system' (W. Va. Code § 37-6A-1(17)); the sentence sits in the deposit article's definitions ('When used in this article'), so its reach outside art. 37-6A is unsettled (Claude's reading). Deposit: W. Va. Code § 37-6A-2(b)(3). No allocation method, fee or disclosure statute (WV battery utility-billing (submetering, allocation or resale of utilities to tenants): 5 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits: tax and electric-rate sections and § 37-6A-1). 'Public utility' (W. Va. Code § 24-1-2) does not address landlords; PSC rules not read. Lead's open utility-billing clause candidate; proposal A-1. Topics `utility-allowance-cap` and `utility-disclosure-attachment` answered here. Stormwater providers, not landlords, must give a tenant a report of the stormwater fee for the whole property and the tenant's portion (W. Va. Code §§ 8-20-10(a)(3), 16-13-16(c), 16-13A-9(e)). Topic `utility-submetering-disclosure`: `utility-billing-wv`. Utility charges, and any billing fee, that the lease makes the tenant's obligation are part of the obligation, not charges 'incidental to the principal obligation' under W. Va. Code § 46A-2-128(d); a late or collection charge added on unpaid utility bills would be incidental and needs statutory authority; whether the late charge for 'rent' in W. Va. Code § 37-6A-2(b)(1) reaches utility charges, which fall within the deposit article's broad definition of rent (W. Va. Code § 37-6A-1(11)), is unsettled (Claude's reading, the library-wide reading; case law not searched). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
