    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "eviction-process",
    bodyText:
      "Most West Virginia evictions use the summary procedure for wrongful occupation of residential rental property. The owner or the owner's agent files a verified (sworn) petition in the magistrate court or circuit court of the county where the property is, describing the property and the unpaid rent, lease breach or damage relied on; magistrate court can hear it so long as title to the property is not in dispute. The court sets a hearing 5 to 10 judicial days after filing, and you must immediately have notice of the hearing served on the tenant, under the civil service rule (the court's summons form lists the sheriff, a private process server, or certified mail) or by certified mail, return receipt requested, and then file the petition and proof of service. The tenant may serve a written defense within five days of receiving the notice and may raise any defense available in an ejectment or unlawful detainer case, and either side may elect a jury by giving the court written notice within five days after service. If the tenant has filed nothing by the hearing, the court orders immediate possession; otherwise it hears the case. The court may also enter judgment for rent due, and its order sets the date and time to vacate, after which the sheriff removes the tenant. Either party may appeal a magistrate court judgment to circuit court within 20 days by posting a bond, unless excused as unable to pay. Holding over after the lease ends is not one of the listed grounds for the summary petition; unlawful detainer is the procedure written for it (see the holdover row), and the squatter-removal law cannot be used against a current or former tenant.",
    notes: "WV: Petition: W. Va. Code § 55-3A-1(a) ('by verified petition'; owner or agent; description; grounds; prayer for possession), (b) ('not be less than five nor more than 10 judicial days following the filing'), (c) (notice served 'Immediately upon being apprised of the time and place for hearing' under Rule 4 of the West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure 'or by certified mail, return receipt requested'; written defense 'within five days of the receipt by the tenant of the notice'; file petition and proof of service on the return). Defenses: W. Va. Code § 55-3A-2. Default, hearing, continuance, order: W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(a)-(f) (sheriff 'shall forthwith remove the tenant, taking precautions to guard against damage to the property of the landlord and the tenant'). Rent: W. Va. Code § 50-4-5 (in any case involving possession of rental property a party may 'plead, prove and obtain judgment for all rent due and owing'); § 55-3A-3(e). Jurisdiction: W. Va. Code § 50-2-1 ('so long as the title to such property is not in dispute'). Removal to circuit court at $2,500 or more: W. Va. Code § 50-4-8. Jury: Magistrate Court Civil Rule 6A(b)(2) (5 days after service in expedited proceedings such as wrongful occupation; saved rules) and the summons form MLTSMWO (rev. 01/12/2026); answer within 5 days, Rule 4(b)(4). Statute and rule differ on the jury deadline (W. Va. Code § 50-5-8(a): 20 days after the first answer) and on the appeal bond (W. Va. Code § 50-5-12(a): not less than appeal costs nor more than judgment plus costs; Rule 18(b): not less than judgment plus costs) (WV log §10). Appeal: W. Va. Code § 50-5-12(a) (20 days; bond; no bond for a person permitted to proceed without prepayment; appeal stays enforcement); W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(g). Squatters: W. Va. Code § 55-3C-2(c) (squatters not entitled to eviction proceedings), § 55-3C-3(a)(6) (law enforcement removal unavailable against 'current or former lawful tenants'). Rule 39 screen of arts. 55-3A, 55-3, 50-4, 50-5 and the saved Magistrate Court Civil Rules: landlord duties are the verified petition, immediate service and filing of proof (§ 55-3A-1(a), (c)) and an affidavit or sworn testimony for a default judgment (W. Va. Code § 50-4-10(a)(1); Rule 10(a)); limits on default against minors and servicemembers are in `edu-minor-defendants-wv` and `edu-servicemember-rights-wv`; the only landlord immunity is for disposing of belongings under § 55-3A-3(h) (`edu-post-eviction-property-wv`); no prohibition on landlord conduct found beyond the court process itself ((WV battery self-help (lockouts, utility shutoffs or self-help by landlords): 9 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-lockout-r2 (landlord lock changes, removal of doors or cutting off services to a tenant): 3 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205); `edu-self-help-eviction-wv`). Forms saved: MLTPTWR petition, MLTSMWO summons, MLTAWWO answer (defenses listed: habitability, retaliation, breach of a covenant on which rent depends). Holdover: `edu-holdover-wv`. Stays: `edu-eviction-hardship-stay-wv`. Federal: `edu-cares-act-notice`. Case law not searched. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
