    topicKey: "eviction-hardship-stay",
    bodyText:
      "No West Virginia statute lets a court postpone an eviction because of the tenant's hardship, the weather or the season. In a summary eviction, though, the court's order sets the time by which the tenant must vacate, taking into account whether the property is furnished or unfurnished, the possible relative harm to the parties and other relevant facts, and only after that time does the sheriff remove the tenant. Continuances are for cause only, and a tenant who gets one must pay into court the rent that comes due meanwhile. A party may appeal a magistrate court judgment to circuit court within 20 days, or ask the magistrate within 20 days to set it aside, and a timely appeal or motion stays enforcement; but the summary eviction statute says a tenant is not entitled to remain in possession during an appeal if the tenancy has otherwise expired. Expect delay when a tenant appeals, and get advice on how the two rules apply.",
    notes: "WV: Confirmed absent (hardship or weather stay): (WV battery b-hardship-stay (stay or postponement of an eviction for hardship or weather): 0 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-hardship-stay-nc (stay or postponement of an eviction for hardship or weather (no context limb)): 0 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-hardship-ev (hardship, extreme weather or freezing (everyday rerun)): 8 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-hardship-ev-nc (hardship, extreme weather or freezing (everyday rerun) (no context limb)): 131 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits concern taxes, garnishment, wells and other subjects). Vacate time: the order 'shall specify the time when the tenant shall vacate the property, taking into consideration such factors as the nature of the property (i.e., furnished or unfurnished), the possibility of relative harm to the parties, and other material facts' (W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(f)). Continuance: W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(d). Appeal: W. Va. Code § 50-5-12(a) ('The filing or granting of an appeal shall automatically stay further proceedings to enforce the judgment.'); Magistrate Court Civil Rules 17(a) and 18A (saved rules: a timely appeal or motion to set aside stays execution). Conflict (rule 31): 'During the pendency of any such appeal, the tenant is not entitled to remain in possession of the property if the period of the tenancy has otherwise expired.' (W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(g)); how the specific summary-relief sentence and the general automatic stay fit together is case law, not searched. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
