    bodyText:
      "West Virginia's landlord-tenant statutes do not expressly ban lockouts or utility shutoffs by a residential landlord (its rules for manufactured-home lot rentals do), but the lawful ways to recover possession are a court case or, for a tenant who abandons owing rent, the posted-notice procedure. A court order for possession is carried out by the sheriff, and goods can be seized for rent (distress) only by the sheriff or a deputy sheriff under a magistrate's warrant. A person forcibly or unlawfully turned out of possession may sue within three years to recover it, without having to prove title, and recover damages. For units on direct public utility connections you must supply running water, reasonable hot water at all times and reasonable heat from October 1 through the last day of April, unless the tenant controls the installation. Do not change the locks, remove doors or belongings, or cut off utilities to force a tenant out; what else such conduct may expose you to is a question of case law.",
    notes: "WV: No express ban for dwellings: (WV battery self-help (lockouts, utility shutoffs or self-help by landlords): 9 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery self-help-nc (lockouts, utility shutoffs or self-help by landlords (no context limb)): 58 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-lockout-r2 (landlord lock changes, removal of doors or cutting off services to a tenant): 3 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-lockout-r2-nc (landlord lock changes, removal of doors or cutting off services to a tenant (no context limb)): 7 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (the only landlord-tenant hit is the lot-rental rule: 'A landlord may not cause the eviction of a tenant by willfully interrupting gas, electricity, water or any other essential service, or by removal of the factory-built home from the factory-built home site, or by any other willful self-help measure.' (W. Va. Code § 37-15-6(d)), out of scope). Possession: W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(f) (sheriff removes); W. Va. Code § 37-6-6(a) (abandonment). Distress by 'any sheriff or constable' under 'a warrant from a justice' (W. Va. Code § 37-6-12); on and after January 1, 1977 'the office of constable shall be abolished, and the duties previously performed by constables shall be assumed by deputy sheriffs or by such other persons as elsewhere provided in this code', and 'justice' in that sense means magistrate (W. Va. Code § 50-1-17); consistent with `edu-landlord-lien-wv` (sheriff or deputy sheriff). Forcible or unlawful entry: 'the party so turned out of possession, no matter what right or title he had thereto' may sue 'within three years' (W. Va. Code § 55-3-1); damages (W. Va. Code § 55-3-2). Utilities: W. Va. Code § 37-6-30(a)(7). Squatter removal is unavailable against current or former lawful tenants (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-3(a)(6)). Topic `lockout-for-rent-delinquency` (TX) answered here. Tort exposure: case law not searched. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
