    bodyText:
      "After a court grants you possession in a summary eviction, if the tenant has not removed all personal property by the date and time the order sets, you may: dispose of it without liability if the tenant tells you in writing that it is abandoned or if it is garbage; remove and store it, and dispose of it without liability after 30 days if the tenant has not paid your reasonable storage and removal costs and taken it back, or if the storage costs equal its value; or leave it on the property and dispose of it after 30 days if the tenant has not paid your reasonable costs of leaving it there and taken it back. If property worth more than $300 was not removed within those 30 days with the costs paid, and the tenant or someone holding a security interest in it tells you they intend to remove it, you must store it for up to 30 more days, provided they pay your reasonable storage and removal costs. You may apply the security deposit to reasonable removal and storage costs. The statute requires no separate notice before these steps and has no rule for pets or other animals left behind, and it does not cover belongings left after an unlawful detainer judgment; giving the tenant written notice and a reasonable chance to collect belongings, and keeping records, is the safe course.",
