    bodyText:
      "West Virginia nuisance laws can reach a property owner whose rental is used for crime. A place where lewdness, assignation or prostitution is conducted or permitted is a nuisance: the owner, agent or lessee can be enjoined as maintaining it, and the court may close the place for a year; an owner may ask the court to release it by paying the costs and posting a bond for the property's full value to abate the nuisance, and the court may release it if satisfied of the owner's good faith, lack of knowledge of the use, and that reasonable care would not have revealed it. A place where alcoholic liquor is unlawfully made, sold or stored is a public nuisance that can be abated or closed. A building used by members of an organized criminal enterprise for qualifying offenses is a nuisance, and a court may order the owner or lessee to evict the persons using it and seal the premises. If law enforcement notifies you that a clandestine drug laboratory was found on your residential property, you must keep it unoccupied until the state certifies the remediation, or demolish it. Outside these proceedings, removing a tenant for criminal activity goes through the ordinary eviction process.",
    notes: "WV: Lewdness nuisance: W. Va. Code § 61-9-1 (definition), § 61-9-2 ('the owner, agent, or lessee of any interest in any such nuisance' is 'guilty of maintaining a nuisance and shall be enjoined'), § 61-9-4 (closing order; release is discretionary: on payment of costs and a bond 'in the amount of the full value of the property', the court 'may deliver such property to the owner thereof' if satisfied 'of the good faith of the owner' and that 'with reasonable care and diligence such owner could not have known thereof'), § 61-9-6 (closing for one year). Alcohol: W. Va. Code §§ 60-6-16, 60-6-17. Organized criminal enterprise: W. Va. Code § 61-13-4(b) ('the court may order the owner of record or the lessee of the premises to remove or evict the persons from the premises and order the premises sealed'). Drug laboratory: W. Va. Code § 60A-11-5(a) (owner keeps the property unoccupied until certification; may demolish instead), (b)-(c) (immunity after certified remediation for an owner who neither knew nor should have known). The paraphernalia nuisance section, § 60A-4-403a, prints [Repealed.]. Screens: (WV battery nuisance-abatement (public nuisance abatement against property used for drugs or crime): 19 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-nuisance-lease (nuisance law reaching leases, lessees or owners): 2 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-nuisance-lease-nc (nuisance law reaching leases, lessees or owners (no context limb)): 12 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery criminal-activity (criminal activity or drugs as grounds against a tenant): 8 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205). Drug forfeiture: `edu-no-expedited-criminal-eviction-wv`. Local nuisance ordinances not searched. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
