      "No West Virginia statute lets a tenant protected by a domestic violence order change the locks, or requires you to change them or to refuse a key to the person restrained. A protective order may grant the petitioner possession of the home the parties shared when the abuse occurred and order the respondent to stay away from the petitioner's residence; if a tenant shows you such an order, follow it, and get legal advice before giving a key or access to a person the order excludes, even one named on the lease.",
