    topicKey: "guarantor-renewal",
    bodyText:
      "A guaranty of a tenant's lease must be in writing and signed by the guarantor (or the guarantor's agent) to be enforced. Once the tenant is in default, a guarantor may give you written notice to sue; if you do not, within a reasonable time, sue every party to the lease who lives in West Virginia and is not insolvent and pursue the case diligently to judgment and execution, you lose your claim against the guarantor. A judgment against the tenant does not bind a guarantor who was not made a party and served, so name the guarantor in your suit. No West Virginia statute says whether a guaranty covers a renewal or extension of the lease; that depends on the guaranty's own words, so say in it whether renewals are covered. The consumer-credit cosigner notice rules apply to leases of goods, not to dwelling rentals. A guaranty is lawful; this library has no guaranty clause, because a guaranty is the guarantor's own contract, and a landlord who wants one can add their own after taking advice.",
