    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "tenant-death",
    bodyText:
      "When a tenant under a residential lease dies, an heir, personal representative, devisee or assignee of the tenant may end the lease before it expires by notice to you, hand-delivered or mailed postage prepaid. The termination takes effect on the last day of the calendar month that is two months after the notice is hand-delivered or postmarked: for example, notice mailed March 10 ends the lease on May 31. The tenant's estate still owes rent and other sums due before and during that two-month notice period, and the cost of restoring the property to its condition at the start of the tenancy, less ordinary wear and tear. No one can waive this right by contract or otherwise, and a lease term requiring longer notice is void. The statute applies to residential leases entered into or renewed on or after July 1, 2012; it does not say how it reaches a month-to-month tenancy that began earlier, so honor it for every tenancy. Otherwise rent may be recovered from the tenant's heirs or personal representative as with other debts. No statute sets a procedure for a deceased tenant's belongings; work with the personal representative or heirs and keep records.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 37-6-11(b)(1) ('Notwithstanding any other provision of this code to the contrary'; heir, personal representative, devisee or assignee), (2) ('shall become effective on the last day of the calendar month that is two months after' hand delivery or the postmarked mailing), (3) (rent 'prior to or during the two month written notice period' and restoration, 'ordinary wear and tear excepted'), (4) ('may not be waived by a lessor, lessee or lessee's heir, personal representative, devisee or assignee, by contract or otherwise'; longer lease notice 'void and unenforceable'), (5) (leases 'entered into or renewed on or after July 1, 2012'); W. Va. Code § 37-6-11(a) (liability of heirs and personal representatives). The March 10 to May 31 example is Claude's reading of (b)(2). Rule 37: how (b)(5) reaches an older periodic tenancy is unsettled (case law not searched); the body says to follow it for every tenancy. Whether one co-tenant's death lets the estate end the whole lease is not addressed (Claude's reading). Belongings: (WV battery b-death-property (belongings of a deceased tenant): 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery b-death-property-nc (belongings of a deceased tenant (no context limb)): 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery tenant-death (death of a tenant and the lease): 17 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery tenant-death-nc (death of a tenant and the lease (no context limb)): 24 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205). Shared `early-termination-ks` saves any death-termination limit the law imposes, consistent with (b)(4). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
