      "If a law-enforcement agency notifies you that chemicals, equipment, supplies or precursors indicating a clandestine drug laboratory were found on your residential property, you are responsible for meeting the Department of Health's remediation standards (or you may demolish the building instead), and you must keep the property unoccupied from that notice until the Department certifies that the remediation meets its decontamination levels. The statute also directs the Department to set, by legislative rule, requirements for owners, sellers and landlords to disclose any former clandestine laboratory site or activity to any potential occupant. Those rules are not covered here: before renting a property with that history, check the current rule and give the disclosure in writing before the tenant signs. Once remediation is certified, an owner who neither knew nor should have known of the illegal use is immune from suits by later renters based on that prior use, and the same immunity applies to such an owner who completes certified remediation voluntarily before any notice. A person convicted of possessing precursors to make methamphetamine whose actions made remediation necessary is liable for its costs, including reasonable attorney's fees and court costs to collect them.",
