    topicKey: "required-disclosures",
    bodyText:
      "West Virginia requires few disclosures to residential tenants. Federal lead-based paint disclosure applies to pre-1978 housing, and the Department of Health's rules govern disclosure of a former clandestine drug laboratory. The written lease must be clear and coherent, in everyday words, in easily readable type with contrasting ink, and organized and captioned by section; a violation does not void the lease, but a tenant may demand a conforming lease and sue to reform it, and cannot waive that right. Submetering or utility allocation must be provided for in the rental agreement, and a pet or application fee is nonrefundable only if agreed in writing. A real estate licensee must disclose in writing, on the Real Estate Commission's form, whom the licensee represents. A declarant converting a building must give tenants a conversion notice, and on request you must sign a rent statement for a senior tax relief claimant. No statute requires an owner-identity notice, a move-in checklist or a tenant-rights summary, but a managing agent who does not disclose the owner's name is treated as the landlord under the deposit law.",
