    topicKey: "tenant-confidential-information",
    bodyText:
      "No West Virginia statute specifically limits what a landlord may disclose about tenants or applicants. But if you keep tenants' or applicants' names linked to Social Security numbers, driver's license or state ID numbers, or financial account or card numbers (with any code needed for access) in unencrypted, unredacted computerized form, and an unauthorized person accesses and acquires that data in a way you reasonably believe has caused or will cause identity theft or fraud, you must notify each affected West Virginia resident without unreasonable delay. The notice must describe the kinds of information taken, give a phone number or website for questions, and give the major credit bureaus' contact information and how to place a fraud alert or security freeze; if you must notify more than 1,000 people, you must also notify the nationwide consumer reporting agencies. A vendor holding your tenants' data must tell you of a breach. Only the Attorney General enforces this, and civil penalties (up to $150,000 per breach) apply only to repeated and willful violations.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 46A-2A-101(1), (6) (personal information), (7) (notice methods), (8); § 46A-2A-102(a)-(d), (f) ('more than one thousand persons'); § 46A-2A-103 (own procedures); § 46A-2A-104(a)-(b) ('No civil penalty shall exceed $150,000 per breach of security of the system'). Landlords and managers 'owns or licenses computerized data' (§ 46A-2A-102(a)) when they keep application files electronically: Claude's reading. No tenant-information disclosure statute: (WV battery d-tenant-confid (confidentiality of tenant or applicant personal information): 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery tenant-privacy (tenant personal information, privacy or data): 8 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits read: licensing, voter and family records; none governs landlords). Oregon's tenant-confidential-information program has no West Virginia counterpart. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
