    topicKey: "tenant-screening",
    bodyText:
      "No West Virginia statute sets criminal-history look-back periods, income tests, or rules for using credit or eviction reports in tenant screening, and none protects applicants because of a housing voucher or other source of income. The West Virginia Fair Housing Act bars refusing to rent, setting different terms, or advertising a preference because of race, color, religion, ancestry, sex, familial status, blindness, disability or national origin. It does not bar refusing a person convicted of illegally manufacturing or distributing a controlled substance, and it does not require renting to a person whose tenancy would be a direct threat to others' health or safety or would cause substantial physical damage to others' property (you must prove it). An owner-occupied rooming house with no more than four rented rooms is largely exempt, and some small owners are exempt as to familial status only. No West Virginia statute bars or requires asking an applicant for a Social Security number; if you ask, ask every applicant the same way (national origin is a protected class), and if you keep Social Security numbers electronically, the data-breach notice duties apply (see Tenant Information and Data Breaches). Apply written criteria to every applicant the same way; federal law, including the Fair Credit Reporting Act, was not read.",
