    topicKey: "notice-delivery-methods",
    bodyText:
      "West Virginia has no general rule for delivering landlord-tenant notices; each statute sets its own method, and this library's Notices clause defers to those methods rather than replacing them. A notice ending a year-to-year tenancy must be in writing; for a shorter periodic tenancy the statute calls for \"like notice\" or notice for one full period, which is best read as written notice too, so give it in writing. Either way, yours may be served on the tenant or on anyone holding the premises under the tenant, and the tenant's on any owner or the owner's agent. To recover possession of abandoned property you post a written notice on the property; to dispose of belongings you also mail notice first-class with a certificate of mailing, in an envelope marked \"Please Forward\", to the property, any known post office box and any forwarding address. The deposit refund and itemization may be delivered personally or mailed to the tenant's last known or forwarding address. An eviction hearing notice is served under the civil procedure rules or by certified mail, return receipt requested. Default, eviction and right-to-cure notices for a primary residence are outside the electronic transactions act, so deliver them on paper; other notices may go by e-mail only if both sides agreed to deal electronically and no statute requires a particular method, such as posting or mail. If you know an electronic notice did not reach the tenant, try again at another e-mail or the postal address on file.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 37-6-5 ('notice in writing' for year-to-year; 'may be terminated by like notice, or by notice for one full period before the end of any period' for shorter periods; that 'like notice' means written notice is Claude's reading; 'it may be served upon him or upon anyone holding under him the leased premises'); § 37-6-6(a), (c)(1)-(2); § 37-6A-2(g); § 37-6-11(b)(2) (heir's notice: hand delivery or mailing); W. Va. Code § 55-3A-1(c) ('Rule 4 of the West Virginia Rules of Civil Procedure or by certified mail, return receipt requested'); W. Va. Code § 39A-2-11(2)(B); § 39A-1-5(b); § 39A-1-8(b)(2), (d)(2) (mail methods varied only 'to the extent permitted by the other law'); consumer transactions W. Va. Code § 46A-6I-3 (redelivery), § 46A-6I-2 (consumer may respond by the same method), § 39A-1-8(b)(2) ('must be sent, communicated or transmitted by the method specified in the other law'), § 46A-6I-6 (not variable); reach of art. 46A-6I to leases is Claude's reading of § 46A-6I-1(b). Trustee-sale purchasers: § 38-1-16(c). (WV battery notice-method (how notices to tenants or landlords are delivered): 68 hits, control 0; known positives passed (3 real sections, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (68 hits read: the sections above plus §§ 37-6-11, 38-1-16, 36B-4-112; the rest municipal, mining, licensing). Shared `notices` tagged; topics `actual-notice-method` and `written-notice-required` answered here. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
