    topicKey: "sale-or-management-change",
    bodyText:
      "When rented property is sold, inherited or assigned, the new owner can enforce the lease's conditions and covenants as you could, and the tenant has the same benefit of the lease's conditions, covenants and promises against the new owner as against you (except any warranty); the transfer is effective without the tenant's attornment (acknowledgment). A tenant who pays rent to you before notice of the transfer is protected, so give the tenant written notice of the sale and where to pay. Whoever holds the landlord's interest when the tenancy ends must return any deposit owed, whether or not the deposit was handed over. No statute says when the seller's own liability ends or requires notice of a change of manager, but a managing agent who does not disclose the owner's name is treated as the landlord under the deposit law. Converting a rental building to a condominium or other common interest community gives residential tenants 120 days' notice and a 60-day option to buy, and a buyer at a trustee's sale under a deed of trust has its own notice rules (see Tenants After a Trustee's Sale).",
