      "No West Virginia statute requires mediation, arbitration or a pre-suit meeting before a landlord-tenant case. Under West Virginia's Revised Uniform Arbitration Act, an agreement in a record made on or after July 1, 2015 to arbitrate existing or future disputes is valid, enforceable and irrevocable except on grounds for revoking any contract; a court that orders arbitration must stay a related court case; and some of the Act's protections, such as the right to be represented by a lawyer, cannot be waived before a dispute arises. Whether an arbitration clause can stop a residential eviction case, or is enforceable in a residential lease at all, is case law not checked here (federal arbitration law was not read). This library does not offer an arbitration or mediation clause: it could delay the eviction hearing, which is held 5 to 10 judicial days after filing.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 55-10-8(a) ('An agreement contained in a record to submit to arbitration any existing or subsequent controversy arising between the parties to the agreement is valid, enforceable and irrevocable except upon a ground that exists at law or in equity for the revocation of a contract'); § 55-10-5(a); § 55-10-6(b)-(c); § 55-10-9(f)-(g); W. Va. Code § 55-3A-1(b). (WV battery d-arbitration (arbitration or mediation near lease, tenant or consumer words): 17 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (17 hits read: § 46A-6E-6 motor vehicle warranties; §§ 55-3A-1, -3 match on 'rental' only; none requires or regulates landlord-tenant ADR). Rule 54: declined (WV log §6.1). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
