    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "municipal-utility-lien",
    bodyText:
      "Whether a tenant's unpaid water, sewer or stormwater bill can be charged to you or become a lien on your rental depends on which West Virginia statute the utility operates under. For a city waterworks, a city sewer service where another provider supplies the water, a combined city water and sewer system, and a public service district, the statute says an owner may not be held liable for a tenant's delinquent charges, and no lien attaches to the property for them, unless the owner contracted directly with the utility for the service. Other statutes, for city sewerage and stormwater works, other city public works (only if the city's ordinance so provides), and the general section on water shutoffs for unpaid sewer and stormwater bills, which covers privately owned and homeowners' association utilities among others, make unpaid charges a lien on the premises served with no exception for a tenant's bill; city sewerage charges are payable by the property owner, and city stormwater charges by the owner or any user. Before relying on a tenant's own account, ask the utility which law it bills under and whether a tenant's unpaid bill can become a lien. A city may not have a lien for unpaid garbage collection fees, but may adopt an ordinance allowing liens for unpaid fire, police or street fees after at least 90 days' certified-mail notice to the owner.",
    notes: "WV: Owner protected: W. Va. Code § 8-19-12a(b) (municipal waterworks: 'an owner of real property may not be held liable for the delinquent rates or charges for services or facilities of a tenant, nor shall any lien attach to real property for the reason of delinquent rates or charges for services or facilities of a tenant of such real property, unless the owner has contracted directly with the municipality to purchase such services or facilities' (W. Va. Code § 8-19-12a(b))); W. Va. Code § 8-18-23(a), (c) (municipal sewer where another provider supplies water; same proviso; lien when notice is recorded with the county commission clerk); W. Va. Code § 8-20-10(c) (combined systems; same proviso); W. Va. Code § 16-13A-9(f) (public service districts; 'unless the owner has contracted directly with the public service district to purchase the services or facilities' (W. Va. Code § 16-13A-9(f))). No tenant proviso: W. Va. Code § 16-13-16(a)(1)-(2) (sewerage and stormwater charges 'to be paid by the owner of each lot, parcel of real estate or building' (W. Va. Code § 16-13-16(a)(1))); W. Va. Code § 16-13-16a(e) ('All rates, fees, or charges, if not paid when due, shall constitute a lien upon the premises served by the works' (W. Va. Code § 16-13-16a(e))); W. Va. Code § 8-16-18 (municipal public works; charges 'may, if the governing body so provide in the ordinance provided for under §8-16-7 of this code, constitute a lien upon the premises served by such works' (W. Va. Code § 8-16-18)); W. Va. Code § 16-13-16(a)(2) (stormwater: the owner 'or any user of such stormwater works' (W. Va. Code § 16-13-16(a)(2))); W. Va. Code § 24-3-10(h) (lien 'upon the premises served by the works' (W. Va. Code § 24-3-10(h)), recoverable by the provider, in a section on water shutoffs for sewer and stormwater bills whose subsections name publicly and privately owned utilities, municipalities, public service districts and homeowners association utilities; its exact reach is Claude's reading). Rule 31: § 8-19-12a(a)(1) says 'the owner, user and property shall be held liable at law' (W. Va. Code § 8-19-12a(a)(1)) for water charges unpaid 20 days, while subsection (b) bars owner liability for a tenant's charges; § 16-13A-9(j) makes all charges a lien on the premises without repeating the subsection (f) proviso (Claude's reading: the specific tenant provisos control; case law not searched). Which statute a given utility operates under is a fact question for the landlord (Claude's reading). Deposits from new applicants, held for a tenant until the tenant discontinues service: W. Va. Code §§ 8-19-12a(a)(2), 8-20-10(a)(4), 16-13-16(d), 16-13A-9(a)(3). Garbage, fire, police and street fees: 'A municipality may not have a lien on any property as security for payments due under subsection (a) of this section except as provided in subsection (e) of this section' (W. Va. Code § 8-13-13(d)); W. Va. Code § 8-13-13(e) (ordinance; notice to the property owner by certified mail at least 90 days before filing; appeal to circuit court). Statutes searched (WV battery utility-liens (tenant utility bills, owner liability and liens): 10 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery e-utility-lien (owner liability and liens for a tenant's utility bills (water, sewer, stormwater, gas)): 13 hits, control 0; known positives passed (6 real sections, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits beyond those cited: urban renewal, dealer recovery funds, abandoned aircraft; none on point). Public Service Commission rules and local ordinances not read. `edu-utility-landlord-account-wv`; `edu-government-fees-wv`. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
