    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "unauthorized-occupant-removal",
    bodyText:
      "Since July 10, 2025, West Virginia's Stop Squatters Act lets a property owner or authorized agent ask a law enforcement officer to remove a squatter from a residential dwelling without going to court, by submitting a completed and verified complaint, if: the person is unlawfully occupying the property; the property was not open to the public when the person entered; you told the person to leave; the person is not a current or former owner or lawful tenant, or an immediate family member of the owner or tenants; and no litigation about the property is pending between you and the person. After preliminary fact-finding, the agency serves a notice to vacate immediately, puts you in possession, and on request removes a person who does not leave; it may charge a fee for serving the notice. The Act defines a squatter as someone unlawfully occupying who is not entitled under a rental or lease agreement or is not authorized by the tenant or owner to be there, and police removal is only for an unauthorized person who is not a current or former lawful tenant, so it does not reach a guest the tenant let in or a current or former tenant, including one holding over; for them, use the court eviction process. A bad-faith complaint can be prosecuted as false swearing, and a person wrongly removed can sue for possession, costs, damages and attorney fees. Separately, knowingly listing or advertising residential property for rent without legal title or authority is a felony, so check the authority of anyone who offers to rent property to you or to manage it for you, and watch for copies of your own listings posted by someone else.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 55-3C-2(a) (squatter: a person unlawfully occupying a dwelling unit or other structure 'who is not entitled under a rental or lease agreement or who is not authorized by the tenant or owner to occupy that dwelling unit or structure' (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-2(a)); excludes 'a tenant who holds over in a periodic tenancy as described in §37-6-5 of this code, or an owner' (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-2(a))), (b) (squatting is trespass under §§ 61-3B-2, 61-3B-3), (c) (no eviction required); W. Va. Code § 55-3C-3(a)(1)-(8), (b) (completed and verified complaint), (c) (preliminary fact-finding; notice to immediately vacate; owner put in possession), (d) (fee for service; removal on request), (e); W. Va. Code § 55-3C-4 (damage by an unlawful occupant: misdemeanor under $1,000, felony over); W. Va. Code § 55-3C-5(a) (bad faith: false swearing; indemnify the agency), (b) (wrongful removal: 'restoration of possession, actual costs, damages, and attorney fees' (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-5(b))); W. Va. Code § 55-3C-6 ('Any person who knowingly lists or advertises residential real property or a commercial building for sale or renting without legal title or authority is guilty of a felony' (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-6); one to 10 years, or jail up to one year, and a fine up to $2,500). W. Va. Code § 37-6-31(a)-(c) (2024: art. 37-6 does not govern squatters; no court may require eviction to remove one). Enrolled Committee Substitute for H.B. 2434 (2025 Reg. Sess.), passed April 11, 2025, in effect July 10, 2025 (saved session-laws file b; the bill-status page's code-affected list prints a stale art. 55-3D). Court process for tenants: W. Va. Code § 55-3A-1(a)(3) (petition to remove a tenant; grounds include breach of a leasehold covenant). Guests of a tenant fall outside the removal process because it requires that 'An unauthorized person or persons are unlawfully occupying the property' (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-3(a)(3)) and excludes 'current or former lawful tenants' (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-3(a)(6)) and 'immediate family members of the property owner or tenants' (W. Va. Code § 55-3C-3(a)(7)); that a tenant-authorized guest is neither unauthorized nor unlawfully occupying is Claude's reading, and a former guest whose permission has ended is unsettled; `edu-no-guest-rights-rule-wv`. The statutory complaint form, if any, and the service fee amount are not in the Code (not located). Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
