    id: "edu-firearms-wv",
    title: "Firearms",
    group: "Rules & Regulations",
    states: ["WV"],
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "firearms",
    bodyText:
      "West Virginia's Constitution protects the right to keep and bear arms for the defense of self, family, home and state. No West Virginia statute addresses a private landlord's lease rules on firearms inside a rented home. An owner, lessee or other person in charge of real property may prohibit carrying firearms on property under their control, and a person who refuses a request to give up the firearm temporarily or to leave commits a misdemeanor; whether that lets you bar firearms inside a unit leased to and controlled by the tenant is not settled. The same law bars an owner or lessee from prohibiting a customer, employee or invitee from keeping a lawfully possessed firearm out of view and locked in or to a vehicle in a parking lot, or from searching the vehicle for one; whether a residential tenant counts as a customer or invitee is not settled, so the safer course is not to ban guns kept locked and out of view in vehicles. Firing a gun within 500 feet of a dwelling house is generally a misdemeanor, with a narrow exception for residents. Cities may not limit firearm possession in a way that conflicts with state law.",
    notes: "WV: 'A person has the right to keep and bear arms for the defense of self, family, home and state, and for lawful hunting and recreational use' (W. Va. Const. art. III, § 22); whether it limits private lease terms is case law, not searched. W. Va. Code § 61-7-14(b) ('any owner, lessee, or other person charged with the care, custody, and control of real property may prohibit the carrying openly or concealing of any firearm or deadly weapon on property under his or her domain' (W. Va. Code § 61-7-14(b))), (c) (refusal to relinquish or leave: misdemeanor, up to $1,000 or six months), (a)(1) (parking lot: property available to customers, employees or invitees; excludes the private parking area at a business at the owner's primary residence), (a)(5) (invitee means 'any business invitee, including a customer or visitor, who is lawfully on the premises of a public or private employer' (W. Va. Code § 61-7-14(a)(5))), (d)(1)-(3), (5) (vehicle storage; no search; no removal), (e) (no duty of care; immunity for compliance), (f) (Attorney General enforcement, civil penalties up to $5,000; private action, costs and fees to the prevailing party). Application of customer or invitee to residential tenants is Claude's reading (unsettled). W. Va. Code § 20-2-58(a)(3) (within 500 feet of any dwelling house; resident exception requires the consent of all residents and no other dwelling house within 500 feet), (b) (misdemeanor), (d) (licensed indoor ranges). W. Va. Code § 8-12-5a(a) (municipalities), (g) (a municipality is not a person charged with control of property for § 61-7-14). Statutes searched (WV battery firearms (firearms on property owned or leased by others): 155 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery e-firearms-lease (firearms or weapons and landlords, tenants, leases or dwellings): 6 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits: campus residence halls, minors on another's premises with the owner's or lessee's permission, riot control, mental hygiene; none governs leases). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
