      "No West Virginia statute sets a notice period, hours or permitted purposes for a landlord's entry into a residential rental, so the lease's access terms govern, subject to the tenant's right to possession under case law not reviewed here; state in the lease how much notice you will give and when you may enter without notice. Statutes address entry only in narrow cases. If a tenant who owes rent abandons the property and does not pay within one month after you post a written notice, you are entitled to possession and may enter. A cable operator asked to provide service may enter a building of three or more households to survey or install after serving notice on you and the tenant. If a tenant refuses entry that the lease allows, the refusal breaches a lease covenant, which is a ground for a summary eviction case, so document your request and the refusal.",
