    topicKey: "telecom-access",
    bodyText:
      "If you own or manage premises with dwelling units for three or more households, including a mobile home park, West Virginia bars you from interfering with the installation, maintenance, operation or removal of cable television facilities, from taking any payment from a tenant for permitting cable service, from taking payment from a cable operator beyond just compensation set under the law, and from charging different rent to tenants who do and do not receive cable. You may require that installation meet reasonable conditions to protect the safety, functioning and appearance of the premises and other tenants' convenience, that the cable operator or tenant bear the full cost of installation or removal, and that the operator indemnify you for damage. The operator must give you written notice at least 15 days before installing; the tenant must get the same written notice, or verbal notice of the date and time of entry at least 24 hours ahead. You are entitled to a single payment of just compensation, set by the Public Service Commission if you cannot agree, but not for a later rebuild, upgrade or rewiring. Separately, an agreement or contract made effective after West Virginia's broadband law took effect may not regulate or prevent exterior installation of antennas and equipment used for broadband; whether that reaches a residential lease is unsettled. The federal rule on satellite dishes and antennas was not reviewed.",
