      "West Virginia's mechanics' lien law gives a contractor, supplier or worker a lien on a building and on the owner's interest in the land when the work is done under a contract with the owner. No West Virginia statute says whether improvements a tenant orders can bind the landlord's interest, or lets a lease or a recorded notice protect the landlord. Whether a contractor hired by a tenant can reach your interest, for example where you approved the work, depends on case law not reviewed here. Requiring your written consent before alterations, and requiring the tenant to pay for any work the tenant orders, are lease terms between you and the tenant.",
