    topicKey: "home-business",
    bodyText:
      "West Virginia makes using a residential dwelling for a home-based business a permitted use. A home-based business is one owned and operated by the owner or tenant of the dwelling and carried on inside it, limited to the sale of lawful goods and services, that does not generate on-street parking or a substantial increase in traffic and has no signage visible from the street. A city may adopt reasonable regulations and may limit or prohibit some activities, such as selling illegal drugs or products containing alcohol or tobacco, operating a sober living home or operating a vape shop, but may not require rezoning, fire sprinklers in a dwelling of one or two units, or a license not required of similar businesses. The permission does not override any deed restriction, covenant or agreement restricting the use of land, or a common interest community's documents, so a lease limiting the property to residential use still binds the tenant; if you will allow a home business, say so in the lease and set your conditions.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 8-40-1(b) (home-based business: 'owned and operated by the owner or tenant of a residential dwelling' (W. Va. Code § 8-40-1(b)); conditions (1)-(3)); 'The use of a residential dwelling for a home-based business is a permitted use' (W. Va. Code § 8-40-2(a)), except that the permission does not supersede 'Any deed restriction, covenant, or agreement restricting the use of land' (W. Va. Code § 8-40-2(a)) or community documents (§ 8-40-2(a)(2)); W. Va. Code § 8-40-3(a)(1)-(3) (reasonable municipal regulations; activities a city may limit or prohibit); W. Va. Code § 8-40-4(a)(1)-(3) (no rezoning, sprinkler or extra license requirement); W. Va. Code § 8-40-5 (the municipality must show a challenged regulation complies). Claude's reading: a lease covenant limiting use to residential purposes is an agreement restricting the use of land, so § 8-40-2(a)(1) preserves it; the article sits in the municipal chapter and its regulation sections address municipalities, so whether § 8-40-2 reaches county land-use rules is unsettled (case law not searched). Art. 8-40 printed whole (§§ 8-40-1 to 8-40-5; no § 8-40-6). Shared `residential-use-only` tagged. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
