    ruleTypes: ["CONSTRAINED"],
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "returned-payments",
    bodyText:
      "If a tenant's check is dishonored for insufficient funds or credit, West Virginia lets you, as the payee, charge a fee of up to $25. You may not impose or collect the fee after a complaint for a worthless-check warrant has been delivered to magistrate court, and once a complaint has been presented there, payment may be made only through the court. State the fee in the lease and keep it at $25 or less. No statute authorizes a fee for a failed electronic payment, so this library's Returned Payments clause charges none, though it lets you require certified funds to replace any failed payment. If the tenant knowingly fails to pay the check amount in cash within 30 days after your written demand, you may sue for the face amount, damages of $500 or the face amount (whichever is less) and reasonable filing costs, but you may not use both that civil remedy and the criminal worthless-check procedure for the same check. The criminal worthless-check statutes do not apply to a postdated check, or to a check you knew or had reason to believe was not covered when you accepted it.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 61-3-39e ('The payee or holder of any dishonored check may impose a fee of up to $25 a worthless check.'; 'This fee may not be imposed or collected after a complaint for warrant has been delivered to magistrate court.'). Reading the $25 as the most a lease may charge for a dishonored check is Claude's reading: if the debt-collection part of the Consumer Credit and Protection Act reaches rent (Claude's reading of W. Va. Code § 46A-2-122(b)-(d); case law not searched), a fee incidental to the obligation needs express authority in 'the agreement creating or modifying the obligation and by statute or regulation' (W. Va. Code § 46A-2-128(d)). Under the library-wide reading of (d) (Claude's reading, case law not searched), a returned-check fee is a charge added because of a default, so it is incidental and needs statutory authority, and § 61-3-39e is the only statute authorizing a dishonored-check fee (WV battery returned-check (returned or dishonored check fees): 59 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (59 hits read by heading: tax, licensing, government and insurance fees; § 55-16-1 is the civil remedy; none authorizes a larger fee). Scope: the fee sentence speaks of 'any dishonored check', and the rest of § 61-3-39e of 'a check, draft or order' dishonored 'because of insufficient funds or credit'; § 55-16-1(a) defines a check as 'a draft or other written order payable on demand and drawn on a bank or depository'. A failed electronic payment is not a written order, so no statute authorizes a fee for it and, under the library-wide reading of § 46A-2-128(d), a fee for it would be an unauthorized incidental charge (Claude's reading, case law not searched). After a complaint: 'In the event complaint for warrant has been presented to magistrate court, payment may be made only through the court' (W. Va. Code § 61-3-39e), and a payee who accepts payment afterwards is liable for the court costs (same section). Civil remedy: W. Va. Code § 55-16-1(b) ('within thirty days following written demand'), (c)(1)-(3), (d) (court may waive the damages for recent discharge from employment, illness or catastrophic loss), (e) (demand by personal service, certified mail, or regular mail supported by a certificate of mailing or notarized affidavit), (g) ('No action may be brought pursuant to both this section and sections thirty-nine-a through thirty-nine-h, article three, chapter sixty-one of this code on the same check.'). Criminal: W. Va. Code § 61-3-39a(a) (check on a preexisting debt), (b) ('nor shall this section apply to any postdated check, draft or order'); § 61-3-39 (same exclusions); § 61-3-39e (notice form, ten days to pay; relinquish the check on tender before a complaint); § 61-3-39g (magistrate notice, $25 court costs, 'The complainant is forbidden by law to accept payment after the complaint is filed.'); § 61-3-39h(a) (costs on the complainant who accepts payment after filing, had reason to believe the check would bounce, or took a postdated check). Stale cross-reference (rule 77): § 55-16-1(e) refers to 'The written demand required in subsection (a) of this section', but the demand is in subsection (b). Lead clause `returned-payments-wv` (overrides `returned-payments`): fee bracket not more than $25, only for a check dishonored for insufficient funds or credit (the fee sentence names only a check), none after a warrant complaint; certified-funds replacement for any failed payment, with no fee for a failed electronic payment. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
