    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "rent-increase-notice",
    bodyText:
      "Apart from the temporary limit on month-to-month rent during a declared state of emergency or preparedness (see the disaster row), no West Virginia statute sets a notice period, frequency limit or cap for raising rent on a home. During a fixed-term lease, the rent stays as the lease states unless the lease provides for an increase; this library's optional Mid-Term Rent Increase clause requires 30 days' written notice and lets the tenant end the lease without a fee instead. For a periodic tenancy, give written notice of the new rent at least as far ahead as the notice needed to end the tenancy: a full rental period before the end of a period for month-to-month or week-to-week, and three months before the end of a year for year-to-year, unless your lease fixes a different notice period. Avoid raising rent soon after a tenant's complaint; see the retaliation row for how retaliation can be raised.",
    notes: "WV: Confirmed absent: (WV battery f-rent-increase (rent increases (notice, limits, frequency)): 5 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery f-rent-increase-nc (rent increases (notice, limits, frequency) (no context limb)): 6 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (6 hits: housing authority and agricultural association revenue sections, a child-support worksheet, § 55-3A-3, and the only rent-increase rule, W. Va. Code § 37-15-7(a), which bars selectively increasing rent in retaliation at factory-built home sites (art. 37-15, out of scope)); (WV battery rent-increase (notice of rent increase): 2 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205). Periodic tenancies: 'A periodic tenancy, in which the period is less than one year, may be terminated by like notice, or by notice for one full period before the end of any period.' (W. Va. Code § 37-6-5); year-to-year 'at least three months prior to the end of any year' (W. Va. Code § 37-6-5); 'This section shall not apply where, by special agreement, some other period of notice is fixed, or no notice is to be given' (W. Va. Code § 37-6-5). Using the termination notice as the floor for an increase, and the fixed-term rule, are Claude's reading; case law not searched. Rule 37: the floor differs by tenancy type as stated. Retaliation: `edu-retaliation-wv`; W. Va. Code § 55-3A-3(g) ('absent an issue of title, retaliatory eviction, or breach of warranty'). Emergency limit: during a state of emergency or state of preparedness it is unlawful to sell or offer housing 'for a price greater than 10 percent above the price charged by that person for those goods or services on the 10th day immediately preceding the declaration' (W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-3(a)), and housing means 'any rental housing leased on a month-to-month term' (W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-2(g)); under a state of preparedness only items in the proclamation are covered (W. Va. Code § 46A-6J-3(d)); `edu-disaster-rent-limits-wv`; `edu-termination-notice-wv`. Optional clause: `rent-increase-midterm-wv` (30 days' written notice, tenant may leave without a fee instead). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
