    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    topicKey: "rent-control",
    bodyText:
      "Apart from the emergency limit below, no West Virginia statute caps the rent a private landlord may charge or how much it may be raised, and no statute expressly allows or forbids local rent control. A provision of the municipal home rule law says municipalities may not prohibit or effectively limit the rental of a property, or regulate the duration, frequency or location of rentals. A municipality may regulate activities that arise when a property is used as a rental only if the regulation applies uniformly to all properties, rented or not (local hotel occupancy taxes are allowed). Whether these limits would bar a local limit on rent amounts has not been decided in the material searched here. Check your city's ordinances. During a declared state of emergency or state of preparedness a temporary price limit applies to month-to-month rentals (see the disaster row).",
    notes: "WV: Confirmed absent (statewide cap and express preemption): (WV battery f-rent-control (rent control, rent caps or local rent regulation): 2 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery f-rent-control-nc (rent control, rent caps or local rent regulation (no context limb)): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery rent-control (rent control or local regulation of rent amounts): 2 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery rent-control-nc (rent control or local regulation of rent amounts (no context limb)): 4 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (hits: public housing authorities must rent 'at rentals within the financial reach of the persons' (W. Va. Code § 16-15-18(a)(1)) and may pledge rents (§ 16-15-21); an appraisal-management section and a controlled-substances definition; none caps private rent or addresses local rent control). Municipal limit: 'Municipalities may not prohibit or effectively limit the rental of a property, in whole or in part, or regulate the duration, frequency, or location of such rental, in whole or in part.' (W. Va. Code § 8-1-5a(k)); 'A municipality may regulate activities that arise when a property is used as a rental: Provided, That such regulation applies uniformly to all properties, without regard to whether such properties are used as a rental' (W. Va. Code § 8-1-5a(k)); the subsection's last proviso preserves a hotel occupancy tax under § 7-18-1 et seq. A rent cap that applied only to rented property would not apply uniformly to all properties (Claude's reading). It sits in the Municipal Home Rule Program section but is not limited by its words to participating municipalities (Claude's reading); whether 'effectively limit the rental' reaches a rent-amount ordinance is unsettled (case law not searched). Emergency limit: `edu-disaster-rent-limits-wv` (W. Va. Code art. 46A-6J). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
