    topicKey: "deposit-escheat",
    bodyText:
      "West Virginia's unclaimed property law lists a security deposit as property a holder must turn over to the State Treasurer when the owner doesn't claim it. If you hold deposits in the course of your rental business, a refund you owe a former tenant is presumed abandoned three years after the tenant became entitled to it if the tenant has not contacted you about it in that time. You must then report it and pay it to the Treasurer: reports are due before November 1 each year, covering the 12 months before July 1, and for $50 or more you must first send the tenant written notice 60 to 120 days before filing if you have an address not known to be wrong and the tenant's claim is not time-barred. The deposit law does not let you keep a refund the tenant does not collect; if personal delivery is not reasonably possible and a mailed refund comes back undeliverable, hold it six months for the tenant to claim, then keep it for the unclaimed property process. Late reports carry 12% interest and daily civil penalties; paying the Treasurer in good faith ends your liability for the money.",
    notes: "WV: W. Va. Code § 36-8-1: 'Property' includes property referred to as or evidenced by a 'security deposit' and must be held, issued or owed in the course of the holder's business; a holder is a person obligated to hold for the account of, or deliver or pay to, the owner property subject to the article; that a residential landlord owing a deposit refund is a holder is Claude's reading. Period: three years under § 36-8-2(a)(18) (all other property, three years after the owner's right to demand it arises) or (a)(6) (money owed to a customer from a retail business transaction, three years); unclaimed means no communication or indication of interest (§ 36-8-2(c)-(d)). Custody when the last known address is in West Virginia and in the other cases in § 36-8-4. Report and owner notice: § 36-8-7(b), (d), (e) ('not more than one hundred twenty days or less than sixty days before filing the report'; conditions in (e)(1)-(3), including 'The claim of the apparent owner is not barred by a statute of limitations'), (g); payment with the report: § 36-8-8(a); good-faith payment relieves the holder: § 36-8-10(b); interest and penalties: § 36-8-24(a)-(c), waived for good faith without negligence, (e); records kept ten years: § 36-8-21(a); a dormancy charge only under a written contract: § 36-8-5. Deposit law: W. Va. Code § 37-6A-2(g) (when 'personal delivery is not reasonably possible' and the mailed deposit is returned, hold it 'for the period of six months'); art. 37-6A (read whole) has no forfeiture of an unclaimed deposit, and § 37-6A-4 bars a lease waiver. (WV battery f-unclaimed-deposit (security deposits as unclaimed property): 15 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (WV battery unclaimed-deposit (unclaimed property: deposits and refunds): 11 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (no other statute addresses unclaimed tenant deposits). Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; the State Treasurer's legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
