    bodyText:
      "No West Virginia statute sets or forbids a lease schedule of preset cleaning, carpet or repair charges. When the tenancy ends you may apply the deposit to damages from the tenant's noncompliance with the lease, less reasonable wear and tear, and to other damages or charges the rental agreement provides for, with a written itemization. A preset charge the tenant pays whatever the unit's condition, such as repainting or carpet cleaning after normal use, risks being treated as a charge for reasonable wear and tear or as a waiver of the tenant's deposit rights; the statute does not settle it. If you use a schedule, tie each charge to actual damage or uncleanliness beyond normal wear and itemize it.",
