    bodyText:
      "West Virginia has no statute setting the signs, notice or fees for a private property owner who has a vehicle towed from a parking lot. A vehicle left on private property without the consent of the owner or person in control for more than five days is an abandoned motor vehicle, and abandoning one there is a misdemeanor. Police and other enforcement agencies may take it, but only after sending you and the vehicle's owner, if known, 30 days' notice by registered or certified mail; the owner and lienholders may then reclaim it within 10 days of notice by paying the towing and storage charges. Tow companies' maximum rates for wrecked or disabled vehicles are set by the Public Service Commission. A tenant's vehicle parked with your permission is not abandoned; enforce parking rules through the lease, and check local ordinances before towing.",
    notes: "WV: Confirmed absent (private-lot towing procedure): (WV battery f-towing-private (towing vehicles from private or rental property): 7 hits, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (7 hits; the only towing provision is § 17C-13-5, removal from highways by police or the Division of Highways) (WV battery f-towing-sign-r2 (signs required before towing from a parking lot (tight rerun)): 2 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 2 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (2 hits: accessible parking § 17C-13-6, a law-enforcement revenue account; none on towing signs) (WV battery towing (towing or booting vehicles from private property): 10 hits, control 0; known positives passed (0 real sections, 1 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205); the Code regulates self-help towing only on state college campuses (WV battery f-towing-campus (towing by a property owner without police (real positive control)): 1 hit, control 0; known positives passed (1 real section, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205) (§ 18B-4-6). Abandoned vehicles: 'any motor vehicle, or major part thereof, which has remained on private property without consent of the owner or person in control of the property for any period over five days' (W. Va. Code § 17-24A-1(3)); misdemeanor (W. Va. Code § 17-24A-2(a), (c)-(f)); enforcement agency custody after 'a thirty-day notice by registered or certified mail' to the private property owner and vehicle owner (W. Va. Code § 17-24A-3); reclaim within 10 days 'upon payment of all towing, preservation and storage charges' (W. Va. Code § 17-24A-4(a)(3)); disposal (W. Va. Code §§ 17-24A-5, -6). (WV battery f-abandoned-vehicle (abandoned or junked vehicles on private property): 11 hits, control 0; known positives passed (2 real sections, 0 synthetic); scope CODE 31329, CONST 205). Towing carriers: registration with the Public Service Commission and charges no greater than its rates for 'any wrecked or disabled motor vehicle' (W. Va. Code § 24A-2-2a(c)); whether that reaches a tow of an operable parked car, and the Commission's rules, were not searched. Stale cross-reference (rule 77): § 17-24A-5(b) refers to appliances 'collected in accordance with section seven of this article', but § 17-24A-7 is the injunction section; custody is under § 17-24A-3. Shared `parking-vehicle-rules` (tagged) tows in accordance with applicable law. Boundary: the West Virginia Code and Constitution were searched; legislative rules, case law and local ordinances were not. Read section-open 2026-10-05 from the saved, hash-matched corpus of the whole West Virginia Code (code.wvlegislature.gov, every article's official text with section pages for the sections the article view did not head; currency from each section page's Bill History and the 2024-2026 signed-bill \"code affected\" lists) and the West Virginia Constitution (WV log §1). Rule 15: written section-open.",
