      "No West Virginia statute says whether accepting rent, or part of the rent, after a tenant's default or after a notice to quit waives your right to end a residential tenancy or seek possession. That is a question of case law, which was not searched here. If you accept a payment after giving notice or filing for possession, say in writing that you accept it without waiving the default or your right to possession. Separately, in an ejectment or unlawful detainer case for unpaid rent, a statute ends the case if all the rent and arrears, with interest and costs, are paid or tendered to you or your attorney, or paid into court, before trial (see the redemption row).",
