    topicKey: "late-fee",
    bodyText:
      "No West Virginia statute caps a late fee on a residential lease or sets a grace period before one may be charged. State the fee and when it applies in the lease and keep it reasonable: you may take late charges out of the security deposit only if they are reasonable charges for late payment of rent specified in the rental agreement. West Virginia's consumer debt-collection law bars collecting a charge incidental to an obligation unless both the agreement and a statute or regulation expressly authorize it, with a penalty of up to $1,000 per violation; it does not say whether it reaches rent, and this library reads the deposit statute's recognition of reasonable, agreed late charges as that authority, but courts have not been checked on either point. A very large fee also risks being treated as an unenforceable penalty. Unpaid rent separately bears interest as on other contracts. This library's lease does not let you end the tenancy for an unpaid late fee alone.",
